			}
		}
		h6 id=script-supporting-elements {
			span class=secno "3.2.5.2.9"
			- " Script-supporting elements"
			a class=self-link href="#script-supporting-elements"
		}
		p {
			dfn id=script-supporting-elements-2 "Script-supporting elements"
			- " are those that do not "
			a href="#represents" id=script-supporting-elements:represents represent
			- " anything themselves (i.e. they are not rendered), but are used to support scripts, e.g. to provide functionality for the user."
		}
		p "The following elements are script-supporting elements:"
		ul class="brief category-list" {
			li {
				code id=script-supporting-elements:the-script-element {
					a href="#the-script-element" script
				}
			}
			li {
				code id=script-supporting-elements:the-template-element {
					a href="#the-template-element" template
				}
			}
		}
		h5 id=transparent-content-models {
			span class=secno "3.2.5.3"
			- " Transparent content models"
			a class=self-link href="#transparent-content-models"
		}
		p {
			- "Some elements are described as "
			dfn id=transparent transparent
			- "; they have \"transparent\" in the description of their content model. The content model of a "
			a href="#transparent" id=transparent-content-models:transparent transparent
			- " element is derived from the content model of its parent element: the elements required in the part of the content model that is \"transparent\" are the same elements as required in the part of the content model of the parent of the transparent element in which the transparent element finds itself."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "For instance, an "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-ins-element {
					a href="#the-ins-element" ins
				}
				- " element inside a "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-ruby-element {
					a href="#the-ruby-element" ruby
				}
				- " element cannot contain an "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-rt-element {
					a href="#the-rt-element" rt
				}
				- " element, because the part of the "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-ruby-element-2 {
					a href="#the-ruby-element" ruby
				}
				- " element's content model that allows "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-ins-element-2 {
					a href="#the-ins-element" ins
				}
				- " elements is the part that allows "
				a href="#phrasing-content-2" id=transparent-content-models:phrasing-content-2 "phrasing content"
				- ", and the "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-rt-element-2 {
					a href="#the-rt-element" rt
				}
				- " element is not "
				a href="#phrasing-content-2" id=transparent-content-models:phrasing-content-2-2 "phrasing content"
				- .
			}
		}
		p class=note "In some cases, where transparent elements are nested in each other, the process has to be applied iteratively."
		div class=example {
			p "Consider the following markup fragment:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" object
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" ins
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" map
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/\""
					c- p="" >
					- Apples
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" map
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" ins
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" object
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p {
				- "To check whether \"Apples\" is allowed inside the "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-a-element {
					a href="#the-a-element" a
				}
				- " element, the content models are examined. The "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-a-element-2 {
					a href="#the-a-element" a
				}
				- " element's content model is transparent, as is the "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-map-element {
					a href="#the-map-element" map
				}
				- " element's, as is the "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-ins-element-3 {
					a href="#the-ins-element" ins
				}
				- " element's, as is the "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-object-element {
					a href="#the-object-element" object
				}
				- " element's. The "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-object-element-2 {
					a href="#the-object-element" object
				}
				- " element is found in the "
				code id=transparent-content-models:the-p-element {
					a href="#the-p-element" p
				}
				- " element, whose content model is "
				a href="#phrasing-content-2" id=transparent-content-models:phrasing-content-2-3 "phrasing content"
				- ". Thus, \"Apples\" is allowed, as text is phrasing content."
			}
		}
		p {
			- "When a transparent element has no parent, then the part of its content model that is \"transparent\" must instead be treated as accepting any "
			a href="#flow-content-2" id=transparent-content-models:flow-content-2 "flow content"
			- .
		}
		h5 id=paragraphs {
			span class=secno "3.2.5.4"
			- " Paragraphs"
			a class=self-link href="#paragraphs"
		}
		p class=note {
			- "The term "
			a href="#paragraph" id=paragraphs:paragraph paragraph
			- " as defined in this section is used for more than just the definition of the "
			code id=paragraphs:the-p-element {
				a href="#the-p-element" p
			}
			- " element. The "
			a href="#paragraph" id=paragraphs:paragraph-2 paragraph
			- " concept defined here is used to describe how to interpret documents. The "
			code id=paragraphs:the-p-element-2 {
				a href="#the-p-element" p
			}
			- " element is merely one of several ways of marking up a "
			a href="#paragraph" id=paragraphs:paragraph-3 paragraph
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "A "
			dfn id=paragraph paragraph
			- " is typically a run of "
			a href="#phrasing-content-2" id=paragraphs:phrasing-content-2 "phrasing content"
			- " that forms a block of text with one or more sentences that discuss a particular topic, as in typography, but can also be used for more general thematic grouping. For instance, an address is also a paragraph, as is a part of a form, a byline, or a stanza in a poem."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "In the following example, there are two paragraphs in a section. There is also a heading, which contains phrasing content that is not a paragraph. Note how the comments and "
				a href="#inter-element-whitespace" id=paragraphs:inter-element-whitespace "inter-element whitespace"
				- " do not form paragraphs."
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" section
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h2
					c- p="" >
					- "Example of paragraphs"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h2
					c- p="" >
					- " This is the "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- first
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- " paragraph in this example. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "This is the second."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- c="" "<!-- This is not a paragraph. -->"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" section
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Paragraphs in "
			a href="#flow-content-2" id=paragraphs:flow-content-2 "flow content"
			- " are defined relative to what the document looks like without the "
			code id=paragraphs:the-a-element {
				a href="#the-a-element" a
			}
			- ", "
			code id=paragraphs:the-ins-element {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- ", "
			code id=paragraphs:the-del-element {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- ", and "
			code id=paragraphs:the-map-element {
				a href="#the-map-element" map
			}
			- " elements complicating matters, since those elements, with their hybrid content models, can straddle paragraph boundaries, as shown in the first two examples below."
		}
		p class=note "Generally, having elements straddle paragraph boundaries is best avoided. Maintaining such markup can be difficult."
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "The following example takes the markup from the earlier example and puts "
				code id=paragraphs:the-ins-element-2 {
					a href="#the-ins-element" ins
				}
				- " and "
				code id=paragraphs:the-del-element-2 {
					a href="#the-del-element" del
				}
				- " elements around some of the markup to show that the text was changed (though in this case, the changes admittedly don't make much sense). Notice how this example has exactly the same paragraphs as the previous one, despite the "
				code id=paragraphs:the-ins-element-3 {
					a href="#the-ins-element" ins
				}
				- " and "
				code id=paragraphs:the-del-element-3 {
					a href="#the-del-element" del
				}
				- " elements — the "
				code id=paragraphs:the-ins-element-4 {
					a href="#the-ins-element" ins
				}
				- " element straddles the heading and the first paragraph, and the "
				code id=paragraphs:the-del-element-4 {
					a href="#the-del-element" del
				}
				- " element straddles the boundary between the two paragraphs."
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" section
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" ins
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" h2
					c- p="" >
					- "Example of paragraphs"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h2
					c- p="" >
					- " This is the "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- first
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- " paragraph in"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" ins
					c- p="" >
					- " this example"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" del
					c- p="" >
					- ". "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "This is the second."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" del
					c- p="" >
					c- c="" "<!-- This is not a paragraph. -->"
					c- p="" "</"
