				a href="#window" Window
			}
			- " object with the same names."
		}
		p class=note {
			- "While these attributes apply to all elements, they are not useful on all elements. For example, only "
			a href="#media-element" id=global-attributes:media-element "media elements"
			- " will ever receive a "
			code id=global-attributes:event-media-volumechange {
				a href="#event-media-volumechange" volumechange
			}
			- " event fired by the user agent."
		}
		hr
		p {
			a href="#custom-data-attribute" id=global-attributes:custom-data-attribute "Custom data attributes"
			- " (e.g. "
			code data-foldername
			- " or "
			code data-msgid
			- ") can be specified on any "
			a href="#html-elements" id=global-attributes:html-elements-8 "HTML element"
			- ", to store custom data, state, annotations, and similar, specific to the page."
		}
		hr
		p {
			- "In "
			a data-x-internal=html-documents href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#html-document" id=global-attributes:html-documents "HTML documents"
			- ", elements in the "
			a data-x-internal=html-namespace-2 href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#html-namespace" id=global-attributes:html-namespace-2 "HTML namespace"
			- " may have an "
			code xmlns
			- " attribute specified, if, and only if, it has the exact value \""
			code "http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml"
			- "\". This does not apply to "
			a data-x-internal=xml-documents href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#xml-document" id=global-attributes:xml-documents "XML documents"
			- .
		}
		p class=note {
			- "In HTML, the "
			code xmlns
			- " attribute has absolutely no effect. It is basically a talisman. It is allowed merely to make migration to and from XML mildly easier. When parsed by an "
			a href="#html-parser" id=global-attributes:html-parser "HTML parser"
			- ", the attribute ends up in no namespace, not the \""
			code "http://www.w3.org/2000/xmlns/"
			- "\" namespace like namespace declaration attributes in XML do."
		}
		p class=note {
			- "In XML, an "
			code xmlns
			- " attribute is part of the namespace declaration mechanism, and an element cannot actually have an "
			code xmlns
			- " attribute in no namespace specified."
		}
		hr
		p {
			cite XML
			- " also allows the use of the "
			code id=global-attributes:attr-xml-space {
				a data-x-internal=attr-xml-space href="https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#sec-white-space" xml:space
			}
			- " attribute in the "
			a data-x-internal=xml-namespace href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#xml-namespace" id=global-attributes:xml-namespace "XML namespace"
			- " on any element in an "
			a data-x-internal=xml-documents href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#xml-document" id=global-attributes:xml-documents-2 "XML document"
			- ". This attribute has no effect on "
			a href="#html-elements" id=global-attributes:html-elements-9 "HTML elements"
			- ", as the default behavior in HTML is to preserve whitespace. "
			a href="#refsXML" "[XML]"
		}
		p class=note {
			- "There is no way to serialize the "
			code id=global-attributes:attr-xml-space-2 {
				a data-x-internal=attr-xml-space href="https://www.w3.org/TR/xml/#sec-white-space" xml:space
			}
			- " attribute on "
			a href="#html-elements" id=global-attributes:html-elements-10 "HTML elements"
			- " in the "
			code id="global-attributes:text/html" {
				a href="#text/html" "text/html"
			}
			- " syntax."
		}
