		h5 id=article-or-section {
			span class=secno "4.3.12.1"
			- " Article or section?"
			a class=self-link href="#article-or-section"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "A "
			code id=article-or-section:the-section-element {
				a href="#the-section-element" section
			}
			- " forms part of something else. An "
			code id=article-or-section:the-article-element {
				a href="#the-article-element" article
			}
			- " is its own thing. But how does one know which is which? Mostly the real answer is \"it depends on author intent\"."
		}
		p {
			- "For example, one could imagine a book with a \"Granny Smith\" chapter that just said \"These juicy, green apples make a great filling for apple pies.\"; that would be a "
			code id=article-or-section:the-section-element-2 {
				a href="#the-section-element" section
			}
			- " because there'd be lots of other chapters on (maybe) other kinds of apples."
		}
		p {
			- "On the other hand, one could imagine a tweet or reddit comment or tumblr post or newspaper classified ad that just said \"Granny Smith. These juicy, green apples make a great filling for apple pies.\"; it would then be "
			code id=article-or-section:the-article-element-2 {
				a href="#the-article-element" article
			}
			- "s because that was the whole thing."
		}
		p {
			- "A comment on an article is not part of the "
			code id=article-or-section:the-article-element-3 {
				a href="#the-article-element" article
			}
			- " on which it is commenting, therefore it is its own "
