		div class=note {
			p {
				- "List elements (in particular, "
				code id=the-p-element:the-ol-element-2 {
					a href="#the-ol-element" ol
				}
				- " and "
				code id=the-p-element:the-ul-element-2 {
					a href="#the-ul-element" ul
				}
				- " elements) cannot be children of "
				code id=the-p-element:the-p-element-6 {
					a href="#the-p-element" p
				}
				- " elements. When a sentence contains a bulleted list, therefore, one might wonder how it should be marked up."
			}
			div class=example {
				p "For instance, this fantastic sentence has bullets relating to"
				ul {
					li "wizards, "
					li "faster-than-light travel, and "
					li "telepathy, "
				}
				p "and is further discussed below."
			}
			p {
				- "The solution is to realize that a "
				i id=the-p-element:paragraph-2 {
					a href="#paragraph" paragraph
				}
				- ", in HTML terms, is not a logical concept, but a structural one. In the fantastic example above, there are actually "
				em five
				a href="#paragraph" id=the-p-element:paragraph-3 paragraphs
				- " as defined by this specification: one before the list, one for each bullet, and one after the list."
			}
			div class=example {
				p "The markup for the above example could therefore be:"
				pre {
					code class=html {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
						- "For instance, this fantastic sentence has bullets relating to"
						c- p="" "</"
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" ul
						c- p="" >
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" li
						c- p="" >
						- "wizards, "
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" li
						c- p="" >
						- "faster-than-light travel, and "
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" li
						c- p="" >
						- "telepathy, "
						c- p="" "</"
						c- f="" ul
						c- p="" >
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
						- "and is further discussed below."
						c- p="" "</"
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
					}
				}
			}
			p {
				- "Authors wishing to conveniently style such \"logical\" paragraphs consisting of multiple \"structural\" paragraphs can use the "
				code id=the-p-element:the-div-element-2 {
					a href="#the-div-element" div
				}
				- " element instead of the "
				code id=the-p-element:the-p-element-7 {
					a href="#the-p-element" p
				}
				- " element."
			}
			div class=example {
				p "Thus for instance the above example could become the following:"
				pre {
					code class=html {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" div
						c- p="" >
						- "For instance, this fantastic sentence has bullets relating to "
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" ul
						c- p="" >
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" li
						c- p="" >
						- "wizards, "
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" li
						c- p="" >
						- "faster-than-light travel, and "
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" li
						c- p="" >
						- "telepathy, "
						c- p="" "</"
						c- f="" ul
						c- p="" >
						- " and is further discussed below."
						c- p="" "</"
						c- f="" div
						c- p="" >
					}
				}
				p {
					- "This example still has five structural paragraphs, but now the author can style just the "
					code id=the-p-element:the-div-element-3 {
						a href="#the-div-element" div
					}
					- " instead of having to consider each part of the example separately."
				}
			}
		}
