		p {
			- "The "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element {
				a href="#the-strong-element" strong
			}
			- " element "
			a href="#represents" id=the-strong-element:represents represents
			- " strong importance, seriousness, or urgency for its contents."
		}
		p {
			strong Importance
			- ": the "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-2 {
				a href="#the-strong-element" strong
			}
			- " element can be used in a heading, caption, or paragraph to distinguish the part that really matters from other parts that might be more detailed, more jovial, or merely boilerplate. (This is distinct from marking up subheadings, for which the "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-hgroup-element {
				a href="#the-hgroup-element" hgroup
			}
			- " element is appropriate.)"
		}
		p class=example {
			- "For example, the first word of the previous paragraph is marked up with "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-3 {
				a href="#the-strong-element" strong
			}
			- " to distinguish it from the more detailed text in the rest of the paragraph."
		}
		p {
			strong Seriousness
			- ": the "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-4 {
				a href="#the-strong-element" strong
			}
			- " element can be used to mark up a warning or caution notice."
		}
		p {
			strong Urgency
			- ": the "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-5 {
				a href="#the-strong-element" strong
			}
			- " element can be used to denote contents that the user needs to see sooner than other parts of the document."
		}
		p {
			- "The relative level of importance of a piece of content is given by its number of ancestor "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-6 {
				a href="#the-strong-element" strong
			}
			- " elements; each "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-7 {
				a href="#the-strong-element" strong
			}
			- " element increases the importance of its contents."
		}
		p {
			- "Changing the importance of a piece of text with the "
			code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-8 {
				a href="#the-strong-element" strong
			}
			- " element does not change the meaning of the sentence."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "Here, the word \"chapter\" and the actual chapter number are mere boilerplate, and the actual name of the chapter is marked up with "
				code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-9 {
					a href="#the-strong-element" strong
				}
				- :
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					- "Chapter 1: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- "The Praxis"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p {
				- "In the following example, the name of the diagram in the caption is marked up with "
				code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-10 {
					a href="#the-strong-element" strong
				}
				- ", to distinguish it from boilerplate text (before) and the description (after):"
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" figcaption
					c- p="" >
					- "Figure 1. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- "Ant colony dynamics"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- ". The ants in this colony are affected by the heat source (upper left) and the food source (lower right)."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" figcaption
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p {
				- "In this example, the heading is really \"Flowers, Bees, and Honey\", but the author has added a light-hearted addition to the heading. The "
				code id=the-strong-element:the-strong-element-11 {
					a href="#the-strong-element" strong
				}
				- " element is thus used to mark up the first part to distinguish it from the latter part."
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- "Flowers, Bees, and Honey"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- " and other things I don't understand"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Here is an example of a warning notice in a game, with the various parts marked up according to how important they are:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- Warning.
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- " This dungeon is dangerous. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- "Avoid the ducks."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- " Take any gold you find. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- "Do not take any of the diamonds"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- ", they are explosive and "
