		h4 id=edits-and-paragraphs {
			span class=secno "4.7.4"
			- " Edits and paragraphs"
			a class=self-link href="#edits-and-paragraphs"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "Since the "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-ins-element {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " and "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-del-element {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " elements do not affect "
			a href="#paragraph" id=edits-and-paragraphs:paragraph paragraphing
			- ", it is possible, in some cases where paragraphs are "
			a href="#paragraph" id=edits-and-paragraphs:paragraph-2 implied
			- " (without explicit "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-p-element {
				a href="#the-p-element" p
			}
			- " elements), for an "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-ins-element-2 {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " or "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-del-element-2 {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " element to span both an entire paragraph or other non-"
			a href="#phrasing-content-2" id=edits-and-paragraphs:phrasing-content-2 "phrasing content"
			- " elements and part of another paragraph. For example:"
		}
		pre {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" section
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" ins
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" >
				- " This is a paragraph that was inserted. "
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" >
				- " This is another paragraph whose first sentence was inserted at the same time as the paragraph above. "
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" ins
				c- p="" >
				- " This is a second sentence, which was there all along. "
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" section
				c- p="" >
			}
		}
		p {
			- "By only wrapping some paragraphs in "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-p-element-2 {
				a href="#the-p-element" p
			}
			- " elements, one can even get the end of one paragraph, a whole second paragraph, and the start of a third paragraph to be covered by the same "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-ins-element-3 {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " or "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-del-element-3 {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " element (though this is very confusing, and not considered good practice):"
		}
		pre class=bad {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" section
				c- p="" >
				- " This is the first paragraph. "
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" ins
				c- p="" >
				- "This sentence was inserted. "
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" >
				- "This second paragraph was inserted."
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" >
				- " This sentence was inserted too."
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" ins
				c- p="" >
				- " This is the third paragraph in this example. "
				c- c="" "<!-- (don't do this) -->"
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" section
				c- p="" >
			}
		}
		p {
			- "However, due to the way "
			a href="#paragraph" id=edits-and-paragraphs:paragraph-3 "implied paragraphs"
			- " are defined, it is not possible to mark up the end of one paragraph and the start of the very next one using the same "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-ins-element-4 {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " or "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-del-element-4 {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " element. You instead have to use one (or two) "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-p-element-3 {
				a href="#the-p-element" p
			}
			- " element(s) and two "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-ins-element-5 {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " or "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-del-element-5 {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " elements, as for example:"
		}
		pre {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" section
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" >
				- "This is the first paragraph. "
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" del
				c- p="" >
				- "This sentence was deleted."
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" del
				c- p="" "></"
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" ><
				c- f="" del
				c- p="" >
				- "This sentence was deleted too."
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" del
				c- p="" >
				- " That sentence needed a separate "
				c- ni="" "&lt;"
				- del
				c- ni="" "&gt;"
				- " element."
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" section
				c- p="" >
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Partly because of the confusion described above, authors are strongly encouraged to always mark up all paragraphs with the "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-p-element-4 {
				a href="#the-p-element" p
			}
			- " element, instead of having "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-ins-element-6 {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " or "
			code id=edits-and-paragraphs:the-del-element-6 {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " elements that cross "
			a href="#paragraph" id=edits-and-paragraphs:paragraph-4 "implied paragraphs"
			- " boundaries."
		}
		h4 id=edits-and-lists {
			span class=secno "4.7.5"
			- " Edits and lists"
			a class=self-link href="#edits-and-lists"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "The content models of the "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-ol-element {
				a href="#the-ol-element" ol
			}
			- " and "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-ul-element {
				a href="#the-ul-element" ul
			}
			- " elements do not allow "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-ins-element {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " and "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-del-element {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " elements as children. Lists always represent all their items, including items that would otherwise have been marked as deleted."
		}
		p {
			- "To indicate that an item is inserted or deleted, an "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-ins-element-2 {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " or "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-del-element-2 {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " element can be wrapped around the contents of the "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-li-element {
				a href="#the-li-element" li
			}
			- " element. To indicate that an item has been replaced by another, a single "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-li-element-2 {
				a href="#the-li-element" li
			}
			- " element can have one or more "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-del-element-3 {
				a href="#the-del-element" del
			}
			- " elements followed by one or more "
			code id=edits-and-lists:the-ins-element-3 {
				a href="#the-ins-element" ins
			}
			- " elements."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In the following example, a list that started empty had items added and removed from it over time. The bits in the example that have been emphasized show the parts that are the \"current\" state of the list. The list item numbers don't take into account the edits, though."
			pre {
