		h4 id=images {
			span class=secno "4.8.4"
			- " Images"
			a class=self-link href="#images"
		}
		h5 id=introduction-3 {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.1"
			- " Introduction"
			a class=self-link href="#introduction-3"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "To embed an image in HTML, when there is only a single image resource, use the "
			code id=introduction-3:the-img-element {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " element and its "
			code id=introduction-3:attr-img-src {
				a href="#attr-img-src" src
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		div class=example {
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h2
					c- p="" >
					- "From today's featured article"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h2
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"/uploads/100-marie-lloyd.jpg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- e="" width
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"100\""
						c- e="" height
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"150\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/wiki/Marie_Lloyd\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Marie Lloyd"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- " (1870–1922) was an English "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/wiki/Music_hall\""
					c- p="" >
					- "music hall"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" >
					- " singer, ..."
				}
			}
		}
		p "However, there are a number of situations for which the author might wish to use multiple image resources that the user agent can choose from:"
		ul {
			li {
				p "Different users might have different environmental characteristics:"
				ul {
					li {
						p "The users' physical screen size might be different from one another."
						div class=example {
							p "A mobile phone's screen might be 4 inches diagonally, while a laptop's screen might be 14 inches diagonally."
							svg aria-label="The phone's screen is much smaller compared to the laptop's screen." font-family=sans-serif font-size="2.5" height="200" role=img viewBox="0 0 80 32" {
								rect fill=white height="12" rx="1" stroke=black width="6" x="6" y="18"
								text dominant-baseline=middle text-anchor=middle transform="translate(9 24)\n        rotate(-60)" "4″"
								rect fill=white height="26" rx="2" stroke=black stroke-width="2" width="40" x="30" y="2"
								line stroke=black stroke-linecap=round stroke-width="2" x1="26" x2="74" y1="30" y2="30"
								text dominant-baseline=middle text-anchor=middle transform="translate(50 15)\n        rotate(-30)" "14″"
							}
						}
						p class=note {
							- "This is only relevant when an image's rendered size depends on the "
							a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport viewport
							- " size."
						}
					}
					li {
						p "The users' screen pixel density might be different from one another."
						div class=example {
							p "A mobile phone's screen might have three times as many physical pixels per inch compared to another mobile phone's screen, regardless of their physical screen size."
							svg aria-label="One phone has big pixels, the other has small pixels." font-family=sans-serif font-size="2.5" height="170" role=img viewBox="0 0 56 27" {
								defs {
									pattern height="3" id=img-intro-pixel patternUnits=userSpaceOnUse stroke-linecap=round width="3" x="0" y="0" {
										rect fill=black height="3" width="3" x="0" y="0"
										line stroke=red x1="0.5" x2="0.5" y1="0.5" y2="2.5"
										line stroke=lime x1="1.5" x2="1.5" y1="0.5" y2="2.5"
										line stroke=blue x1="2.5" x2="2.5" y1="0.5" y2="2.5"
									}
								}
								rect fill=white height="12" rx="1" stroke=black width="6" x="6" y="8"
								rect fill=white height="12" rx="1" stroke=black width="6" x="36" y="8"
								line stroke=brown stroke-width="3" x1="15" x2="22" y1="16" y2="24"
								circle cx="10" cy="10" fill="url(#img-intro-pixel)" r="8" stroke=black
								line stroke=brown stroke-width="3" x1="45" x2="52" y1="16" y2="24"
								circle cx="120" cy="30" fill="url(#img-intro-pixel)" r="24" stroke=black stroke-width="3" transform="scale(0.333333)"
								text dominant-baseline=middle x="20" y="10" "1x"
								text dominant-baseline=middle x="50" y="10" "3x"
							}
						}
					}
					li {
						p "The users' zoom level might be different from one another, or might change for a single user over time."
						p class=example "A user might zoom in to a particular image to be able to get a more detailed look."
						p {
							- "The zoom level and the screen pixel density (the previous point) can both affect the number of physical screen pixels per "
							a data-x-internal='px' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px" id=introduction-3:'px' "CSS pixel"
							- ". This ratio is usually referred to as "
							dfn id=device-pixel-ratio device-pixel-ratio
							- .
						}
					}
					li {
						p "The users' screen orientation might be different from one another, or might change for a single user over time."
						div class=example {
							p "A tablet can be held upright or rotated 90 degrees, so that the screen is either \"portrait\" or \"landscape\"."
							svg aria-label="The tablet has two orientations." font-family=sans-serif font-size="2.5" height="200" role=img viewBox="0 0 60 32" {
								rect fill=white height="20" rx="1" stroke=black width="14" x="6" y="5"
								line stroke=black stroke-width="1.1" x1="6" x2="20" y1="24" y2="24"
								text dominant-baseline=middle text-anchor=middle x="13" y="14.5" Portrait
								rect fill=white height="14" rx="1" stroke=black width="20" x="30" y="11"
								line stroke=black stroke-width="1.1" x1="31" x2="31" y1="25" y2="11"
								text dominant-baseline=middle text-anchor=middle x="40.5" y="18" Landscape
							}
						}
					}
					li {
						p "The users' network speed, network latency and bandwidth cost might be different from one another, or might change for a single user over time."
						p class=example "A user might be on a fast, low-latency and constant-cost connection while at work, on a slow, low-latency and constant-cost connection while at home, and on a variable-speed, high-latency and variable-cost connection anywhere else."
					}
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors might want to show the same image content but with different rendered size depending on, usually, the width of the "
					a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-2 viewport
					- ". This is usually referred to as "
					dfn id=viewport-based-selection "viewport-based selection"
					- .
				}
				div class=example {
					p {
						- "A web page might have a banner at the top that always spans the entire "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-3 viewport
						- " width. In this case, the rendered size of the image depends on the physical size of the screen (assuming a maximised browser window)."
					}
					svg aria-label="The upright-held phone shows a\n     small wolf at the top, and the tablet shows the same image but it is bigger." height="160" role=img viewBox="0 0 52 25.6" {
						rect fill=white height="12" rx="1" stroke=black width="6" x="6" y="8"
						rect fill="#716966" height="2" width="4" x="7" y="9"
						image height="2" width="4" x="7" xlink:href="/images/wolf.jpg" y="9"
						rect fill=white height="14" rx="1" stroke=black width="20" x="25" y="6"
						line stroke=black stroke-width="1.1" x1="26" x2="26" y1="20" y2="6"
						rect fill="#716966" height="8.5" width="17" x="27" y="7"
						image height="8.5" width="17" x="27" xlink:href="/images/wolf.jpg" y="7"
					}
				}
				div class=example {
					p {
						- "Another web page might have images in columns, with a single column for screens with a small physical size, two columns for screens with medium physical size, and three columns for screens with big physical size, with the images varying in rendered size in each case to fill up the "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-4 viewport
						- ". In this case, the rendered size of an image might be "
						em bigger
						- " in the one-column layout compared to the two-column layout, despite the screen being smaller."
					}
					svg aria-label="The rotated phone shows a top part of an image of a kettlebell swing; the\n     upright-held tablet shows a bit smaller images in two columns; the laptop shows images in three\n     columns." font-family=sans-serif font-size="2.5" height="200" role=img viewBox="0 0 115 32" {
						rect fill=white height="6" rx="1" stroke=black width="12" x="6" y="24"
						rect fill="#987b5a" height="4.5" width="10" x="7" y="25"
						image height="4.5" preserveAspectRatio="xMinYMin slice" width="10" x="7" xlink:href="/images/kettlebell.jpg" y="25"
						text text-anchor=middle x="12" y="21" "Narrow, 1 column"
						rect fill=white height="20" rx="1" stroke=black width="14" x="32" y="10"
						line stroke=black stroke-width="1.1" x1="32" x2="46" y1="29" y2="29"
						rect fill="#987b5a" height="5.5" width="5.5" x="33" y="11"
						image height="5.5" width="5.5" x="33" xlink:href="/images/kettlebell.jpg" y="11"
						rect fill=burlywood height="5.5" width="5.5" x="39.5" y="11"
						rect fill=silver height="5.5" width="5.5" x="33" y="17.5"
						text text-anchor=middle x="39" y="7" "Medium, 2 columns"
						rect fill=white height="26" rx="2" stroke=black stroke-width="2" width="40" x="65" y="2"
						line stroke=black stroke-linecap=round stroke-width="2" x1="61" x2="109" y1="30" y2="30"
						rect fill="#987b5a" height="10" width="10" x="67" y="4"
						image height="10" width="10" x="67" xlink:href="/images/kettlebell.jpg" y="4"
						rect fill=burlywood height="10" width="10" x="80" y="4"
						rect fill=silver height="10" width="10" x="93" y="4"
						text text-anchor=middle x="85" y="25" "Wide, 3 columns"
					}
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors might want to show different image content depending on the rendered size of the image. This is usually referred to as "
					dfn id=art-direction "art direction"
					- .
				}
				div class=example {
					p "When a web page is viewed on a screen with a large physical size (assuming a maximised browser window), the author might wish to include some less relevant parts surrounding the critical part of the image. When the same web page is viewed on a screen with a small physical size, the author might wish to show only the critical part of the image."
					svg aria-label="The upright-held phone shows a\n     cropped image of a wolf; the rotated tablet shows the uncropped image." height="160" role=img viewBox="0 0 52 25.6" {
						rect fill=white height="12" rx="1" stroke=black width="6" x="6" y="8"
						rect fill="#716966" height="6" width="4" x="7" y="9"
						image height="6" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid slice" width="4" x="7" xlink:href="/images/wolf.jpg" y="9"
						rect fill=white height="14" rx="1" stroke=black width="20" x="25" y="6"
						line stroke=black stroke-width="1.1" x1="26" x2="26" y1="20" y2="6"
						rect fill="#716966" height="8.5" width="17" x="27" y="7"
						image height="8.5" width="17" x="27" xlink:href="/images/wolf.jpg" y="7"
					}
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Authors might want to show the same image content but using different image formats, depending on which image formats the user agent supports. This is usually referred to as "
					dfn id=image-format-based-selection "image format-based selection"
					- .
				}
				p class=example "A web page might have some images in the JPEG, WebP and JPEG XR image formats, with the latter two having better compression abilities compared to JPEG. Since different user agents can support different image formats, with some formats offering better compression ratios, the author would like to serve the better formats to user agents that support them, while providing JPEG fallback for user agents that don't."
			}
		}
		p {
			- "The above situations are not mutually exclusive. For example, it is reasonable to combine different resources for different "
			a href="#device-pixel-ratio" id=introduction-3:device-pixel-ratio device-pixel-ratio
			- " with different resources for "
			a href="#art-direction" id=introduction-3:art-direction "art direction"
			- .
		}
		p "While it is possible to solve these problems using scripting, doing so introduces some other problems:"
		ul {
			li {
				p "Some user agents aggressively download images specified in the HTML markup, before scripts have had a chance to run, so that web pages complete loading sooner. If a script changes which image to download, the user agent will potentially start two separate downloads, which can instead cause worse page loading performance."
			}
			li {
				p "If the author avoids specifying any image in the HTML markup and instead instantiates a single download from script, that avoids the double download problem above but then no image will be downloaded at all for users with scripting disabled and the aggressive image downloading optimization will also be disabled."
			}
		}
		p "With this in mind, this specification introduces a number of features to address the above problems in a declarative manner."
		dl {
			dt {
				a href="#device-pixel-ratio" id=introduction-3:device-pixel-ratio-2 Device-pixel-ratio
				- "-based selection when the rendered size of the image is fixed"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "The "
					code id=introduction-3:attr-img-src-2 {
						a href="#attr-img-src" src
					}
					- " and "
					code id=introduction-3:attr-img-srcset {
						a href="#attr-img-srcset" srcset
					}
					- " attributes on the "
					code id=introduction-3:the-img-element-2 {
						a href="#the-img-element" img
					}
					- " element can be used, using the "
					code x
					- " descriptor, to provide multiple images that only vary in their size (the smaller image is a scaled-down version of the bigger image)."
				}
				p class=note {
					- "The "
					code x
					- " descriptor is not appropriate when the rendered size of the image depends on the "
					a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-5 viewport
					- " width ("
					a href="#viewport-based-selection" id=introduction-3:viewport-based-selection "viewport-based selection"
					- "), but can be used together with "
					a href="#art-direction" id=introduction-3:art-direction-2 "art direction"
					- .
				}
				div class=example {
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" h2
							c- p="" >
							- "From today's featured article"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" h2
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" img
							strong {
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"/uploads/100-marie-lloyd.jpg\""
							}
							strong {
								c- e="" srcset
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"/uploads/150-marie-lloyd.jpg 1.5x, /uploads/200-marie-lloyd.jpg 2x\""
							}
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"\""
							c- e="" width
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"100\""
							c- e="" height
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"150\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" ><
							c- f="" b
							c- p="" ><
							c- f="" a
							c- e="" href
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"/wiki/Marie_Lloyd\""
							c- p="" >
							- "Marie Lloyd"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" a
							c- p="" "></"
							c- f="" b
							c- p="" >
							- " (1870–1922) was an English "
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" a
							c- e="" href
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"/wiki/Music_hall\""
							c- p="" >
							- "music hall"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" a
							c- p="" >
							- " singer, ..."
						}
					}
					p "The user agent can choose any of the given resources depending on the user's screen's pixel density, zoom level, and possibly other factors such as the user's network conditions."
					p {
						- "For backwards compatibility with older user agents that don't yet understand the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-srcset-2 {
							a href="#attr-img-srcset" srcset
						}
						- " attribute, one of the URLs is specified in the "
						code id=introduction-3:the-img-element-3 {
							a href="#the-img-element" img
						}
						- " element's "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-src-3 {
							a href="#attr-img-src" src
						}
						- " attribute. This will result in something useful (though perhaps lower-resolution than the user would like) being displayed even in older user agents. For new user agents, the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-src-4 {
							a href="#attr-img-src" src
						}
						- " attribute participates in the resource selection, as if it was specified in "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-srcset-3 {
							a href="#attr-img-srcset" srcset
						}
						- " with a "
						code "1x"
						- " descriptor."
					}
					p {
						- "The image's rendered size is given in the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-dim-width {
							a href="#attr-dim-width" width
						}
						- " and "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-dim-height {
							a href="#attr-dim-height" height
						}
						- " attributes, which allows the user agent to allocate space for the image before it is downloaded."
					}
				}
			}
			dt {
				a href="#viewport-based-selection" id=introduction-3:viewport-based-selection-2 "Viewport-based selection"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "The "
					code id=introduction-3:attr-img-srcset-4 {
						a href="#attr-img-srcset" srcset
					}
					- " and "
					code id=introduction-3:attr-img-sizes {
						a href="#attr-img-sizes" sizes
					}
					- " attributes can be used, using the "
					code w
					- " descriptor, to provide multiple images that only vary in their size (the smaller image is a scaled-down version of the bigger image)."
				}
				div class=example {
					p {
						- "In this example, a banner image takes up the entire "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-6 viewport
						- " width (using appropriate CSS)."
					}
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" ><
							c- f="" img
							strong {
								c- e="" sizes
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"100vw\""
								c- e="" srcset
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"wolf-400.jpg 400w, wolf-800.jpg 800w, wolf-1600.jpg 1600w\""
							}
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"wolf-400.jpg\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"The rad wolf\""
							c- p="" "></"
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p {
						- "The user agent will calculate the effective pixel density of each image from the specified "
						code w
						- " descriptors and the specified rendered size in the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-sizes-2 {
							a href="#attr-img-sizes" sizes
						}
						- " attribute. It can then choose any of the given resources depending on the user's screen's pixel density, zoom level, and possibly other factors such as the user's network conditions."
					}
					p {
						- "If the user's screen is 320 "
						a data-x-internal='px' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px" id=introduction-3:'px'-2 "CSS pixels"
						- " wide, this is equivalent to specifying "
						code "wolf-400.jpg 1.25x, wolf-800.jpg 2.5x, wolf-1600.jpg 5x"
						- ". On the other hand, if the user's screen is 1200 "
						a data-x-internal='px' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px" id=introduction-3:'px'-3 "CSS pixels"
						- " wide, this is equivalent to specifying "
						code "wolf-400.jpg 0.33x, wolf-800.jpg 0.67x, wolf-1600.jpg 1.33x"
						- ". By using the "
						code w
						- " descriptors and the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-sizes-3 {
							a href="#attr-img-sizes" sizes
						}
						- " attribute, the user agent can choose the correct image source to download regardless of how large the user's device is."
					}
					p {
						- "For backwards compatibility, one of the URLs is specified in the "
						code id=introduction-3:the-img-element-4 {
							a href="#the-img-element" img
						}
						- " element's "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-src-5 {
							a href="#attr-img-src" src
						}
						- " attribute. In new user agents, the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-src-6 {
							a href="#attr-img-src" src
						}
						- " attribute is ignored when the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-srcset-5 {
							a href="#attr-img-srcset" srcset
						}
						- " attribute uses "
						code w
						- " descriptors."
					}
				}
				div class=example {
					p {
						- "In this example, the web page has three layouts depending on the width of the "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-7 viewport
						- ". The narrow layout has one column of images (the width of each image is about 100%), the middle layout has two columns of images (the width of each image is about 50%), and the widest layout has three columns of images, and some page margin (the width of each image is about 33%). It breaks between these layouts when the "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-8 viewport
						- " is "
						code "30em"
						- " wide and "
						code "50em"
						- " wide, respectively."
					}
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" img
							strong {
								c- e="" sizes
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"(max-width: 30em) 100vw, (max-width: 50em) 50vw, calc(33vw - 100px)\""
							}
							strong {
								c- e="" srcset
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"swing-200.jpg 200w, swing-400.jpg 400w, swing-800.jpg 800w, swing-1600.jpg 1600w\""
							}
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"swing-400.jpg\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"Kettlebell Swing\""
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p {
						- "The "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-sizes-4 {
							a href="#attr-img-sizes" sizes
						}
						- " attribute sets up the layout breakpoints at "
						code "30em"
						- " and "
						code "50em"
						- ", and declares the image sizes between these breakpoints to be "
						code "100vw"
						- ", "
						code "50vw"
						- ", or "
						code "calc(33vw - 100px)"
						- ". These sizes do not necessarily have to match up exactly with the actual image width as specified in the CSS."
					}
					p {
						- "The user agent will pick a width from the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-sizes-5 {
							a href="#attr-img-sizes" sizes
						}
						- " attribute, using the first item with a "
						a data-x-internal=media-condition href="https://drafts.csswg.org/mediaqueries/#typedef-media-condition" id=introduction-3:media-condition <media-condition>
						- " (the part in parentheses) that evaluates to true, or using the last item ("
						code "calc(33vw - 100px)"
						- ") if they all evaluate to false."
					}
					p {
						- "For example, if the "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-9 viewport
						- " width is "
						code "29em"
						- ", then "
						code "(max-width: 30em)"
						- " evaluates to true and "
						code "100vw"
						- " is used, so the image size, for the purpose of resource selection, is "
						code "29em"
						- ". If the "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-10 viewport
						- " width is instead "
						code "32em"
						- ", then "
						code "(max-width: 30em)"
						- " evaluates to false, but "
						code "(max-width: 50em)"
						- " evaluates to true and "
						code "50vw"
						- " is used, so the image size, for the purpose of resource selection, is "
						code "16em"
						- " (half the "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-11 viewport
						- " width). Notice that the slightly wider "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-12 viewport
						- " results in a smaller image because of the different layout."
					}
					p "The user agent can then calculate the effective pixel density and choose an appropriate resource similarly to the previous example."
				}
				div class=example {
					p {
						- "This example is the same as the previous example, but the image is "
						a href="#attr-loading-lazy-state" id=introduction-3:attr-loading-lazy-state lazy-loaded
						- ". In this case, the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-img-sizes-6 {
							a href="#attr-img-sizes" sizes
						}
						- " attribute can use the "
						code id=introduction-3:valdef-sizes-auto {
							a href="#valdef-sizes-auto" auto
						}
						- " keyword, and the user agent will use the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-dim-width-2 {
							a href="#attr-dim-width" width
						}
						- " attribute (or the width specified in CSS) for the "
						a href="#source-size-2" id=introduction-3:source-size-2 "source size"
						- .
					}
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" img
							strong {
								c- e="" loading
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"lazy\""
								c- e="" width
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"200\""
								c- e="" height
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"200\""
								c- e="" sizes
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"auto\""
							}
							c- e="" srcset
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"swing-200.jpg 200w, swing-400.jpg 400w, swing-800.jpg 800w, swing-1600.jpg 1600w\""
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"swing-400.jpg\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"Kettlebell Swing\""
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p {
						- "For better backwards-compatibility with legacy user agents that don't support the "
						code id=introduction-3:valdef-sizes-auto-2 {
							a href="#valdef-sizes-auto" auto
						}
						- " keyword, fallback sizes can be specified if desired."
					}
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" img
							c- e="" loading
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"lazy\""
							c- e="" width
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"200\""
							c- e="" height
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"200\""
							strong {
								c- e="" sizes
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"auto, (max-width: 30em) 100vw, (max-width: 50em) 50vw, calc(33vw - 100px)\""
							}
							c- e="" srcset
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"swing-200.jpg 200w, swing-400.jpg 400w, swing-800.jpg 800w, swing-1600.jpg 1600w\""
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"swing-400.jpg\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"Kettlebell Swing\""
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
				}
			}
			dt {
				a href="#art-direction" id=introduction-3:art-direction-3 "Art direction"
				- "-based selection"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "The "
					code id=introduction-3:the-picture-element {
						a href="#the-picture-element" picture
					}
					- " element and the "
					code id=introduction-3:the-source-element {
						a href="#the-source-element" source
					}
					- " element, together with the "
					code id=introduction-3:attr-source-media {
						a href="#attr-source-media" media
					}
					- " attribute, can be used to provide multiple images that vary the image content (for instance the smaller image might be a cropped version of the bigger image)."
				}
				div class=example {
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" picture
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" source
							strong {
								c- e="" media
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"(min-width: 45em)\""
							}
							c- e="" srcset
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"large.jpg\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" source
							strong {
								c- e="" media
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"(min-width: 32em)\""
							}
							c- e="" srcset
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"med.jpg\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" img
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"small.jpg\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"The wolf runs through the snow.\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" picture
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p {
						- "The user agent will choose the first "
						code id=introduction-3:the-source-element-2 {
							a href="#the-source-element" source
						}
						- " element for which the media query in the "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-source-media-2 {
							a href="#attr-source-media" media
						}
						- " attribute matches, and then choose an appropriate URL from its "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-source-srcset {
							a href="#attr-source-srcset" srcset
						}
						- " attribute."
					}
					p "The rendered size of the image varies depending on which resource is chosen. To specify dimensions that the user agent can use before having downloaded the image, CSS can be used."
					pre {
						code class=css {
							c- f="" "img "
							c- p="" "{"
							c- k="" width
							c- p="" :
							c- m="" "300"
							c- l="" px
							c- p="" ";"
							c- k="" height
							c- p="" :
							c- m="" "300"
							c- l="" px
							c- p="" "}"
							c- n="" @media
							c- p="" "("
							- min-width
							c- f="" ": 32em) "
							c- p="" "{"
							- " img { width: 500px; height:300px } } @media (min-width: 45em) { img { width: 700px; height:400px } }"
						}
					}
				}
				div class=example {
					p {
						- "This example combines "
						a href="#art-direction" id=introduction-3:art-direction-4 "art direction"
						- "- and "
						a href="#device-pixel-ratio" id=introduction-3:device-pixel-ratio-3 device-pixel-ratio
						- "-based selection. A banner that takes half the "
						a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=introduction-3:viewport-13 viewport
						- " is provided in two versions, one for wide screens and one for narrow screens."
					}
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" picture
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" source
							c- e="" media
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"(max-width: 500px)\""
							c- e="" srcset
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"banner-phone.jpeg, banner-phone-HD.jpeg 2x\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" img
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"banner.jpeg\""
							c- e="" srcset
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"banner-HD.jpeg 2x\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"The Breakfast Combo\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" picture
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
				}
			}
			dt {
				a href="#image-format-based-selection" id=introduction-3:image-format-based-selection "Image format-based selection"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "The "
					code id=introduction-3:attr-source-type {
						a href="#attr-source-type" type
					}
					- " attribute on the "
					code id=introduction-3:the-source-element-3 {
						a href="#the-source-element" source
					}
					- " element can be used to provide multiple images in different formats."
				}
				div class=example {
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" h2
							c- p="" >
							- "From today's featured article"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" h2
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" picture
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" source
							c- e="" srcset
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"/uploads/100-marie-lloyd.webp\""
							strong {
								c- e="" type
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"image/webp\""
							}
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" source
							c- e="" srcset
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"/uploads/100-marie-lloyd.jxr\""
							strong {
								c- e="" type
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"image/vnd.ms-photo\""
							}
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" img
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"/uploads/100-marie-lloyd.jpg\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"\""
							c- e="" width
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"100\""
							c- e="" height
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"150\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" picture
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" ><
							c- f="" b
							c- p="" ><
							c- f="" a
							c- e="" href
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"/wiki/Marie_Lloyd\""
							c- p="" >
							- "Marie Lloyd"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" a
							c- p="" "></"
							c- f="" b
							c- p="" >
							- " (1870–1922) was an English "
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" a
							c- e="" href
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"/wiki/Music_hall\""
							c- p="" >
							- "music hall"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" a
							c- p="" >
							- " singer, ..."
						}
					}
					p {
						- "In this example, the user agent will choose the first source that has a "
						code id=introduction-3:attr-source-type-2 {
							a href="#attr-source-type" type
						}
						- " attribute with a supported MIME type. If the user agent supports WebP images, the first "
						code id=introduction-3:the-source-element-4 {
							a href="#the-source-element" source
						}
						- " element will be chosen. If not, but the user agent does support JPEG XR images, the second "
						code id=introduction-3:the-source-element-5 {
							a href="#the-source-element" source
						}
						- " element will be chosen. If neither of those formats are supported, the "
						code id=introduction-3:the-img-element-5 {
							a href="#the-img-element" img
						}
						- " element will be chosen."
					}
				}
			}
		}
		h6 id=adaptive-images {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.1.1"
			- " Adaptive images"
			a class=self-link href="#adaptive-images"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "CSS and media queries can be used to construct graphical page layouts that adapt dynamically to the user's environment, in particular to different "
			a data-x-internal=viewport href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css2/#viewport" id=adaptive-images:viewport viewport
			- " dimensions and pixel densities. For content, however, CSS does not help; instead, we have the "
			code id=adaptive-images:the-img-element {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " element's "
			code id=adaptive-images:attr-img-srcset {
				a href="#attr-img-srcset" srcset
			}
			- " attribute and the "
			code id=adaptive-images:the-picture-element {
				a href="#the-picture-element" picture
			}
			- " element. This section walks through a sample case showing how to use these features."
		}
		p {
			- "Consider a situation where on wide screens (wider than 600 "
			a data-x-internal='px' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px" id=adaptive-images:'px' "CSS pixels"
			- ") a 300×150 image named "
			code a-rectangle.png
			- " is to be used, but on smaller screens (600 "
			a data-x-internal='px' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px" id=adaptive-images:'px'-2 "CSS pixels"
			- " and less), a smaller 100×100 image called "
			code a-square.png
			- " is to be used. The markup for this would look like this:"
		}
		pre {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" figure
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" picture
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" source
				c- e="" srcset
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"a-square.png\""
				c- e="" media
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"(max-width: 600px)\""
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" img
				c- e="" src
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"a-rectangle.png\""
				c- e="" alt
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"Barney Frank wears a suit and glasses.\""
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" picture
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" figcaption
				c- p="" >
				- "Barney Frank, 2011"
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" figcaption
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" figure
				c- p="" >
			}
		}
		p class=note {
			- "For details on what to put in the "
			code id=adaptive-images:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute, see the "
			a href="#alt" "Requirements for providing text to act as an alternative for images"
			- " section."
		}
		p "The problem with this is that the user agent does not necessarily know what dimensions to use for the image when the image is loading. To avoid the layout having to be reflowed multiple times as the page is loading, CSS and CSS media queries can be used to provide the dimensions:"
		pre {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" style
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" "#"
				c- nn="" a
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" width
				c- p="" :
				c- mi="" "300"
				c- b="" px
				c- p="" ";"
				c- k="" height
				c- p="" :
				c- mi="" "150"
				c- b="" px
				c- p="" ";"
				c- p="" "}"
				c- p="" @
				c- k="" media
				c- o="" "("
				c- f="" max-width
				c- o="" :
				c- f="" "600px"
				c- o="" ")"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- p="" "#"
				c- nn="" a
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" width
				c- p="" :
				c- mi="" "100"
				c- b="" px
				c- p="" ";"
				c- k="" height
				c- p="" :
				c- mi="" "100"
				c- b="" px
				c- p="" ";"
				c- p="" "}"
				c- p="" "}"
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" style
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" figure
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" picture
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" source
				c- e="" srcset
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"a-square.png\""
				c- e="" media
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"(max-width: 600px)\""
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" img
				c- e="" src
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"a-rectangle.png\""
				c- e="" alt
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"Barney Frank wears a suit and glasses.\""
				c- e="" id
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"a\""
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" picture
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" figcaption
				c- p="" >
				- "Barney Frank, 2011"
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" figcaption
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" figure
				c- p="" >
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Alternatively, the "
			code id=adaptive-images:attr-dim-width {
				a href="#attr-dim-width" width
			}
			- " and "
			code id=adaptive-images:attr-dim-height {
				a href="#attr-dim-height" height
			}
			- " attributes can be used to provide the width and height for legacy user agents, using CSS just for the user agents that support "
			code id=adaptive-images:the-picture-element-2 {
				a href="#the-picture-element" picture
			}
			- :
		}
		pre {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" style
				c- e="" media
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"(max-width: 600px)\""
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" "#"
				c- nn="" a
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" width
				c- p="" :
				c- mi="" "100"
				c- b="" px
				c- p="" ";"
				c- k="" height
				c- p="" :
				c- mi="" "100"
				c- b="" px
				c- p="" ";"
				c- p="" "}"
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" style
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" figure
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" picture
				c- p="" >
