		}
		p {
			- "The most general rule to consider when writing alternative text is the following: "
			strong {
				- "the intent is that replacing every image with the text of its "
				code id=general-guidelines:attr-img-alt-3 {
					a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
				}
				- " attribute does not change the meaning of the page"
			}
			- .
		}
		p "So, in general, alternative text can be written by considering what one would have written had one not been able to include the image."
		p {
			- "A corollary to this is that the "
			code id=general-guidelines:attr-img-alt-4 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute's value should never contain text that could be considered the image's "
			em caption
			- ", "
			em title
			- ", or "
			em legend
			- ". It is supposed to contain replacement text that could be used by users "
			em instead
			- " of the image; it is not meant to supplement the image. The "
			code id=general-guidelines:attr-title {
				a href="#attr-title" title
			}
			- " attribute can be used for supplemental information."
		}
		p {
			- "Another corollary is that the "
			code id=general-guidelines:attr-img-alt-5 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute's value should not repeat information that is already provided in the prose next to the image."
		}
		p class=note "One way to think of alternative text is to think about how you would read the page containing the image to someone over the phone, without mentioning that there is an image present. Whatever you say instead of the image is typically a good start for writing the alternative text."
		h6 id=a-link-or-button-containing-nothing-but-the-image {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.2"
			- " A link or button containing nothing but the image"
			a class=self-link href="#a-link-or-button-containing-nothing-but-the-image"
		}
		p {
			- "When an "
			code id=a-link-or-button-containing-nothing-but-the-image:the-a-element {
				a href="#the-a-element" a
			}
			- " element that creates a "
			a href="#hyperlink" id=a-link-or-button-containing-nothing-but-the-image:hyperlink hyperlink
			- ", or a "
			code id=a-link-or-button-containing-nothing-but-the-image:the-button-element {
				a href="#the-button-element" button
			}
			- " element, has no textual content but contains one or more images, the "
			code id=a-link-or-button-containing-nothing-but-the-image:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attributes must contain text that together convey the purpose of the link or button."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In this example, a user is asked to pick their preferred color from a list of three. Each color is given by an image, but for users who have configured their user agent not to display images, the color names are used instead:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					- "Pick your color"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" ul
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"green.html\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"green.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Green\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"blue.html\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"blue.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Blue\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"red.html\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"red.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Red\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" ul
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In this example, each button has a set of images to indicate the kind of color output desired by the user. The first image is used in each case to give the alternative text."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" button
					c- e="" name
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"rgb\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"red\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"RGB\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"green\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"blue\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" button
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" button
					c- e="" name
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"cmyk\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"cyan\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"CMYK\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"magenta\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"yellow\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"black\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" button
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "Since each image represents one part of the text, it could also be written like this:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" button
					c- e="" name
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"rgb\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"red\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"R\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"green\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"G\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"blue\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"B\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" button
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" button
					c- e="" name
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"cmyk\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"cyan\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"C\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"magenta\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"M\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"yellow\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Y\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"black\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"K\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" button
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "However, with other alternative text, this might not work, and putting all the alternative text into one image in each case might make more sense:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" button
					c- e="" name
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"rgb\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"red\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"sRGB profile\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"green\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"blue\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" button
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" button
					c- e="" name
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"cmyk\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"cyan\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"CMYK profile\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"magenta\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"yellow\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"black\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" button
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		h6 id=a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.3"
			- " A phrase or paragraph with an alternative graphical representation: charts, diagrams, graphs, maps, illustrations"
			a class=self-link href="#a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations"
		}
		p {
			- "Sometimes something can be more clearly stated in graphical form, for example as a flowchart, a diagram, a graph, or a simple map showing directions. In such cases, an image can be given using the "
			code id=a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations:the-img-element {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " element, but the lesser textual version must still be given, so that users who are unable to view the image (e.g. because they have a very slow connection, or because they are using a text-only browser, or because they are listening to the page being read out by a hands-free automobile voice web browser, or simply because they are blind) are still able to understand the message being conveyed."
		}
		p {
			- "The text must be given in the "
			code id=a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute, and must convey the same message as the image specified in the "
			code id=a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations:attr-img-src {
				a href="#attr-img-src" src
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		p {
			- "It is important to realize that the alternative text is a "
			em replacement
			- " for the image, not a description of the image."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "In the following example we have "
				a href="/images/parsing-model-overview.svg" "a flowchart"
				- " in image form, with text in the "
				code id=a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations:attr-img-alt-2 {
					a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
				}
				- " attribute rephrasing the flowchart in prose form:"
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "In the common case, the data handled by the tokenization stage comes from the network, but it can also come from script."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"images/parsing-model-overview.svg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"The Network"
						c- s="" "passes data to the Input Stream Preprocessor, which passes it to the"
						c- s="" "Tokenizer, which passes it to the Tree Construction stage. From there,"
						c- s="" "data goes to both the DOM and to Script Execution. Script Execution is"
						c- s="" "linked to the DOM, and, using document.write(), passes data to the"
						c- s="" "Tokenizer.\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Here's another example, showing a good solution and a bad solution to the problem of including an image in a description."
			p "First, here's the good solution. This sample shows how the alternative text should just be what you would have put in the prose if the image had never existed."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- c="" "<!-- This is the correct way to do things. -->"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " You are standing in an open field west of a house. "
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"house.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"The house is white, with a boarded front door.\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					- " There is a small mailbox here. "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "Second, here's the bad solution. In this incorrect way of doing things, the alternative text is simply a description of the image, instead of a textual replacement for the image. It's bad because when the image isn't shown, the text doesn't flow as well as in the first example."
			pre class=bad {
				code class=html {
					c- c="" "<!-- "
					em {
						c- c="" "This is the wrong way to do things."
					}
					c- c="" " -->"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " You are standing in an open field west of a house. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"house.jpeg\""
					c- e="" alt
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"A white house, with a boarded front door.\""
					c- p="" >
					- " There is a small mailbox here. "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p {
				- "Text such as \"Photo of white house with boarded door\" would be equally bad alternative text (though it could be suitable for the "
				code id=a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations:attr-title {
					a href="#attr-title" title
				}
				- " attribute or in the "
				code id=a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations:the-figcaption-element {
					a href="#the-figcaption-element" figcaption
				}
				- " element of a "
				code id=a-phrase-or-paragraph-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-charts,-diagrams,-graphs,-maps,-illustrations:the-figure-element {
					a href="#the-figure-element" figure
				}
				- " with this image)."
			}
		}
		h6 id=a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.4"
			- " A short phrase or label with an alternative graphical representation: icons, logos"
			a class=self-link href="#a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos"
		}
		p "A document can contain information in iconic form. The icon is intended to help users of visual browsers to recognize features at a glance."
		p {
			- "In some cases, the icon is supplemental to a text label conveying the same meaning. In those cases, the "
			code id=a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must be present but must be empty."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "Here the icons are next to text that conveys the same meaning, so they have an empty "
				code id=a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos:attr-img-alt-2 {
					a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
				}
				- " attribute:"
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" nav
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/help/\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"/icons/help.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					- " Help"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/configure/\""
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"/icons/configuration.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					- " Configuration Tools"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" nav
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "In other cases, the icon has no text next to it describing what it means; the icon is supposed to be self-explanatory. In those cases, an equivalent textual label must be given in the "
			code id=a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos:attr-img-alt-3 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Here, posts on a news site are labeled with an icon indicating their topic."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" body
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" article
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" header
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					- "Ratatouille wins "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" i
					c- p="" >
					- "Best Movie of the Year"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" i
					c- p="" >
					- " award"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"movies.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Movies\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" header
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Pixar has won yet another "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" i
					c- p="" >
					- "Best Movie of the Year"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" i
					c- p="" >
					- " award, making this its 8th win in the last 12 years."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" article
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" article
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" header
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					- "Latest TWiT episode is online"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"podcasts.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Podcasts\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" header
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "The latest TWiT episode has been posted, in which we hear several tech news stories as well as learning much more about the iPhone. This week, the panelists compare how reflective their iPhones' Apple logos are."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" article
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" body
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p "Many pages include logos, insignia, flags, or emblems, which stand for a particular entity such as a company, organization, project, band, software package, country, or some such."
		p {
			- "If the logo is being used to represent the entity, e.g. as a page heading, the "
			code id=a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos:attr-img-alt-4 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must contain the name of the entity being represented by the logo. The "
			code id=a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos:attr-img-alt-5 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must "
			em not
			- " contain text like the word \"logo\", as it is not the fact that it is a logo that is being conveyed, it's the entity itself."
		}
		p {
			- "If the logo is being used next to the name of the entity that it represents, then the logo is supplemental, and its "
			code id=a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos:attr-img-alt-6 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must instead be empty."
		}
		p "If the logo is merely used as decorative material (as branding, or, for example, as a side image in an article that mentions the entity to which the logo belongs), then the entry below on purely decorative images applies. If the logo is actually being discussed, then it is being used as a phrase or paragraph (the description of the logo) with an alternative graphical representation (the logo itself), and the first entry above applies."
		div class=example {
			p "In the following snippets, all four of the above cases are present. First, we see a logo used to represent a company:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"XYZ.gif\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"The XYZ company\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "Next, we see a paragraph which uses a logo right next to the company name, and so doesn't have any alternative text: "
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" article
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h2
					c- p="" >
					- News
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h2
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "We have recently been looking at buying the "
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"alpha.gif\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
						- " ΑΒΓ company"
					}
					- ", a small Greek company specializing in our type of product."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "In this third snippet, we have a logo being used in an aside, as part of the larger article discussing the acquisition:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" aside
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"alpha-large.gif\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" aside
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "The ΑΒΓ company has had a good quarter, and our pie chart studies of their accounts suggest a much bigger blue slice than its green and orange slices, which is always a good sign."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" article
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "Finally, we have an opinion piece talking about a logo, and the logo is therefore described in detail in the alternative text."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Consider for a moment their logo:"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"/images/logo\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"It consists of a green circle with a"
						c- s="" "green question mark centered inside it.\""
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "How unoriginal can you get? I mean, oooooh, a question mark, how "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- revolutionary
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- ", how utterly "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- ground-breaking
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- ", I'm sure everyone will rush to adopt those specifications now! They could at least have tried for some sort of, I don't know, sequence of rounded squares with varying shades of green and bold white outlines, at least that would look good on the cover of a blue book."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p {
				- "This example shows how the alternative text should be written such that if the image isn't "
				i id=a-short-phrase-or-label-with-an-alternative-graphical-representation:-icons,-logos:img-available {
					a href="#img-available" available
				}
				- ", and the text is used instead, the text flows seamlessly into the surrounding text, as if the image had never been there in the first place."
			}
		}
		h6 id=text-that-has-been-rendered-to-a-graphic-for-typographical-effect {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.5"
			- " Text that has been rendered to a graphic for typographical effect"
			a class=self-link href="#text-that-has-been-rendered-to-a-graphic-for-typographical-effect"
		}
		p "Sometimes, an image just consists of text, and the purpose of the image is not to highlight the actual typographic effects used to render the text, but just to convey the text itself."
		p {
			- "In such cases, the "
			code id=text-that-has-been-rendered-to-a-graphic-for-typographical-effect:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must be present but must consist of the same text as written in the image itself."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Consider a graphic containing the text \"Earth Day\", but with the letters all decorated with flowers and plants. If the text is merely being used as a heading, to spice up the page for graphical users, then the correct alternative text is just the same text \"Earth Day\", and no mention need be made of the decorations:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"earthdayheading.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Earth Day\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p "An illuminated manuscript might use graphics for some of its images. The alternative text in such a situation is just the character that the image represents."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"initials/o.svg\""
					c- e="" alt
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"O\""
					c- p="" >
					- "nce upon a time and a long long time ago, late at night, when it was dark, over the hills, through the woods, across a great ocean, in a land far away, in a small house, on a hill, under a full moon..."
				}
			}
		}
		p "When an image is used to represent a character that cannot otherwise be represented in Unicode, for example gaiji, itaiji, or new characters such as novel currency symbols, the alternative text should be a more conventional way of writing the same thing, e.g. using the phonetic hiragana or katakana to give the character's pronunciation."
		div class=example {
			p "In this example from 1997, a new-fangled currency symbol that looks like a curly E with two bars in the middle instead of one is represented using an image. The alternative text gives the character's pronunciation."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Only "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"euro.png\""
					c- e="" alt
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"euro \""
					c- p="" >
					- "5.99!"
				}
			}
		}
		p "An image should not be used if characters would serve an identical purpose. Only when the text cannot be directly represented using text, e.g., because of decorations or because there is no appropriate character (as in the case of gaiji), would an image be appropriate."
		p class=note "If an author is tempted to use an image because their default system font does not support a given character, then web fonts are a better solution than images."
		h6 id=a-graphical-representation-of-some-of-the-surrounding-text {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.6"
			- " A graphical representation of some of the surrounding text"
			a class=self-link href="#a-graphical-representation-of-some-of-the-surrounding-text"
		}
		p {
			- "In many cases, the image is actually just supplementary, and its presence merely reinforces the surrounding text. In these cases, the "
			code id=a-graphical-representation-of-some-of-the-surrounding-text:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must be present but its value must be the empty string."
		}
		p "In general, an image falls into this category if removing the image doesn't make the page any less useful, but including the image makes it a lot easier for users of visual browsers to understand the concept."
		div class=example {
			p "A flowchart that repeats the previous paragraph in graphical form:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "The Network passes data to the Input Stream Preprocessor, which passes it to the Tokenizer, which passes it to the Tree Construction stage. From there, data goes to both the DOM and to Script Execution. Script Execution is linked to the DOM, and, using document.write(), passes data to the Tokenizer."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"images/parsing-model-overview.svg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
					}
				}
			}
			p {
				- "In these cases, it would be wrong to include alternative text that consists of just a caption. If a caption is to be included, then either the "
				code id=a-graphical-representation-of-some-of-the-surrounding-text:attr-title {
					a href="#attr-title" title
				}
				- " attribute can be used, or the "
				code id=a-graphical-representation-of-some-of-the-surrounding-text:the-figure-element {
					a href="#the-figure-element" figure
				}
				- " and "
				code id=a-graphical-representation-of-some-of-the-surrounding-text:the-figcaption-element {
					a href="#the-figcaption-element" figcaption
				}
				- " elements can be used. In the latter case, the image would in fact be a phrase or paragraph with an alternative graphical representation, and would thus require alternative text."
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- c="" "<!-- Using the title=\"\" attribute -->"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "The Network passes data to the Input Stream Preprocessor, which passes it to the Tokenizer, which passes it to the Tree Construction stage. From there, data goes to both the DOM and to Script Execution. Script Execution is linked to the DOM, and, using document.write(), passes data to the Tokenizer."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"images/parsing-model-overview.svg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- e="" title
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Flowchart representation of the parsing model.\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- c="" "<!-- Using <figure> and <figcaption> -->"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "The Network passes data to the Input Stream Preprocessor, which passes it to the Tokenizer, which passes it to the Tree Construction stage. From there, data goes to both the DOM and to Script Execution. Script Execution is linked to the DOM, and, using document.write(), passes data to the Tokenizer."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" figure
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"images/parsing-model-overview.svg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"The Network leads to"
						c- s="" " the Input Stream Preprocessor, which leads to the Tokenizer, which"
						c- s="" " leads to the Tree Construction stage. The Tree Construction stage"
						c- s="" " leads to two items. The first is Script Execution, which leads via"
						c- s="" " document.write() back to the Tokenizer. The second item from which"
						c- s="" " Tree Construction leads is the DOM. The DOM is related to the Script"
						c- s="" " Execution.\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" figcaption
					c- p="" >
					- "Flowchart representation of the parsing model."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" figcaption
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" figure
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			pre class=bad {
				code class=html {
					c- c="" "<!-- This is WRONG. Do not do this. Instead, do what the above examples do. -->"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "The Network passes data to the Input Stream Preprocessor, which passes it to the Tokenizer, which passes it to the Tree Construction stage. From there, data goes to both the DOM and to Script Execution. Script Execution is linked to the DOM, and, using document.write(), passes data to the Tokenizer."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"images/parsing-model-overview.svg\""
					c- e="" alt
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"Flowchart representation of the parsing model.\""
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- c="" "<!-- Never put the image's caption in the alt=\"\" attribute! -->"
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p "A graph that repeats the previous paragraph in graphical form:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "According to a study covering several billion pages, about 62% of documents on the web in 2007 triggered the Quirks rendering mode of web browsers, about 30% triggered the Almost Standards mode, and about 9% triggered the Standards mode."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"rendering-mode-pie-chart.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
					}
				}
			}
		}
		h6 id=ancillary-images {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.7"
			- " Ancillary images"
			a class=self-link href="#ancillary-images"
		}
		p {
			- "Sometimes, an image is not critical to the content, but is nonetheless neither purely decorative nor entirely redundant with the text. In these cases, the "
			code id=ancillary-images:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must be present, and its value should either be the empty string, or a textual representation of the information that the image conveys. If the image has a caption giving the image's title, then the "
			code id=ancillary-images:attr-img-alt-2 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute's value must not be empty (as that would be quite confusing for non-visual readers)."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Consider a news article about a political figure, in which the individual's face was shown in an image. The image is not purely decorative, as it is relevant to the story. The image is not entirely redundant with the story either, as it shows what the politician looks like. Whether any alternative text need be provided is an authoring decision, decided by whether the image influences the interpretation of the prose."
			p "In this first variant, the image is shown without context, and no alternative text is provided:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"president.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					- " Ahead of today's referendum, the President wrote an open letter to all registered voters. In it, she admitted that the country was divided."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "If the picture is just a face, there might be no value in describing it. It's of no interest to the reader whether the individual has red hair or blond hair, whether the individual has white skin or black skin, whether the individual has one eye or two eyes."
			p "However, if the picture is more dynamic, for instance showing the politician as angry, or particularly happy, or devastated, some alternative text would be useful in setting the tone of the article, a tone that might otherwise be missed:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"president.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"The President is sad.\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					- " Ahead of today's referendum, the President wrote an open letter to all registered voters. In it, she admitted that the country was divided. "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"president.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"The President is happy!\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					- " Ahead of today's referendum, the President wrote an open letter to all registered voters. In it, she admitted that the country was divided. "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "Whether the individual was \"sad\" or \"happy\" makes a difference to how the rest of the paragraph is to be interpreted: is she likely saying that she is unhappy with the country being divided, or is she saying that the prospect of a divided country is good for her political career? The interpretation varies based on the image."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "If the image has a caption, then including alternative text avoids leaving the non-visual user confused as to what the caption refers to."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Ahead of today's referendum, the President wrote an open letter to all registered voters. In it, she admitted that the country was divided."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" figure
						c- p="" >
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"president.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"A high forehead, cheerful disposition, and dark hair round out the President's face.\""
						c- p="" >
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" figcaption
						c- p="" >
						- " The President of Ruritania. Photo © 2014 PolitiPhoto. "
						c- p="" "</"
						c- f="" figcaption
						c- p="" >
						c- p="" "</"
						c- f="" figure
						c- p="" >
					}
				}
			}
		}
		h6 id=a-purely-decorative-image-that-doesn't-add-any-information {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.8"
			- " A purely decorative image that doesn't add any information"
			a class=self-link href="#a-purely-decorative-image-that-doesn't-add-any-information"
		}
		p "If an image is decorative but isn't especially page-specific — for example an image that forms part of a site-wide design scheme — the image should be specified in the site's CSS, not in the markup of the document."
		p {
			- "However, a decorative image that isn't discussed by the surrounding text but still has some relevance can be included in a page using the "
			code id=a-purely-decorative-image-that-doesn't-add-any-information:the-img-element {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " element. Such images are decorative, but still form part of the content. In these cases, the "
			code id=a-purely-decorative-image-that-doesn't-add-any-information:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must be present but its value must be the empty string."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Examples where the image is purely decorative despite being relevant would include things like a photo of the Black Rock City landscape in a blog post about an event at Burning Man, or an image of a painting inspired by a poem, on a page reciting that poem. The following snippet shows an example of the latter case (only the first verse is included in this snippet):"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					- "The Lady of Shalott"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"shalott.jpeg\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "On either side the river lie"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" br
					c- p="" >
					- " Long fields of barley and of rye,"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" br
					c- p="" >
					- " That clothe the wold and meet the sky;"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" br
					c- p="" >
					- " And through the field the road run by"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" br
					c- p="" >
					- " To many-tower'd Camelot;"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" br
					c- p="" >
					- " And up and down the people go,"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" br
					c- p="" >
					- " Gazing where the lilies blow"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" br
					c- p="" >
					- " Round an island there below,"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" br
					c- p="" >
					- " The island of Shalott."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		h6 id=a-group-of-images-that-form-a-single-larger-picture-with-no-links {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.9"
			- " A group of images that form a single larger picture with no links"
			a class=self-link href="#a-group-of-images-that-form-a-single-larger-picture-with-no-links"
		}
		p {
			- "When a picture has been sliced into smaller image files that are then displayed together to form the complete picture again, one of the images must have its "
			code id=a-group-of-images-that-form-a-single-larger-picture-with-no-links:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute set as per the relevant rules that would be appropriate for the picture as a whole, and then all the remaining images must have their "
			code id=a-group-of-images-that-form-a-single-larger-picture-with-no-links:attr-img-alt-2 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute set to the empty string."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "In the following example, a picture representing a company logo for "
				span "XYZ Corp"
				- " has been split into two pieces, the first containing the letters \"XYZ\" and the second with the word \"Corp\". The alternative text (\"XYZ Corp\") is all in the first image."
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"logo1.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"XYZ Corp\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"logo2.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In the following example, a rating is shown as three filled stars and two empty stars. While the alternative text could have been \"★★★☆☆\", the author has instead decided to more helpfully give the rating in the form \"3 out of 5\". That is the alternative text of the first image, and the rest have blank alternative text."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Rating: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" meter
					c- e="" max
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "5"
					c- e="" value
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "3"
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"1\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"3 out of 5\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"1\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"1\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"0\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"0\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" >
					}
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" meter
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		h6 id=a-group-of-images-that-form-a-single-larger-picture-with-links {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.10"
			- " A group of images that form a single larger picture with links"
			a class=self-link href="#a-group-of-images-that-form-a-single-larger-picture-with-links"
		}
		p {
			- "Generally, "
			a href="#image-map" id=a-group-of-images-that-form-a-single-larger-picture-with-links:image-map "image maps"
			- " should be used instead of slicing an image for links."
		}
		p {
			- "However, if an image is indeed sliced and any of the components of the sliced picture are the sole contents of links, then one image per link must have alternative text in its "
			code id=a-group-of-images-that-form-a-single-larger-picture-with-links:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute representing the purpose of the link."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In the following example, a picture representing the flying spaghetti monster emblem, with each of the left noodly appendages and the right noodly appendages in different images, so that the user can pick the left side or the right side in an adventure."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					- "The Church"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "You come across a flying spaghetti monster. Which side of His Noodliness do you wish to reach out for?"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					strong {
						c- p="" <
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" a
						c- e="" href
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"?go=left\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"fsm-left.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Left side. \""
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" a
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"fsm-middle.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" a
						c- e="" href
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"?go=right\""
						c- p="" ><
						c- f="" img
						c- e="" src
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"fsm-right.png\""
						c- e="" alt
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"Right side.\""
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" a
						c- p="" "></"
						c- f="" p
						c- p="" >
					}
				}
			}
		}
		h6 id=a-key-part-of-the-content {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.11"
			- " A key part of the content"
			a class=self-link href="#a-key-part-of-the-content"
		}
		p {
			- "In some cases, the image is a critical part of the content. This could be the case, for instance, on a page that is part of a photo gallery. The image is the whole "
			em point
			- " of the page containing it."
		}
		p "How to provide alternative text for an image that is a key part of the content depends on the image's provenance."
		dl {
			dt "The general case"
			dd {
				p {
					- "When it is possible for detailed alternative text to be provided, for example if the image is part of a series of screenshots in a magazine review, or part of a comic strip, or is a photograph in a blog entry about that photograph, text that can serve as a substitute for the image must be given as the contents of the "
					code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-img-alt {
						a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
					}
					- " attribute."
				}
				div class=example {
					p "A screenshot in a gallery of screenshots for a new OS, with some alternative text:"
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"KDE%20Light%20desktop.png\""
								c- e="" alt
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"The desktop is blue, with icons along the left hand side in"
								c- s="" " two columns, reading System, Home, K-Mail, etc. A window is"
								c- s="" " open showing that menus wrap to a second line if they"
								c- s="" " cannot fit in the window. The window has a list of icons"
								c- s="" " along the top, with an address bar below it, a list of"
								c- s="" " icons for tabs along the left edge, a status bar on the"
								c- s="" " bottom, and two panes in the middle. The desktop has a bar"
								c- s="" " at the bottom of the screen with a few buttons, a pager, a"
								c- s="" " list of open applications, and a clock.\""
								c- p="" >
							}
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							- "Screenshot of a KDE desktop."
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
				}
				div class=example {
					p "A graph in a financial report:"
					pre {
						code class=html {
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"sales.gif\""
								c- e="" title
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"Sales graph\""
								c- e="" alt
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"From 1998 to 2005, sales increased by the following percentages"
								c- s="" " with each year: 624%, 75%, 138%, 40%, 35%, 9%, 21%\""
								c- p="" >
							}
						}
					}
					p {
						- "Note that \"sales graph\" would be inadequate alternative text for a sales graph. Text that would be a good "
						em caption
						- " is not generally suitable as replacement text."
					}
				}
			}
			dt "Images that defy a complete description"
			dd {
				p "In certain cases, the nature of the image might be such that providing thorough alternative text is impractical. For example, the image could be indistinct, or could be a complex fractal, or could be a detailed topographical map."
				p {
					- "In these cases, the "
					code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-img-alt-2 {
						a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
					}
					- " attribute must contain some suitable alternative text, but it may be somewhat brief."
				}
				div class=example {
					p "Sometimes there simply is no text that can do justice to an image. For example, there is little that can be said to usefully describe a Rorschach inkblot test. However, a description, even if brief, is still better than nothing:"
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"/commons/a/a7/Rorschach1.jpg\""
								c- e="" alt
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"A shape with left-right"
								c- s="" " symmetry with indistinct edges, with a small gap in the center, two"
								c- s="" " larger gaps offset slightly from the center, with two similar gaps"
								c- s="" " under them. The outline is wider in the top half than the bottom"
								c- s="" " half, with the sides extending upwards higher than the center, and"
								c- s="" " the center extending below the sides.\""
								c- p="" >
							}
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							- "A black outline of the first of the ten cards in the Rorschach inkblot test."
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p "Note that the following would be a very bad use of alternative text:"
					pre class=bad {
						code class=html {
							c- c="" "<!-- This example is wrong. Do not copy it. -->"
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" img
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"/commons/a/a7/Rorschach1.jpg\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"A black outline"
							c- s="" " of the first of the ten cards in the Rorschach inkblot test.\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							- "A black outline of the first of the ten cards in the Rorschach inkblot test."
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p "Including the caption in the alternative text like this isn't useful because it effectively duplicates the caption for users who don't have images, taunting them twice yet not helping them any more than if they had only read or heard the caption once."
				}
				div class=example {
					p "Another example of an image that defies full description is a fractal, which, by definition, is infinite in detail."
					p "The following example shows one possible way of providing alternative text for the full view of an image of the Mandelbrot set."
					pre {
						code class=html {
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"ms1.jpeg\""
								c- e="" alt
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"The Mandelbrot set appears as a cardioid with"
								c- s="" "its cusp on the real axis in the positive direction, with a smaller"
								c- s="" "bulb aligned along the same center line, touching it in the negative"
								c- s="" "direction, and with these two shapes being surrounded by smaller bulbs"
								c- s="" "of various sizes.\""
								c- p="" >
							}
						}
					}
				}
				div class=example {
					p "Similarly, a photograph of a person's face, for example in a biography, can be considered quite relevant and key to the content, but it can be hard to fully substitute text for:"
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" section
							c- e="" class
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"bio\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
							- "A Biography of Isaac Asimov"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							- "Born "
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" b
							c- p="" >
							- "Isaak Yudovich Ozimov"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" b
							c- p="" >
							- " in 1920, Isaac was a prolific author."
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" ><
							c- f="" img
							c- e="" src
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"headpics/asimov.jpeg\""
							c- e="" alt
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"Isaac Asimov had dark hair, a tall forehead, and wore glasses."
							c- s="" " Later in life, he wore long white sideburns.\""
							c- p="" "></"
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							- "Asimov was born in Russia, and moved to the US when he was three years old."
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							- ...
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" section
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p "In such cases it is unnecessary (and indeed discouraged) to include a reference to the presence of the image itself in the alternative text, since such text would be redundant with the browser itself reporting the presence of the image. For example, if the alternative text was \"A photo of Isaac Asimov\", then a conforming user agent might read that out as \"(Image) A photo of Isaac Asimov\" rather than the more useful \"(Image) Isaac Asimov had dark hair, a tall forehead, and wore glasses...\"."
				}
			}
			dt id=unknown-images "Images whose contents are not known"
			dd {
				p "In some unfortunate cases, there might be no alternative text available at all, either because the image is obtained in some automated fashion without any associated alternative text (e.g., a webcam), or because the page is being generated by a script using user-provided images where the user did not provide suitable or usable alternative text (e.g. photograph sharing sites), or because the author does not themself know what the images represent (e.g. a blind photographer sharing an image on their blog)."
				p {
					- "In such cases, the "
					code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-img-alt-3 {
						a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
					}
					- " attribute may be omitted, but one of the following conditions must be met as well:"
				}
				ul {
					li id=figcaption-as-alt-condition {
						p {
							- "The "
							code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:the-img-element {
								a href="#the-img-element" img
							}
							- " element is in a "
							code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:the-figure-element {
								a href="#the-figure-element" figure
							}
							- " element that contains a "
							code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:the-figcaption-element {
								a href="#the-figcaption-element" figcaption
							}
							- " element that contains content other than "
							a href="#inter-element-whitespace" id=a-key-part-of-the-content:inter-element-whitespace "inter-element whitespace"
							- ", and, ignoring the "
							code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:the-figcaption-element-2 {
								a href="#the-figcaption-element" figcaption
							}
							- " element and its descendants, the "
							code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:the-figure-element-2 {
								a href="#the-figure-element" figure
							}
							- " element has no "
							a href="#flow-content-2" id=a-key-part-of-the-content:flow-content-2 "flow content"
							- " descendants other than "
							a href="#inter-element-whitespace" id=a-key-part-of-the-content:inter-element-whitespace-2 "inter-element whitespace"
							- " and the "
							code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:the-img-element-2 {
								a href="#the-img-element" img
							}
							- " element."
						}
					}
					li {
						p {
							- "The "
							code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-title {
								a href="#attr-title" title
							}
							- " attribute is present and has a non-empty value."
						}
						p class=note {
							- "Relying on the "
							code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-title-2 {
								a href="#attr-title" title
							}
							- " attribute is currently discouraged as many user agents do not expose the attribute in an accessible manner as required by this specification (e.g. requiring a pointing device such as a mouse to cause a tooltip to appear, which excludes keyboard-only users and touch-only users, such as anyone with a modern phone or tablet)."
						}
					}
				}
				p class=note {
					- "Such cases are to be kept to an absolute minimum. If there is even the slightest possibility of the author having the ability to provide real alternative text, then it would not be acceptable to omit the "
					code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-img-alt-4 {
						a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
					}
					- " attribute."
				}
				div class=example {
					p "A photo on a photo-sharing site, if the site received the image with no metadata other than the caption, could be marked up as follows:"
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"1100670787_6a7c664aef.jpg\""
								c- p="" >
							}
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							- "Bubbles traveled everywhere with us."
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p "It would be better, however, if a detailed description of the important parts of the image obtained from the user and included on the page."
				}
				div class=example {
					p "A blind user's blog in which a photo taken by the user is shown. Initially, the user might not have any idea what the photo they took shows:"
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" article
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
							- "I took a photo"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							- "I went out today and took a photo!"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"photo2.jpeg\""
								c- p="" >
							}
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							- "A photograph taken blindly from my front porch."
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" article
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p "Eventually though, the user might obtain a description of the image from their friends and could then include alternative text:"
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" article
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
							- "I took a photo"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" h1
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							- "I went out today and took a photo!"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"photo2.jpeg\""
								c- e="" alt
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"The photograph shows my squirrel"
								c- s="" " feeder hanging from the edge of my roof. It is half full, but there"
								c- s="" " are no squirrels around. In the background, out-of-focus trees fill the"
								c- s="" " shot. The feeder is made of wood with a metal grate, and it contains"
								c- s="" " peanuts. The edge of the roof is wooden too, and is painted white"
								c- s="" " with light blue streaks.\""
								c- p="" >
							}
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							- "A photograph taken blindly from my front porch."
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figcaption
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" figure
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" article
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
				}
				div class=example {
					p {
						- "Sometimes the entire point of the image is that a textual description is not available, and the user is to provide the description. For instance, the point of a CAPTCHA image is to see if the user can literally read the graphic. Here is one way to mark up a CAPTCHA (note the "
						code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-title-3 {
							a href="#attr-title" title
						}
						- " attribute):"
					}
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" ><
							c- f="" label
							c- p="" >
							- "What does this image say? "
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"captcha.cgi?id=8934\""
								c- e="" title
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"CAPTCHA\""
								c- p="" >
							}
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" input
							c- e="" type
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" text
							c- e="" name
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" captcha
							c- p="" "></"
							c- f="" label
							c- p="" >
							- " (If you cannot see the image, you can use an "
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" a
							c- e="" href
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"?audio\""
							c- p="" >
							- audio
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" a
							c- p="" >
							- " test instead.)"
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" p
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p "Another example would be software that displays images and asks for alternative text precisely for the purpose of then writing a page with correct alternative text. Such a page could have a table of images, like this:"
					pre {
						code class=html {
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" table
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" thead
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" tr
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" th
							c- p="" >
							- " Image "
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" th
							c- p="" >
							- " Description "
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" tbody
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" tr
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" td
							c- p="" >
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"2421.png\""
								c- e="" title
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"Image 640 by 100, filename 'banner.gif'\""
								c- p="" >
							}
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" td
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" input
							c- e="" name
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"alt2421\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" tr
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" td
							c- p="" >
							strong {
								c- p="" <
								c- f="" img
								c- e="" src
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"2422.png\""
								c- e="" title
								c- o="" "="
								c- s="" "\"Image 200 by 480, filename 'ad3.gif'\""
								c- p="" >
							}
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" td
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" <
							c- f="" input
							c- e="" name
							c- o="" "="
							c- s="" "\"alt2422\""
							c- p="" >
							c- p="" "</"
							c- f="" table
							c- p="" >
						}
					}
					p {
						- "Notice that even in this example, as much useful information as possible is still included in the "
						code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-title-4 {
							a href="#attr-title" title
						}
						- " attribute."
					}
				}
				p class=note {
					- "Since some users cannot use images at all (e.g. because they have a very slow connection, or because they are using a text-only browser, or because they are listening to the page being read out by a hands-free automobile voice web browser, or simply because they are blind), the "
					code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-img-alt-5 {
						a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
					}
					- " attribute is only allowed to be omitted rather than being provided with replacement text when no alternative text is available and none can be made available, as in the above examples. Lack of effort from the part of the author is not an acceptable reason for omitting the "
					code id=a-key-part-of-the-content:attr-img-alt-6 {
						a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
					}
					- " attribute."
				}
			}
		}
		h6 id=an-image-not-intended-for-the-user {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.12"
			- " An image not intended for the user"
			a class=self-link href="#an-image-not-intended-for-the-user"
		}
		p {
			- "Generally authors should avoid using "
			code id=an-image-not-intended-for-the-user:the-img-element {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " elements for purposes other than showing images."
		}
		p {
			- "If an "
			code id=an-image-not-intended-for-the-user:the-img-element-2 {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " element is being used for purposes other than showing an image, e.g. as part of a service to count page views, then the "
			code id=an-image-not-intended-for-the-user:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute must be the empty string."
		}
		p {
			- "In such cases, the "
			code id=an-image-not-intended-for-the-user:attr-dim-width {
				a href="#attr-dim-width" width
			}
			- " and "
			code id=an-image-not-intended-for-the-user:attr-dim-height {
				a href="#attr-dim-height" height
			}
			- " attributes should both be set to zero."
		}
		h6 id=an-image-in-an-e-mail-or-private-document-intended-for-a-specific-person-who-is-known-to-be-able-to-view-images {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.13"
			- " An image in an email or private document intended for a specific person who is known to be able to view images"
			a class=self-link href="#an-image-in-an-e-mail-or-private-document-intended-for-a-specific-person-who-is-known-to-be-able-to-view-images"
		}
		p {
			i "This section does not apply to documents that are publicly accessible, or whose target audience is not necessarily personally known to the author, such as documents on a web site, emails sent to public mailing lists, or software documentation."
		}
		p {
			- "When an image is included in a private communication (such as an HTML email) aimed at a specific person who is known to be able to view images, the "
			code id=an-image-in-an-e-mail-or-private-document-intended-for-a-specific-person-who-is-known-to-be-able-to-view-images:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute may be omitted. However, even in such cases authors are strongly urged to include alternative text (as appropriate according to the kind of image involved, as described in the above entries), so that the email is still usable should the user use a mail client that does not support images, or should the document be forwarded on to other users whose abilities might not include easily seeing images."
		}
		h6 id=guidance-for-markup-generators {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.14"
			- " Guidance for markup generators"
			a class=self-link href="#guidance-for-markup-generators"
		}
		p "Markup generators (such as WYSIWYG authoring tools) should, wherever possible, obtain alternative text from their users. However, it is recognized that in many cases, this will not be possible."
		p "For images that are the sole contents of links, markup generators should examine the link target to determine the title of the target, or the URL of the target, and use information obtained in this manner as the alternative text."
		p {
			- "For images that have captions, markup generators should use the "
			code id=guidance-for-markup-generators:the-figure-element {
				a href="#the-figure-element" figure
			}
			- " and "
			code id=guidance-for-markup-generators:the-figcaption-element {
				a href="#the-figcaption-element" figcaption
			}
			- " elements, or the "
			code id=guidance-for-markup-generators:attr-title {
				a href="#attr-title" title
			}
			- " attribute, to provide the image's caption."
		}
		p {
			- "As a last resort, implementers should either set the "
			code id=guidance-for-markup-generators:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute to the empty string, under the assumption that the image is a purely decorative image that doesn't add any information but is still specific to the surrounding content, or omit the "
			code id=guidance-for-markup-generators:attr-img-alt-2 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute altogether, under the assumption that the image is a key part of the content."
		}
		p {
			- "Markup generators may specify a "
			dfn id=attr-img-generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt {
				code generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt
			}
			- " attribute on "
			code id=guidance-for-markup-generators:the-img-element {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " elements for which they have been unable to obtain alternative text and for which they have therefore omitted the "
			code id=guidance-for-markup-generators:attr-img-alt-3 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute. The value of this attribute must be the empty string. Documents containing such attributes are not conforming, but conformance checkers will "
			a href="#guidance-for-conformance-checkers" "silently ignore"
			- " this error."
		}
		p class=note {
			- "This is intended to avoid markup generators from being pressured into replacing the error of omitting the "
			code id=guidance-for-markup-generators:attr-img-alt-4 {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute with the even more egregious error of providing phony alternative text, because state-of-the-art automated conformance checkers cannot distinguish phony alternative text from correct alternative text."
		}
		p "Markup generators should generally avoid using the image's own filename as the alternative text. Similarly, markup generators should avoid generating alternative text from any content that will be equally available to presentation user agents (e.g., web browsers)."
		p class=note "This is because once a page is generated, it will typically not be updated, whereas the browsers that later read the page can be updated by the user, therefore the browser is likely to have more up-to-date and finely-tuned heuristics than the markup generator did when generating the page."
		h6 id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers {
			span class=secno "4.8.4.4.15"
			- " Guidance for conformance checkers"
			a class=self-link href="#guidance-for-conformance-checkers"
		}
		p {
			- "A conformance checker must report the lack of an "
			code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:attr-img-alt {
				a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
			}
			- " attribute as an error unless one of the conditions listed below applies:"
		}
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					- "The "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:the-img-element {
						a href="#the-img-element" img
					}
					- " element is in a "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:the-figure-element {
						a href="#the-figure-element" figure
					}
					- " element that satisfies "
					a href="#figcaption-as-alt-condition" "the conditions described above"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:the-img-element-2 {
						a href="#the-img-element" img
					}
					- " element has a "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:attr-title {
						a href="#attr-title" title
					}
					- " attribute with a value that is not the empty string (also as "
					a href="#unknown-images" "described above"
					- ")."
				}
			}
			li {
				p "The conformance checker has been configured to assume that the document is an email or document intended for a specific person who is known to be able to view images."
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:the-img-element-3 {
						a href="#the-img-element" img
					}
					- " element has a (non-conforming) "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:attr-img-generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt {
						a href="#attr-img-generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt" generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt
					}
					- " attribute whose value is the empty string. A conformance checker that is not reporting the lack of an "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:attr-img-alt-2 {
						a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
					}
					- " attribute as an error must also not report the presence of the empty "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:attr-img-generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt-2 {
						a href="#attr-img-generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt" generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt
					}
					- " attribute as an error. (This case does not represent a case where the document is conforming, only that the generator could not determine appropriate alternative text — validators are not required to show an error in this case, because such an error might encourage markup generators to include bogus alternative text purely in an attempt to silence validators. Naturally, conformance checkers "
					em may
					- " report the lack of an "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:attr-img-alt-3 {
						a href="#attr-img-alt" alt
					}
					- " attribute as an error even in the presence of the "
					code id=guidance-for-conformance-checkers:attr-img-generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt-3 {
						a href="#attr-img-generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt" generator-unable-to-provide-required-alt
					}
					- " attribute; for example, there could be a user option to report "
					em all
					- " conformance errors even those that might be the more or less inevitable result of using a markup generator.)"
				}
			}
		}
		h4 id=the-iframe-element {
			span class=secno "4.8.5"
			- " The "
			dfn data-dfn-type=element {
				code iframe
