		h5 id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality {
			span class=secno "4.10.1.7"
			- " The difference between the field type, the autofill field name, and the input modality"
			a class=self-link href="#the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:attr-input-type {
				a href="#attr-input-type" type
			}
			- ", "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:attr-fe-autocomplete {
				a href="#attr-fe-autocomplete" autocomplete
			}
			- ", and "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:attr-inputmode {
				a href="#attr-inputmode" inputmode
			}
			- " attributes can seem confusingly similar. For instance, in all three cases, the string \""
			code email
			- "\" is a valid value. This section attempts to illustrate the difference between the three attributes and provides advice suggesting how to use them."
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:attr-input-type-2 {
				a href="#attr-input-type" type
			}
			- " attribute on "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:the-input-element {
				a href="#the-input-element" input
			}
			- " elements decides what kind of control the user agent will use to expose the field. Choosing between different values of this attribute is the same choice as choosing whether to use an "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:the-input-element-2 {
				a href="#the-input-element" input
			}
			- " element, a "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:the-textarea-element {
				a href="#the-textarea-element" textarea
			}
			- " element, a "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:the-select-element {
				a href="#the-select-element" select
			}
			- " element, etc."
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:attr-fe-autocomplete-2 {
				a href="#attr-fe-autocomplete" autocomplete
			}
			- " attribute, in contrast, describes what the value that the user will enter actually represents. Choosing between different values of this attribute is the same choice as choosing what the label for the element will be."
		}
		p {
			- "First, consider telephone numbers. If a page is asking for a telephone number from the user, the right form control to use is "
			code id="the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:telephone-state-(type=tel)" {
				a href="#telephone-state-(type=tel)" "<input type=tel>"
			}
			- ". However, which "
			code id=the-difference-between-the-field-type,-the-autofill-field-name,-and-the-input-modality:attr-fe-autocomplete-3 {
				a href="#attr-fe-autocomplete" autocomplete
			}
			- " value to use depends on which phone number the page is asking for, whether they expect a telephone number in the international format or just the local format, and so forth."
		}
		p "For example, a page that forms part of a checkout process on an e-commerce site for a customer buying a gift to be shipped to a friend might need both the buyer's telephone number (in case of payment issues) and the friend's telephone number (in case of delivery issues). If the site expects international phone numbers (with the country code prefix), this could thus look like this:"
		pre {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" p
				c- p="" ><
				c- f="" label
				c- p="" >
				- "Your phone number: "
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" input
				c- e="" type
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" tel
