			code id="the-input-element:dom-textarea/input-setrangetext-3" {
				a href="#dom-textarea/input-setrangetext" "setRangeText()"
			}
			- " and "
			code id="the-input-element:dom-textarea/input-setselectionrange-2" {
				a href="#dom-textarea/input-setselectionrange" "setSelectionRange()"
			}
			- " methods, the "
			code id=the-input-element:dom-input-stepup-2 {
				a href="#dom-input-stepup" "stepUp()"
			}
			- " and "
			code id=the-input-element:dom-input-stepdown-2 {
				a href="#dom-input-stepdown" "stepDown()"
			}
			- " methods, and the "
			code id=the-input-element:event-input {
				a data-x-internal=event-input href="https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#event-type-input" input
			}
			- " and "
			code id=the-input-element:event-change {
				a href="#event-change" change
			}
			- " events "
			dfn id=concept-input-apply apply
			- " to an "
			code id=the-input-element:the-input-element-2 {
				a href="#the-input-element" input
			}
			- " element depends on the state of its "
			code id=the-input-element:attr-input-type-28 {
				a href="#attr-input-type" type
			}
			- " attribute. The subsections that define each type also clearly define in normative \"bookkeeping\" sections which of these feature apply, and which "
			dfn id=do-not-apply "do not apply"
			- ", to each type. The behavior of these features depends on whether they apply or not, as defined in their various sections (q.v. for "
			a href="#common-input-element-attributes" "content attributes"
			- ", for "
			a href="#common-input-element-apis" APIs
			- ", for "
			a href="#common-input-element-events" events
			- ")."
		}
		p {
			- "The following table is non-normative and summarizes which of those content attributes, IDL attributes, methods, and events "
			a href="#concept-input-apply" id=the-input-element:concept-input-apply apply
			- " to each state:"
		}
