					- " attributes of "
					var target
					- ", and set "
					var target
					- "'s bitmap to the same bitmap (so that they are shared)."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "If "
					var context
					- "'s "
					a href="#offscreencontext2d-alpha" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:offscreencontext2d-alpha-3 alpha
					- " flag is set to true, initialize all the pixels of "
					var context
					- "'s "
					a href="#offscreencontext2d-bitmap" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:offscreencontext2d-bitmap-4 bitmap
					- " to "
					a data-x-internal=transparent-black href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#transparent-black" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:transparent-black "transparent black"
					- ". Otherwise, initialize the pixels to "
					a data-x-internal=opaque-black href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#opaque-black" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:opaque-black "opaque black"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Return "
					var context
					- .
				}
			}
		}
		p class=note {
			- "Implementations are encouraged to short-circuit the graphics update steps of the "
			a href="#window-event-loop" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:window-event-loop "window event loop"
			- " for the purposes of updating the contents of a "
			a href="#offscreencanvas-placeholder" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:offscreencanvas-placeholder {
				- "placeholder "
				code canvas
				- " element"
			}
			- " to the display. This could mean, for example, that the bitmap contents are copied directly to a graphics buffer that is mapped to the physical display location of the "
			a href="#offscreencanvas-placeholder" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:offscreencanvas-placeholder-2 {
				- "placeholder "
				code canvas
				- " element"
			}
			- ". This or similar short-circuiting approaches can significantly reduce display latency, especially in cases where the "
			code id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:offscreencanvas-6 {
				a href="#offscreencanvas" OffscreenCanvas
			}
			- " is updated from a "
			a href="#worker-event-loop-2" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:worker-event-loop-2 "worker event loop"
			- " and the "
			a href="#window-event-loop" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:window-event-loop-2 "window event loop"
			- " of the "
			a href="#offscreencanvas-placeholder" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:offscreencanvas-placeholder-3 {
				- "placeholder "
				code canvas
				- " element"
			}
			- " is busy. However, such shortcuts cannot have any script-observable side-effects. This means that the committed bitmap still needs to be sent to the "
			a href="#offscreencanvas-placeholder" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:offscreencanvas-placeholder-4 {
				- "placeholder "
				code canvas
				- " element"
			}
			- ", in case the element is used as a "
			code id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:canvasimagesource {
				a href="#canvasimagesource" CanvasImageSource
			}
			- ", as an "
			code id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:imagebitmapsource {
				a href="#imagebitmapsource" ImageBitmapSource
			}
			- ", or in case "
			code id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:dom-canvas-todataurl {
				a href="#dom-canvas-todataurl" "toDataURL()"
			}
			- " or "
			code id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:dom-canvas-toblob {
				a href="#dom-canvas-toblob" "toBlob()"
			}
			- " are called on it."
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			dfn data-dfn-for=OffscreenCanvas data-dfn-type=attribute id=offscreencontext2d-canvas {
				code canvas
			}
			- " attribute, on getting, must return this "
			code id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:offscreencanvasrenderingcontext2d-24 {
				a href="#offscreencanvasrenderingcontext2d" OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D
			}
			- "'s "
			a href="#associated-offscreencanvas-object" id=the-offscreen-2d-rendering-context:associated-offscreencanvas-object-3 {
				- "associated "
				code OffscreenCanvas
				- " object"
			}
			- ". "
		}
		h5 id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction {
			span class=secno "4.12.5.4"
			- " Color spaces and color space conversion"
			a class=self-link href="#colour-spaces-and-colour-correction"
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			code id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:the-canvas-element {
				a href="#the-canvas-element" canvas
			}
			- " APIs provide mechanisms for specifying the color space of the canvas's backing store. The default backing store color space for all canvas APIs is "
			a data-x-internal='srgb' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#valdef-color-srgb" id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:'srgb' 'srgb'
			- .
		}
		p {
			a data-x-internal=converting-colors href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#color-conversion" id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:converting-colors "Color space conversion"
			- " must be applied to the canvas's backing store when rendering the canvas to the output device. This color space conversion must be identical to the color space conversion that would be applied to an "
			code id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:the-img-element {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " element with a color profile that specifies the same "
			a href="#concept-canvas-color-space" id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:concept-canvas-color-space "color space"
			- " as the canvas's backing store."
		}
		p {
			- "When drawing content to a 2D context, all inputs must be "
			a data-x-internal=converting-colors href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#color-conversion" id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:converting-colors-2 converted
			- " to the "
			a href="#concept-canvas-color-space" id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:concept-canvas-color-space-2 "context's color space"
			- " before drawing. Interpolation of gradient color stops must be performed on color values after conversion to the "
			a href="#concept-canvas-color-space" id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:concept-canvas-color-space-3 "context's color space"
			- ". Alpha blending must be performed on values after conversion to the "
			a href="#concept-canvas-color-space" id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:concept-canvas-color-space-4 "context's color space"
			- .
		}
		p class=note {
			- "There do not exist any inputs to a 2D context for which the color space is undefined. The color space for CSS colors is defined in "
			cite "CSS Color"
			- ". The color space for images that specify no color profile information is assumed to be "
			a data-x-internal='srgb' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#valdef-color-srgb" id=colour-spaces-and-colour-correction:'srgb'-2 'srgb'
			- ", as specified in the "
			a href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#untagged" "Color Spaces of Untagged Colors"
			- " section of "
			cite "CSS Color"
			- ". "
			a href="#refsCSSCOLOR" "[CSSCOLOR]"
		}
		h5 id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file {
			span class=secno "4.12.5.5"
			- " Serializing bitmaps to a file"
			a class=self-link href="#serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file"
		}
		p {
			- "When a user agent is to create "
			dfn id=a-serialisation-of-the-bitmap-as-a-file "a serialization of the bitmap as a file"
			- ", given a "
			var type
			- " and an optional "
			var quality
			- ", it must create an image file in the format given by "
			var type
			- ". If an error occurs during the creation of the image file (e.g. an internal encoder error), then the result of the serialization is null. "
			a href="#refsPNG" "[PNG]"
		}
		p {
			- "The image file's pixel data must be the bitmap's pixel data scaled to one image pixel per coordinate space unit, and if the file format used supports encoding resolution metadata, the resolution must be given as 96dpi (one image pixel per "
			a data-x-internal='px' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values/#px" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:'px' "CSS pixel"
			- ")."
		}
		p {
			- "If "
			var type
			- " is supplied, then it must be interpreted as a "
			a data-x-internal=mime-type href="https://mimesniff.spec.whatwg.org/#mime-type" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:mime-type "MIME type"
			- " giving the format to use. If the type has any parameters, then it must be treated as not supported."
		}
		p class=example {
			- "For example, the value \""
			code id="serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:image/png" {
				a href="#image/png" "image/png"
			}
			- "\" would mean to generate a PNG image, the value \""
			code id="serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:image/jpeg" {
				a href="#image/jpeg" "image/jpeg"
			}
			- "\" would mean to generate a JPEG image, and the value \""
			code id="serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:image/svg+xml" {
				a href="#image/svg+xml" "image/svg+xml"
			}
			- "\" would mean to generate an SVG image (which would require that the user agent track how the bitmap was generated, an unlikely, though potentially awesome, feature)."
		}
		p {
			- "User agents must support PNG (\""
			code id="serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:image/png-2" {
				a href="#image/png" "image/png"
			}
			- "\"). User agents may support other types. If the user agent does not support the requested type, then it must create the file using the PNG format. "
			a href="#refsPNG" "[PNG]"
		}
		p {
			- "User agents must "
			a data-x-internal=converted-to-ascii-lowercase href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ascii-lowercase" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:converted-to-ascii-lowercase "convert the provided type to ASCII lowercase"
			- " before establishing if they support that type."
		}
		p {
			- "For image types that do not support an alpha channel, the serialized image must be the bitmap image composited onto an "
			a data-x-internal=opaque-black href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#opaque-black" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:opaque-black "opaque black"
			- " background using the "
			a data-x-internal=gcop-source-over href="https://drafts.fxtf.org/compositing/#porterduffcompositingoperators_srcover" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:gcop-source-over source-over
			- " compositing operator."
		}
		p {
			- "For image types that support color profiles, the serialized image must include a color profile indicating the color space of the underlying bitmap. For image types that do not support color profiles, the serialized image must be "
			a data-x-internal=converting-colors href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#color-conversion" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:converting-colors converted
			- " to the "
			a data-x-internal='srgb' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color/#valdef-color-srgb" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:'srgb' 'srgb'
			- " color space using "
			a data-x-internal='relative-colorimetric' href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-color-5/#valdef-color-profile-rendering-intent-relative-colorimetric" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:'relative-colorimetric' 'relative-colorimetric'
			- " rendering intent."
		}
		p class=note {
			- "Thus, in the 2D context, calling the "
			code id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:dom-context-2d-drawimage {
				a href="#dom-context-2d-drawimage" "drawImage()"
			}
			- " method to render the output of the "
			code id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:dom-canvas-todataurl {
				a href="#dom-canvas-todataurl" "toDataURL()"
			}
			- " or "
			code id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:dom-canvas-toblob {
				a href="#dom-canvas-toblob" "toBlob()"
			}
			- " method to the canvas, given the appropriate dimensions, has no visible effect beyond, at most, clipping colors of the canvas to a more narrow gamut."
		}
		p {
			- "If "
			var type
			- " is an image format that supports variable quality (such as \""
			code id="serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:image/jpeg-2" {
				a href="#image/jpeg" "image/jpeg"
			}
			- "\"), "
			var quality
			- " is given, and "
			var type
			- " is not \""
			code id="serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:image/png-3" {
				a href="#image/png" "image/png"
			}
			- "\", then, if "
			var quality
			a data-x-internal=js-number href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-ecmascript-language-types-number-type" id=serialising-bitmaps-to-a-file:js-number "is a Number"
			- " in the range 0.0 to 1.0 inclusive, the user agent must treat "
			var quality
			- " as the desired quality level. Otherwise, the user agent must use its default quality value, as if the "
			var quality
			- " argument had not been given."
		}
		p class=note {
			- "The use of type-testing here, instead of simply declaring "
			var quality
			- " as a Web IDL "
			code double
			- ", is a historical artifact."
		}
		p class=note "Different implementations can have slightly different interpretations of \"quality\". When the quality is not specified, an implementation-specific default is used that represents a reasonable compromise between compression ratio, image quality, and encoding time."
		h5 id=security-with-canvas-elements {
			span class=secno "4.12.5.6"
			- " Security with "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:the-canvas-element {
				a href="#the-canvas-element" canvas
			}
			- " elements"
			a class=self-link href="#security-with-canvas-elements"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			strong "Information leakage"
			- " can occur if scripts from one "
			a href="#concept-origin" id=security-with-canvas-elements:concept-origin origin
			- " can access information (e.g. read pixels) from images from another origin (one that isn't the "
			a href="#same-origin" id=security-with-canvas-elements:same-origin same
			- ")."
		}
		p {
			- "To mitigate this, bitmaps used with "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:the-canvas-element-2 {
				a href="#the-canvas-element" canvas
			}
			- " elements, "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:offscreencanvas {
				a href="#offscreencanvas" OffscreenCanvas
			}
			- " objects, and "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:imagebitmap {
				a href="#imagebitmap" ImageBitmap
			}
			- " objects are defined to have a flag indicating whether they are "
			a href="#concept-canvas-origin-clean" id=security-with-canvas-elements:concept-canvas-origin-clean origin-clean
			- ". All bitmaps start with their "
			a href="#concept-canvas-origin-clean" id=security-with-canvas-elements:concept-canvas-origin-clean-2 origin-clean
			- " set to true. The flag is set to false when cross-origin images are used."
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:dom-canvas-todataurl {
				a href="#dom-canvas-todataurl" "toDataURL()"
			}
			- ", "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:dom-canvas-toblob {
				a href="#dom-canvas-toblob" "toBlob()"
			}
			- ", and "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:dom-context-2d-getimagedata {
				a href="#dom-context-2d-getimagedata" "getImageData()"
			}
			- " methods check the flag and will throw a "
			a data-x-internal=securityerror href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#securityerror" id=security-with-canvas-elements:securityerror {
				- "\""
				code SecurityError
				- "\""
			}
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:domexception {
				a data-x-internal=domexception href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#dfn-DOMException" DOMException
			}
			- " rather than leak cross-origin data."
		}
		p {
			- "The value of the "
			a href="#concept-canvas-origin-clean" id=security-with-canvas-elements:concept-canvas-origin-clean-3 origin-clean
			- " flag is propagated from a source's bitmap to a new "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:imagebitmap-2 {
				a href="#imagebitmap" ImageBitmap
			}
			- " object by "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:dom-createimagebitmap {
				a href="#dom-createimagebitmap" "createImageBitmap()"
			}
			- ". Conversely, a destination "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:the-canvas-element-3 {
				a href="#the-canvas-element" canvas
			}
			- " element's bitmap will have its "
			a href="#concept-canvas-origin-clean" id=security-with-canvas-elements:concept-canvas-origin-clean-4 origin-clean
			- " flags set to false by "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:dom-context-2d-drawimage {
				a href="#dom-context-2d-drawimage" drawImage
			}
			- " if the source image is an "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:imagebitmap-3 {
				a href="#imagebitmap" ImageBitmap
			}
			- " object whose bitmap has its "
			a href="#concept-canvas-origin-clean" id=security-with-canvas-elements:concept-canvas-origin-clean-5 origin-clean
			- " flag set to false."
		}
		p {
			- "The flag can be reset in certain situations; for example, when changing the value of the "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:attr-canvas-width {
				a href="#attr-canvas-width" width
			}
			- " or the "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:attr-canvas-height {
				a href="#attr-canvas-height" height
			}
			- " content attribute of the "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:the-canvas-element-4 {
				a href="#the-canvas-element" canvas
			}
			- " element to which a "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:canvasrenderingcontext2d {
				a href="#canvasrenderingcontext2d" CanvasRenderingContext2D
			}
			- " is bound, the bitmap is cleared and its "
			a href="#concept-canvas-origin-clean" id=security-with-canvas-elements:concept-canvas-origin-clean-6 origin-clean
			- " flag is reset."
		}
		p {
			- "When using an "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:imagebitmaprenderingcontext {
				a href="#imagebitmaprenderingcontext" ImageBitmapRenderingContext
			}
			- ", the value of the "
			a href="#concept-canvas-origin-clean" id=security-with-canvas-elements:concept-canvas-origin-clean-7 origin-clean
			- " flag is propagated from "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:imagebitmap-4 {
				a href="#imagebitmap" ImageBitmap
			}
			- " objects when they are transferred to the "
			code id=security-with-canvas-elements:the-canvas-element-5 {
				a href="#the-canvas-element" canvas
			}
			- " via "
			a href="#dom-imagebitmaprenderingcontext-transferfromimagebitmap" id=security-with-canvas-elements:dom-imagebitmaprenderingcontext-transferfromimagebitmap "transferFromImageBitmap()"
			- .
		}
		h5 id=premultiplied-alpha-and-the-2d-rendering-context {
			span class=secno "4.12.5.7"
			- " Premultiplied alpha and the 2D rendering context"
			a class=self-link href="#premultiplied-alpha-and-the-2d-rendering-context"
		}
		p {
			dfn id=concept-premultiplied-alpha "Premultiplied alpha"
			- " refers to one way of representing transparency in an image, the other being non-premultiplied alpha."
		}
		p "Under non-premultiplied alpha, the red, green, and blue channels of a pixel represent that pixel's color, and its alpha channel represents that pixel's opacity."
		p "Under premultiplied alpha, however, the red, green, and blue channels of a pixel represent the amounts of color that the pixel adds to the image, and its alpha channel represents the amount that the pixel obscures whatever is behind it."
		div class=example {
			p "For instance, assuming the color channels range from 0 (off) to 255 (full intensity), these example colors are represented in the following ways:"
			table {
				thead {
					tr {
						th "CSS color representation "
						th "Premultiplied representation "
						th "Non-premultiplied representation "
						th "Description of color "
						th "Image of color blended above other content "
					}
				}
				tbody {
					tr {
						td "rgba(255, 127, 0, 1) "
						td "255, 127, 0, 255 "
						td "255, 127, 0, 255 "
						td "Completely-opaque orange "
						td {
							img alt="An opaque orange circle sits atop a background" height="96" src="images/premultiplied-example-1.png" width="96"
						}
					}
					tr {
						td "rgba(255, 255, 0, 0.5) "
						td "127, 127, 0, 127 "
						td "255, 255, 0, 127 "
						td "Halfway-opaque yellow "
						td {
							img alt="A yellow circle, halfway transparent, sits atop a background" height="96" src="images/premultiplied-example-2.png" width="96"
						}
					}
					tr {
						td "Unrepresentable "
						td "255, 127, 0, 127 "
						td "Unrepresentable "
						td "Additive halfway-opaque orange "
						td {
							img alt="An orange circle somewhat brightens the background that it sits atop" height="96" src="images/premultiplied-example-3.png" width="96"
						}
					}
					tr {
						td "Unrepresentable "
						td "255, 127, 0, 0 "
						td "Unrepresentable "
						td "Additive fully-transparent orange "
						td {
							img alt="An orange circle completely brightens the background that it sits atop" height="96" src="images/premultiplied-example-4.png" width="96"
						}
					}
					tr {
						td "rgba(255, 127, 0, 0) "
						td "0, 0, 0, 0 "
						td "255, 127, 0, 0 "
						td "Fully-transparent (\"invisible\") orange "
						td {
							img alt="An empty background with nothing atop it" height="96" src="images/premultiplied-example-5.png" width="96"
						}
					}
					tr {
						td "rgba(0, 127, 255, 0) "
						td "0, 0, 0, 0 "
						td "255, 127, 0, 0 "
						td "Fully-transparent (\"invisible\") turquoise "
						td {
							img alt="An empty background with nothing atop it" height="96" src="images/premultiplied-example-5.png" width="96"
						}
					}
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			dfn id=convert-to-premultiplied "Converting a color value from a non-premultiplied representation to a premultiplied one"
			- " involves multiplying the color's red, green, and blue channels by its alpha channel (remapping the range of the alpha channel such that \"fully transparent\" is 0, and \"fully opaque\" is 1)."
		}
		p {
			dfn id=convert-from-premultiplied "Converting a color value from a premultiplied representation to a non-premultiplied one"
			- " involves the inverse: dividing the color's red, green, and blue channels by its alpha channel."
		}
		p "As certain colors can only be represented under premultiplied alpha (for instance, additive colors), and others can only be represented under non-premultiplied alpha (for instance, \"invisible\" colors which hold certain red, green, and blue values even with no opacity); and division and multiplication on 8-bit integers (which is how canvas's colors are currently stored) entails a loss of precision, converting between premultiplied and non-premultiplied alpha is a lossy operation on colors that are not fully opaque."
		p {
			- "A "
			code id=premultiplied-alpha-and-the-2d-rendering-context:canvasrenderingcontext2d {
				a href="#canvasrenderingcontext2d" CanvasRenderingContext2D
			}
			- "'s "
			a href="#output-bitmap" id=premultiplied-alpha-and-the-2d-rendering-context:output-bitmap "output bitmap"
			- " and an "
			code id=premultiplied-alpha-and-the-2d-rendering-context:offscreencanvasrenderingcontext2d {
				a href="#offscreencanvasrenderingcontext2d" OffscreenCanvasRenderingContext2D
			}
			- "'s "
			a href="#offscreencontext2d-bitmap" id=premultiplied-alpha-and-the-2d-rendering-context:offscreencontext2d-bitmap bitmap
			- " must use premultiplied alpha to represent transparent colors."
		}
		p class=note {
			- "It is important for canvas bitmaps to represent colors using premultiplied alpha because it affects the range of representable colors. While additive colors cannot currently be drawn onto canvases directly because CSS colors are non-premultiplied and cannot represent them, it is still possible to, for instance, draw additive colors onto a WebGL canvas and then draw that WebGL canvas onto a 2D canvas via "
			code id=premultiplied-alpha-and-the-2d-rendering-context:dom-context-2d-drawimage {
				a href="#dom-context-2d-drawimage" "drawImage()"
			}
			- .
		}
		h3 id=custom-elements {
			span class=secno "4.13"
			- " Custom elements"
			a class=self-link href="#custom-elements"
		}
		div class="mdn-anno wrapped" {
			button class=mdn-anno-btn onclick="toggleStatus(this)" {
				b class=all-engines-flag title="Support in all current engines." ✔
				span MDN
			}
			div class=feature {
				p {
					a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Web_Components/Using_custom_elements" title="One of the key features of the Web Components standard is the ability to create custom elements that encapsulate your functionality on an HTML page, rather than having to make do with a long, nested batch of elements that together provide a custom page feature. This article introduces the use of the Custom Elements API." Using_custom_elements
				}
				p class=all-engines-text "Support in all current engines."
				div class=support {
					span class="firefox yes" {
						span Firefox
						span "63+"
					}
					span class="safari yes" {
						span Safari
						span "10.1+"
					}
					span class="chrome yes" {
						span Chrome
						span "54+"
					}
					hr
					span class="opera unknown" {
						span Opera
						span ?
					}
					span class="edge_blink yes" {
						span Edge
						span "79+"
					}
					hr
					span class="edge unknown" {
						span "Edge (Legacy)"
						span ?
					}
					span class="ie no" {
						span "Internet Explorer"
						span No
					}
					hr
					span class="firefox_android unknown" {
						span "Firefox Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="safari_ios unknown" {
						span "Safari iOS"
						span ?
					}
					span class="chrome_android unknown" {
						span "Chrome Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="webview_android unknown" {
						span "WebView Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="samsunginternet_android unknown" {
						span "Samsung Internet"
						span ?
					}
					span class="opera_android unknown" {
						span "Opera Android"
						span ?
					}
				}
			}
		}
		h4 id=custom-elements-intro {
			span class=secno "4.13.1"
			- " Introduction"
			a class=self-link href="#custom-elements-intro"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			a href="#custom-element" id=custom-elements-intro:custom-element "Custom elements"
			- " provide a way for authors to build their own fully-featured DOM elements. Although authors could always use non-standard elements in their documents, with application-specific behavior added after the fact by scripting or similar, such elements have historically been non-conforming and not very functional. By "
			a href="#element-definition" id=custom-elements-intro:element-definition defining
			- " a custom element, authors can inform the parser how to properly construct an element and how elements of that class should react to changes."
		}
		p "Custom elements are part of a larger effort to \"rationalise the platform\", by explaining existing platform features (like the elements of HTML) in terms of lower-level author-exposed extensibility points (like custom element definition). Although today there are many limitations on the capabilities of custom elements—both functionally and semantically—that prevent them from fully explaining the behaviors of HTML's existing elements, we hope to shrink this gap over time."
		h5 id=custom-elements-autonomous-example {
			span class=secno "4.13.1.1"
			- " Creating an autonomous custom element"
			a class=self-link href="#custom-elements-autonomous-example"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "For the purposes of illustrating how to create an "
			a href="#autonomous-custom-element" id=custom-elements-autonomous-example:autonomous-custom-element "autonomous custom element"
			- ", let's define a custom element that encapsulates rendering a small icon for a country flag. Our goal is to be able to use it like so:"
		}
		pre {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" flag-icon
				c- e="" country
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"nl\""
				c- p="" "></"
				c- f="" flag-icon
				c- p="" >
			}
		}
		p {
			- "To do this, we first declare a class for the custom element, extending "
			code id=custom-elements-autonomous-example:htmlelement {
				a href="#htmlelement" HTMLElement
			}
			- :
		}
		pre {
			code class=js {
				c- a="" class
				- " FlagIcon "
				c- k="" extends
				- " HTMLElement "
				c- p="" "{"
				- " constructor"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" super
				c- p="" "();"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- "_countryCode "
				c- o="" "="
				c- kc="" "null"
				c- p="" ";"
				c- p="" "}"
				c- k="" static
				- " observedAttributes "
				c- o="" "="
				c- p="" "["
				c- u="" "\"country\""
				c- p="" "];"
				- " attributeChangedCallback"
				c- p="" "("
				- name
				c- p="" ,
				- " oldValue"
				c- p="" ,
				- " newValue"
				c- p="" ")"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- c1="" "// name will always be \"country\" due to observedAttributes"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- "_countryCode "
				c- o="" "="
				- " newValue"
				c- p="" ";"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- _updateRendering
				c- p="" "();"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " connectedCallback"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- _updateRendering
				c- p="" "();"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " get country"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" return
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- _countryCode
				c- p="" ";"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " set country"
				c- p="" "("
				- v
				c- p="" ")"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- setAttribute
				c- p="" "("
				c- u="" "\"country\""
				c- p="" ,
				- " v"
				c- p="" ");"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " _updateRendering"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- c1="" "// Left as an exercise for the reader. But, you'll probably want to"
				c- c1="" "// check this.ownerDocument.defaultView to see if we've been"
				c- c1="" "// inserted into a document with a browsing context, and avoid"
				c- c1="" "// doing any work if not."
				c- p="" "}"
				c- p="" "}"
			}
		}
		p "We then need to use this class to define the element:"
		pre {
			code class=js {
				- customElements
				c- p="" .
				- define
				c- p="" "("
				c- u="" "\"flag-icon\""
				c- p="" ,
				- " FlagIcon"
				c- p="" ");"
			}
		}
		p {
			- "At this point, our above code will work! The parser, whenever it sees the "
			code flag-icon
			- " tag, will construct a new instance of our "
			code FlagIcon
			- " class, and tell our code about its new "
			code country
			- " attribute, which we then use to set the element's internal state and update its rendering (when appropriate)."
		}
		p {
			- "You can also create "
			code flag-icon
			- " elements using the DOM API:"
		}
		pre {
			code class=js {
				c- a="" const
				- " flagIcon "
				c- o="" "="
				- " document"
				c- p="" .
				- createElement
				c- p="" "("
				c- u="" "\"flag-icon\""
				c- p="" ")"
				- " flagIcon"
				c- p="" .
				- "country "
				c- o="" "="
				c- u="" "\"jp\""
				- " document"
				c- p="" .
				- body
				c- p="" .
				- appendChild
				c- p="" "("
				- flagIcon
				c- p="" ")"
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Finally, we can also use the "
			a href="#custom-element-constructor" id=custom-elements-autonomous-example:custom-element-constructor "custom element constructor"
			- " itself. That is, the above code is equivalent to:"
		}
		pre {
			code class=js {
				c- a="" const
				- " flagIcon "
				c- o="" "="
				c- k="" new
				- " FlagIcon"
				c- p="" "()"
				- " flagIcon"
				c- p="" .
				- "country "
				c- o="" "="
				c- u="" "\"jp\""
				- " document"
				c- p="" .
				- body
				c- p="" .
				- appendChild
				c- p="" "("
				- flagIcon
				c- p="" ")"
			}
		}
		h5 id=custom-elements-face-example {
			span class=secno "4.13.1.2"
			- " Creating a form-associated custom element"
			a class=self-link href="#custom-elements-face-example"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "Adding a static "
			code formAssociated
			- " property, with a true value, makes an "
			a href="#autonomous-custom-element" id=custom-elements-face-example:autonomous-custom-element "autonomous custom element"
			- " a "
			a href="#form-associated-custom-element" id=custom-elements-face-example:form-associated-custom-element "form-associated custom element"
			- ". The "
			code id=custom-elements-face-example:elementinternals {
				a href="#elementinternals" ElementInternals
			}
			- " interface helps you to implement functions and properties common to form control elements."
		}
		pre {
			code class=js {
				c- a="" class
				- " MyCheckbox "
				c- k="" extends
				- " HTMLElement "
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" static
				- " formAssociated "
				c- o="" "="
				c- kc="" "true"
				c- p="" ";"
				c- k="" static
				- " observedAttributes "
				c- o="" "="
				c- p="" "["
				c- t="" 'checked'
				c- p="" "];"
				- " constructor"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" super
				c- p="" "();"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- "_internals "
				c- o="" "="
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- attachInternals
				c- p="" "();"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- addEventListener
				c- p="" "("
				c- t="" 'click'
				c- p="" ,
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- _onClick
				c- p="" .
				- bind
				c- p="" "("
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" "));"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " get form"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" return
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- _internals
				c- p="" .
				- form
				c- p="" ";"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " get name"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" return
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- getAttribute
				c- p="" "("
				c- t="" 'name'
				c- p="" ");"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " get type"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" return
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- localName
				c- p="" ";"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " get checked"
				c- p="" "()"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" return
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- hasAttribute
				c- p="" "("
				c- t="" 'checked'
				c- p="" ");"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " set checked"
				c- p="" "("
				- flag
				c- p="" ")"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- toggleAttribute
				c- p="" "("
				c- t="" 'checked'
				c- p="" ,
				- " Boolean"
				c- p="" "("
				- flag
				c- p="" "));"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " attributeChangedCallback"
				c- p="" "("
				- name
				c- p="" ,
				- " oldValue"
				c- p="" ,
				- " newValue"
				c- p="" ")"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- c1="" "// name will always be \"checked\" due to observedAttributes"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- _internals
				c- p="" .
				- setFormValue
				c- p="" "("
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- "checked "
				c- o="" ?
				c- t="" 'on'
				c- o="" :
				c- kc="" "null"
				c- p="" ");"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " _onClick"
				c- p="" "("
				- event
				c- p="" ")"
				c- p="" "{"
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- "checked "
				c- o="" "="
				c- o="" !
				c- k="" this
				c- p="" .
				- checked
				c- p="" ";"
				c- p="" "}"
				c- p="" "}"
				- " customElements"
				c- p="" .
				- define
				c- p="" "("
				c- t="" 'my-checkbox'
				c- p="" ,
				- " MyCheckbox"
				c- p="" ");"
			}
		}
		p {
			- "You can use the custom element "
			code my-checkbox
			- " like a built-in form-associated element. For example, putting it in "
			code id=custom-elements-face-example:the-form-element {
				a href="#the-form-element" form
			}
			- " or "
			code id=custom-elements-face-example:the-label-element {
				a href="#the-label-element" label
			}
			- " associates the "
			code my-checkbox
			- " element with them, and submitting the "
			code id=custom-elements-face-example:the-form-element-2 {
				a href="#the-form-element" form
			}
			- " will send data provided by "
			code my-checkbox
			- " implementation. "
		}
		pre {
			code class=html {
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" form
				c- e="" action
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"...\""
				c- e="" method
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"...\""
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" label
				c- p="" ><
				c- f="" my-checkbox
				c- e="" name
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"agreed\""
				c- p="" "></"
				c- f="" my-checkbox
				c- p="" >
				- " I read the agreement."
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" label
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" <
				c- f="" input
				c- e="" type
				c- o="" "="
				c- s="" "\"submit\""
				c- p="" >
				c- p="" "</"
				c- f="" form
				c- p="" >
			}
		}
		h5 id=custom-elements-accessibility-example {
			span class=secno "4.13.1.3"
			- " Creating a custom element with default accessible roles, states, and properties"
			a class=self-link href="#custom-elements-accessibility-example"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "By using the appropriate properties of "
			code id=custom-elements-accessibility-example:elementinternals {
				a href="#elementinternals" ElementInternals
			}
			- ", your custom element can have default accessibility semantics. The following code expands our form-associated checkbox from the previous section to properly set its default role and checkedness, as viewed by accessibility technology:"
		}
		pre {
			code class=js {
				c- a="" class
