		h4 id=tag-clouds {
			span class=secno "4.14.2"
			- " Tag clouds"
			a class=self-link href="#tag-clouds"
		}
		p id=tag-cloud {
			- "This specification does not define any markup specifically for marking up lists of keywords that apply to a group of pages (also known as "
			i "tag clouds"
			- "). In general, authors are encouraged to either mark up such lists using "
			code id=tag-clouds:the-ul-element {
				a href="#the-ul-element" ul
			}
			- " elements with explicit inline counts that are then hidden and turned into a presentational effect using a style sheet, or to use SVG."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Here, three tags are included in a short tag cloud:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" style
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" .
					c- nc="" tag-cloud
					c- o="" >
					c- f="" li
					c- o="" >
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" "{"
					c- k="" display
					c- p="" :
					c- kc="" none
					c- p="" ";"
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" .
					c- nc="" tag-cloud
					c- o="" >
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" "{"
					c- k="" display
					c- p="" :
					c- kc="" inline
					c- p="" ";"
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" .
					c- nc="" tag-cloud-1
					c- p="" "{"
					c- k="" font-size
					c- p="" :
					c- mf="" "0.7"
					c- b="" em
					c- p="" ";"
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" .
					c- nc="" tag-cloud-2
					c- p="" "{"
					c- k="" font-size
					c- p="" :
					c- mf="" "0.9"
					c- b="" em
					c- p="" ";"
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" .
					c- nc="" tag-cloud-3
					c- p="" "{"
					c- k="" font-size
					c- p="" :
					c- mf="" "1.1"
					c- b="" em
					c- p="" ";"
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" .
					c- nc="" tag-cloud-4
					c- p="" "{"
					c- k="" font-size
					c- p="" :
					c- mf="" "1.3"
					c- b="" em
					c- p="" ";"
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" .
					c- nc="" tag-cloud-5
					c- p="" "{"
					c- k="" font-size
					c- p="" :
					c- mf="" "1.5"
					c- b="" em
					c- p="" ";"
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" @
					c- k="" media
					c- f="" speech
					c- p="" "{"
					c- p="" .
					c- nc="" tag-cloud
					c- o="" >
					c- f="" li
					c- o="" >
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" "{"
					c- k="" display
					c- p="" :
					c- kc="" inline
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" "}"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" style
					c- p="" >
					- " ... "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" ul
					c- e="" class
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"tag-cloud\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" li
					c- e="" class
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"tag-cloud-4\""
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" title
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"28 instances\""
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/t/apple\""
					c- p="" >
					- apple
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- "(popular)"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" li
					c- e="" class
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"tag-cloud-2\""
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" title
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"6 instances\""
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/t/kiwi\""
					c- p="" >
					- kiwi
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- "(rare)"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" li
					c- e="" class
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"tag-cloud-5\""
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" title
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"41 instances\""
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/t/pear\""
					c- p="" >
					- pear
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- "(very popular)"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" ul
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p {
				- "The actual frequency of each tag is given using the "
				code id=tag-clouds:attr-title {
					a href="#attr-title" title
				}
				- " attribute. A CSS style sheet is provided to convert the markup into a cloud of differently-sized words, but for user agents that do not support CSS or are not visual, the markup contains annotations like \"(popular)\" or \"(rare)\" to categorize the various tags by frequency, thus enabling all users to benefit from the information."
			}
			p {
				- "The "
				code id=tag-clouds:the-ul-element-2 {
					a href="#the-ul-element" ul
				}
				- " element is used (rather than "
				code id=tag-clouds:the-ol-element {
					a href="#the-ol-element" ol
				}
				- ") because the order is not particularly important: while the list is in fact ordered alphabetically, it would convey the same information if ordered by, say, the length of the tag."
			}
			p {
				- "The "
				code id=tag-clouds:link-type-tag {
					a href="#link-type-tag" tag
				}
				code id=tag-clouds:attr-hyperlink-rel {
					a href="#attr-hyperlink-rel" rel
				}
				- "-keyword is "
				em not
				- " used on these "
				code id=tag-clouds:the-a-element {
					a href="#the-a-element" a
				}
				- " elements because they do not represent tags that apply to the page itself; they are just part of an index listing the tags themselves."
			}
		}
		h4 id=conversations {
			span class=secno "4.14.3"
			- " Conversations"
			a class=self-link href="#conversations"
		}
		p "This specification does not define a specific element for marking up conversations, meeting minutes, chat transcripts, dialogues in screenplays, instant message logs, and other situations where different players take turns in discourse."
		p {
			- "Instead, authors are encouraged to mark up conversations using "
			code id=conversations:the-p-element {
				a href="#the-p-element" p
			}
			- " elements and punctuation. Authors who need to mark the speaker for styling purposes are encouraged to use "
			code id=conversations:the-span-element {
				a href="#the-span-element" span
			}
			- " or "
			code id=conversations:the-b-element {
				a href="#the-b-element" b
			}
			- ". Paragraphs with their text wrapped in the "
			code id=conversations:the-i-element {
				a href="#the-i-element" i
			}
			- " element can be used for marking up stage directions."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "This example demonstrates this using an extract from Abbot and Costello's famous sketch, "
				cite "Who's on first"
				- :
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " Costello: Look, you gotta first baseman? "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " Abbott: Certainly. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " Costello: Who's playing first? "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " Abbott: That's right. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " Costello becomes exasperated. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " Costello: When you pay off the first baseman every month, who gets the money? "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " Abbott: Every dollar of it."
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "The following extract shows how an IM conversation log could be marked up, using the "
				code id=conversations:the-data-element {
					a href="#the-data-element" data
				}
				- " element to provide Unix timestamps for each line. Note that the timestamps are provided in a format that the "
				code id=conversations:the-time-element {
					a href="#the-time-element" time
				}
				- " element does not support, so the "
				code id=conversations:the-data-element-2 {
					a href="#the-data-element" data
				}
				- " element is used instead (namely, Unix "
				code time_t
				- " timestamps). Had the author wished to mark up the data using one of the date and time formats supported by the "
				code id=conversations:the-time-element-2 {
					a href="#the-time-element" time
				}
				- " element, that element could have been used instead of "
				code id=conversations:the-data-element-3 {
					a href="#the-data-element" data
				}
				- ". This could be advantageous as it would allow data analysis tools to detect the timestamps unambiguously, without coordination with the page author."
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" data
					c- e="" value
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"1319898155\""
					c- p="" >
					- "14:22"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" data
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- egof
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- " I'm not that nerdy, I've only seen 30% of the star trek episodes "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" data
					c- e="" value
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"1319898192\""
					c- p="" >
					- "14:23"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" data
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- kaj
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- " if you know what percentage of the star trek episodes you have seen, you are inarguably nerdy "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" data
					c- e="" value
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"1319898200\""
					c- p="" >
					- "14:23"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" data
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- egof
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- " it's unarguably "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" data
					c- e="" value
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"1319898228\""
					c- p="" >
					- "14:23"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" data
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" i
					c- p="" >
					- "* kaj blinks"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" i
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" data
					c- e="" value
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"1319898260\""
					c- p="" >
					- "14:24"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" data
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- kaj
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" b
					c- p="" >
					- " you are not helping your case"
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "HTML does not have a good way to mark up graphs, so descriptions of interactive conversations from games are more difficult to mark up. This example shows one possible convention using "
				code id=conversations:the-dl-element {
					a href="#the-dl-element" dl
				}
				- " elements to list the possible responses at each point in the conversation. Another option to consider is describing the conversation in the form of a DOT file, and outputting the result as an SVG image to place in the document. "
				a href="#refsDOT" "[DOT]"
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " Next, you meet a fisher. You can say one of several greetings: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Hello there!\" "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " She responds with \"Hello, how may I help you?\"; you can respond with: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"I would like to buy a fish.\" "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " She sells you a fish and the conversation finishes. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Can I borrow your boat?\" "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " She is surprised and asks \"What are you offering in return?\". "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Five gold.\" (if you have enough) "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Ten gold.\" (if you have enough) "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Fifteen gold.\" (if you have enough) "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " She lends you her boat. The conversation ends. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"A fish.\" (if you have one) "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"A newspaper.\" (if you have one) "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"A pebble.\" (if you have one) "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " \"No thanks\", she replies. Your conversation options at this point are the same as they were after asking to borrow her boat, minus any options you've suggested before. "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Vote for me in the next election!\" "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " She turns away. The conversation finishes. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Madam, are you aware that your fish are running away?\" "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " She looks at you skeptically and says \"Fish cannot run, miss\". "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"You got me!\" "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " The fisher sighs and the conversation ends. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Only kidding.\" "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Good one!\" she retorts. Your conversation options at this point are the same as those following \"Hello there!\" above. "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- " \"Oh, then what are they doing?\" "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- " She looks at her fish, giving you an opportunity to steal her boat, which you do. The conversation ends. "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In some games, conversations are simpler: each character merely has a fixed set of lines that they say. In this example, a game FAQ/walkthrough lists some of the known possible responses for each character:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" section
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					- Dialogue
					c- p="" "</"
