		h2 id=microdata {
			span class=secno "5"
			dfn Microdata
			a class=self-link href="#microdata"
		}
		h3 id=introduction-7 {
			span class=secno "5.1"
			- " Introduction"
			a class=self-link href="#introduction-7"
		}
		h4 id=overview {
			span class=secno "5.1.1"
			- " Overview"
			a class=self-link href="#overview"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p "Sometimes, it is desirable to annotate content with specific machine-readable labels, e.g. to allow generic scripts to provide services that are customized to the page, or to enable content from a variety of cooperating authors to be processed by a single script in a consistent manner."
		p "For this purpose, authors can use the microdata features described in this section. Microdata allows nested groups of name-value pairs to be added to documents, in parallel with the existing content."
		h4 id=the-basic-syntax {
			span class=secno "5.1.2"
			- " The basic syntax"
			a class=self-link href="#the-basic-syntax"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "At a high level, microdata consists of a group of name-value pairs. The groups are called "
			a href="#concept-item" id=the-basic-syntax:concept-item items
			- ", and each name-value pair is a property. Items and properties are represented by regular elements."
		}
		p {
			- "To create an item, the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-itemscope {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute is used."
		}
		p {
			- "To add a property to an item, the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:names:-the-itemprop-attribute {
				a href="#names:-the-itemprop-attribute" itemprop
			}
			- " attribute is used on one of the "
			a href="#concept-item" id=the-basic-syntax:concept-item-2 item's
			- " descendants."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Here there are two items, each of which has the property \"name\":"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "My name is "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- Elizabeth
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- .
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "My name is "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- Daniel
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- .
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p "Markup without the microdata-related attributes does not have any effect on the microdata model."
		div class=example {
			p "These two examples are exactly equivalent, at a microdata level, as the previous two examples respectively:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "My "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- name
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" em
					c- p="" >
					- " is "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- E
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- liz
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" strong
					c- p="" >
					- abeth
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- .
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" section
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" aside
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "My name is "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" a
					c- e="" href
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"/?user=daniel\""
					c- p="" >
					- Daniel
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" a
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- .
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" aside
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" section
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p "Properties generally have values that are strings."
		div class=example {
			p "Here the item has three properties:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "My name is "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- Neil
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- .
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "My band is called "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"band\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Four Parts Water"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- .
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "I am "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"nationality\""
					c- p="" >
					- British
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- .
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "When a string value is a "
			a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=the-basic-syntax:url URL
			- ", it is expressed using the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:the-a-element {
				a href="#the-a-element" a
			}
			- " element and its "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-hyperlink-href {
				a href="#attr-hyperlink-href" href
			}
			- " attribute, the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:the-img-element {
				a href="#the-img-element" img
			}
			- " element and its "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-img-src {
				a href="#attr-img-src" src
			}
			- " attribute, or other elements that link to or embed external resources."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In this example, the item has one property, \"image\", whose value is a URL:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"image\""
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"google-logo.png\""
					c- e="" alt
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"Google\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "When a string value is in some machine-readable format unsuitable for human consumption, it is expressed using the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-data-value {
				a href="#attr-data-value" value
			}
			- " attribute of the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:the-data-element {
				a href="#the-data-element" data
			}
			- " element, with the human-readable version given in the element's contents."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Here, there is an item with a property whose value is a product ID. The ID is not human-friendly, so the product's name is used the human-visible text instead of the ID."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" data
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"product-id\""
					c- e="" value
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"9678AOU879\""
					c- p="" >
					- "The Instigator 2000"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" data
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "For numeric data, the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:the-meter-element {
				a href="#the-meter-element" meter
			}
			- " element and its "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-meter-value {
				a href="#attr-meter-value" value
			}
			- " attribute can be used instead."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "Here a rating is given using a "
				code id=the-basic-syntax:the-meter-element-2 {
					a href="#the-meter-element" meter
				}
				- " element."
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- e="" itemtype
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"http://schema.org/Product\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Panasonic White 60L Refrigerator"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"panasonic-fridge-60l-white.jpg\""
					c- e="" alt
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"aggregateRating\""
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- e="" itemtype
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"http://schema.org/AggregateRating\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" meter
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"ratingValue\""
					c- e="" min
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "0"
					c- e="" value
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "3.5"
					c- e="" max
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "5"
					c- p="" >
					- "Rated 3.5/5"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" meter
					c- p="" >
					- " (based on "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"reviewCount\""
					c- p="" >
					- "11"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- " customer reviews) "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Similarly, for date- and time-related data, the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:the-time-element {
				a href="#the-time-element" time
			}
			- " element and its "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-time-datetime {
				a href="#attr-time-datetime" datetime
			}
			- " attribute can be used instead."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In this example, the item has one property, \"birthday\", whose value is a date:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					- " I was born on "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" time
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"birthday\""
					c- e="" datetime
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"2009-05-10\""
					c- p="" >
					- "May 10th 2009"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" time
					c- p="" >
					- ". "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Properties can also themselves be groups of name-value pairs, by putting the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-itemscope-2 {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute on the element that declares the property."
		}
		p {
			- "Items that are not part of others are called "
			a href="#top-level-microdata-items" id=the-basic-syntax:top-level-microdata-items "top-level microdata items"
			- .
		}
		div class=example {
			p "In this example, the outer item represents a person, and the inner one represents a band:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Name: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- Amanda
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Band: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"band\""
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Jazz Band"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- " ("
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"size\""
					c- p="" >
					- "12"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- " players)"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "The outer item here has two properties, \"name\" and \"band\". The \"name\" is \"Amanda\", and the \"band\" is an item in its own right, with two properties, \"name\" and \"size\". The \"name\" of the band is \"Jazz Band\", and the \"size\" is \"12\"."
			p "The outer item in this example is a top-level microdata item."
		}
		p {
			- "Properties that are not descendants of the element with the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-itemscope-3 {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute can be associated with the "
			a href="#concept-item" id=the-basic-syntax:concept-item-3 item
			- " using the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-itemref {
				a href="#attr-itemref" itemref
			}
			- " attribute. This attribute takes a list of IDs of elements to crawl in addition to crawling the children of the element with the "
			code id=the-basic-syntax:attr-itemscope-4 {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "This example is the same as the previous one, but all the properties are separated from their "
				a href="#concept-item" id=the-basic-syntax:concept-item-4 items
				- :
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- e="" id
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"amanda\""
					c- e="" itemref
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"a b\""
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- e="" id
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"a\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Name: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- Amanda
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" id
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"b\""
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"band\""
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- e="" itemref
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"c\""
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" id
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"c\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Band: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Jazz Band"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Size: "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"size\""
					c- p="" >
					- "12"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- " players"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "This gives the same result as the previous example. The first item has two properties, \"name\", set to \"Amanda\", and \"band\", set to another item. That second item has two further properties, \"name\", set to \"Jazz Band\", and \"size\", set to \"12\"."
		}
		p {
			- "An "
			a href="#concept-item" id=the-basic-syntax:concept-item-5 item
			- " can have multiple properties with the same name and different values."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "This example describes an ice cream, with two flavors:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					- "Flavors in my favorite ice cream:"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" ul
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" li
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"flavor\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Lemon sorbet"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" li
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"flavor\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Apricot sorbet"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" li
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" ul
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "This thus results in an item with two properties, both \"flavor\", having the values \"Lemon sorbet\" and \"Apricot sorbet\"."
		}
		p "An element introducing a property can also introduce multiple properties at once, to avoid duplication when some of the properties have the same value."
		div class=example {
			p "Here we see an item with two properties, \"favorite-color\" and \"favorite-fruit\", both set to the value \"orange\":"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" div
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"favorite-color favorite-fruit\""
					c- p="" >
					- orange
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" div
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
		}
		p "It's important to note that there is no relationship between the microdata and the content of the document where the microdata is marked up."
		div class=example {
			p "There is no semantic difference, for instance, between the following two examples:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" figure
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"castle.jpeg\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" figcaption
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- "The Castle"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- " (1986)"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" figcaption
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" figure
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" meta
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- e="" content
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"The Castle\""
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" figure
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"castle.jpeg\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" figcaption
					c- p="" >
					- "The Castle (1986)"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" figcaption
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" figure
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "Both have a figure with a caption, and both, completely unrelated to the figure, have an item with a name-value pair with the name \"name\" and the value \"The Castle\". The only difference is that if the user drags the caption out of the document, in the former case, the item will be included in the drag-and-drop data. In neither case is the image in any way associated with the item."
		}
		h4 id=typed-items {
			span class=secno "5.1.3"
			- " Typed items"
			a class=self-link href="#typed-items"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p "The examples in the previous section show how information could be marked up on a page that doesn't expect its microdata to be re-used. Microdata is most useful, though, when it is used in contexts where other authors and readers are able to cooperate to make new uses of the markup."
		p {
			- "For this purpose, it is necessary to give each "
			a href="#concept-item" id=typed-items:concept-item item
			- " a type, such as \"https://example.com/person\", or \"https://example.org/cat\", or \"https://band.example.net/\". Types are identified as "
			a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=typed-items:url URLs
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "The type for an "
			a href="#concept-item" id=typed-items:concept-item-2 item
			- " is given as the value of an "
			code id=typed-items:attr-itemtype {
				a href="#attr-itemtype" itemtype
			}
			- " attribute on the same element as the "
			code id=typed-items:attr-itemscope {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Here, the item's type is \"https://example.org/animals#cat\":"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" section
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- e="" itemtype
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"https://example.org/animals#cat\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name\""
					c- p="" >
					- Hedral
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"desc\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Hedral is a male american domestic shorthair, with a fluffy black fur with white paws and belly."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"img\""
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"hedral.jpeg\""
					c- e="" alt
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"\""
					c- e="" title
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"Hedral, age 18 months\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" section
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "In this example the \"https://example.org/animals#cat\" item has three properties, a \"name\" (\"Hedral\"), a \"desc\" (\"Hedral is...\"), and an \"img\" (\"hedral.jpeg\")."
		}
		p {
			- "The type gives the context for the properties, thus selecting a vocabulary: a property named \"class\" given for an item with the type \"https://census.example/person\" might refer to the economic class of an individual, while a property named \"class\" given for an item with the type \"https://example.com/school/teacher\" might refer to the classroom a teacher has been assigned. Several types can share a vocabulary. For example, the types \""
			code "https://example.org/people/teacher"
			- "\" and \""
			code "https://example.org/people/engineer"
			- "\" could be defined to use the same vocabulary (though maybe some properties would not be especially useful in both cases, e.g. maybe the \""
			code "https://example.org/people/engineer"
			- "\" type might not typically be used with the \""
			code classroom
			- "\" property). Multiple types defined to use the same vocabulary can be given for a single item by listing the URLs as a space-separated list in the attribute' value. An item cannot be given two types if they do not use the same vocabulary, however."
		}
		h4 id=global-identifiers-for-items {
			span class=secno "5.1.4"
			- " Global identifiers for items"
			a class=self-link href="#global-identifiers-for-items"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "Sometimes, an "
			a href="#concept-item" id=global-identifiers-for-items:concept-item item
			- " gives information about a topic that has a global identifier. For example, books can be identified by their ISBN number."
		}
		p {
			- "Vocabularies (as identified by the "
			code id=global-identifiers-for-items:attr-itemtype {
				a href="#attr-itemtype" itemtype
			}
			- " attribute) can be designed such that "
			a href="#concept-item" id=global-identifiers-for-items:concept-item-2 items
			- " get associated with their global identifier in an unambiguous way by expressing the global identifiers as "
			a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=global-identifiers-for-items:url URLs
			- " given in an "
			code id=global-identifiers-for-items:attr-itemid {
				a href="#attr-itemid" itemid
			}
			- " attribute."
		}
		p {
			- "The exact meaning of the "
			a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=global-identifiers-for-items:url-2 URLs
			- " given in "
			code id=global-identifiers-for-items:attr-itemid-2 {
				a href="#attr-itemid" itemid
			}
			- " attributes depends on the vocabulary used."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Here, an item is talking about a particular book:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dl
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- e="" itemtype
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"https://vocab.example.net/book\""
					strong {
						c- e="" itemid
						c- o="" "="
						c- s="" "\"urn:isbn:0-330-34032-8\""
					}
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- "Title "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"title\""
					c- p="" >
					- "The Reality Dysfunction "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- "Author "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"author\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Peter F. Hamilton "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dt
					c- p="" >
					- "Publication date "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" dd
					c- p="" ><
					c- f="" time
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"pubdate\""
					c- e="" datetime
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"1996-01-26\""
					c- p="" >
					- "26 January 1996"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" time
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" dl
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p {
				- "The \""
				code "https://vocab.example.net/book"
				- "\" vocabulary in this example would define that the "
				code id=global-identifiers-for-items:attr-itemid-3 {
					a href="#attr-itemid" itemid
				}
				- " attribute takes a "
				code id=global-identifiers-for-items:urn-protocol {
					a data-x-internal=urn-protocol href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2141#section-2" urn:
				}
				a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=global-identifiers-for-items:url-3 URL
				- " pointing to the ISBN of the book."
			}
		}
		h4 id=selecting-names-when-defining-vocabularies {
			span class=secno "5.1.5"
			- " Selecting names when defining vocabularies"
			a class=self-link href="#selecting-names-when-defining-vocabularies"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p "Using microdata means using a vocabulary. For some purposes, an ad-hoc vocabulary is adequate. For others, a vocabulary will need to be designed. Where possible, authors are encouraged to re-use existing vocabularies, as this makes content re-use easier."
		p {
			- "When designing new vocabularies, identifiers can be created either using "
			a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=selecting-names-when-defining-vocabularies:url URLs
			- ", or, for properties, as plain words (with no dots or colons). For URLs, conflicts with other vocabularies can be avoided by only using identifiers that correspond to pages that the author has control over."
		}
		div class=example {
			p {
				- "For instance, if Jon and Adam both write content at "
				code example.com
				- ", at "
				code "https://example.com/~jon/..."
				- " and "
				code "https://example.com/~adam/..."
				- " respectively, then they could select identifiers of the form \"https://example.com/~jon/name\" and \"https://example.com/~adam/name\" respectively."
			}
		}
		p "Properties whose names are just plain words can only be used within the context of the types for which they are intended; properties named using URLs can be reused in items of any type. If an item has no type, and is not part of another item, then if its properties have names that are just plain words, they are not intended to be globally unique, and are instead only intended for limited use. Generally speaking, authors are encouraged to use either properties with globally unique names (URLs) or ensure that their items are typed."
		div class=example {
			p "Here, an item is an \"https://example.org/animals#cat\", and most of the properties have names that are words defined in the context of that type. There are also a few additional properties whose names come from other vocabularies."
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" section
					c- e="" itemscope
					c- e="" itemtype
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"https://example.org/animals#cat\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" h1
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"name https://example.com/fn\""
					c- p="" >
					- Hedral
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" h1
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" p
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"desc\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Hedral is a male American domestic shorthair, with a fluffy "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"https://example.com/color\""
					c- p="" >
					- black
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- " fur with "
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" span
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"https://example.com/color\""
					c- p="" >
					- white
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" span
					c- p="" >
					- " paws and belly."
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" p
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" img
					c- e="" itemprop
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"img\""
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"hedral.jpeg\""
					c- e="" alt
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"\""
					c- e="" title
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"Hedral, age 18 months\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" section
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "This example has one item with the type \"https://example.org/animals#cat\" and the following properties:"
			table {
				thead {
					tr {
						td "Property "
						td "Value "
					}
				}
				tbody {
					tr {
						td "name "
						td "Hedral "
					}
					tr {
						td "https://example.com/fn "
						td "Hedral "
					}
					tr {
						td "desc "
						td "Hedral is a male American domestic shorthair, with a fluffy black fur with white paws and belly. "
					}
					tr {
						td "https://example.com/color "
						td "black "
					}
					tr {
						td "https://example.com/color "
						td "white "
					}
					tr {
						td "img "
						td ".../hedral.jpeg "
					}
				}
			}
		}
		h3 id=encoding-microdata {
			span class=secno "5.2"
			- " Encoding microdata"
			a class=self-link href="#encoding-microdata"
		}
		h4 id=the-microdata-model {
			span class=secno "5.2.1"
			- " The microdata model"
			a class=self-link href="#the-microdata-model"
		}
		p {
			- "The microdata model consists of groups of name-value pairs known as "
			a href="#concept-item" id=the-microdata-model:concept-item items
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "Each group is known as an "
			a href="#concept-item" id=the-microdata-model:concept-item-2 item
			- ". Each "
			a href="#concept-item" id=the-microdata-model:concept-item-3 item
			- " can have "
			a href="#item-types" id=the-microdata-model:item-types "item types"
			- ", a "
			a href="#global-identifier" id=the-microdata-model:global-identifier "global identifier"
			- " (if the vocabulary specified by the "
			a href="#item-types" id=the-microdata-model:item-types-2 "item types"
			a href="#support-global-identifiers-for-items" id=the-microdata-model:support-global-identifiers-for-items "support global identifiers for items"
			- "), and a list of name-value pairs. Each name in the name-value pair is known as a "
			a href="#the-properties-of-an-item" id=the-microdata-model:the-properties-of-an-item property
			- ", and each "
			a href="#the-properties-of-an-item" id=the-microdata-model:the-properties-of-an-item-2 property
			- " has one or more "
			a href="#concept-property-value" id=the-microdata-model:concept-property-value values
			- ". Each "
			a href="#concept-property-value" id=the-microdata-model:concept-property-value-2 value
			- " is either a string or itself a group of name-value pairs (an "
			a href="#concept-item" id=the-microdata-model:concept-item-4 item
			- "). The names are unordered relative to each other, but if a particular name has multiple values, they do have a relative order."
		}
		h4 id=items {
			span class=secno "5.2.2"
			- " Items"
			a class=self-link href="#items"
		}
		div class="mdn-anno wrapped before" {
			button class=mdn-anno-btn onclick="toggleStatus(this)" {
				b class=all-engines-flag title="Support in all current engines." ✔
				span MDN
			}
			div class=feature {
				p {
					a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Global_attributes/itemscope" title="itemscope is a boolean global attribute that defines the scope of associated metadata. Specifying the itemscope attribute for an element creates a new item, which results in a number of name-value pairs that are associated with the element." "Global_attributes/itemscope"
				}
				p class=all-engines-text "Support in all current engines."
				div class=support {
					span class="firefox yes" {
						span Firefox
						span Yes
					}
					span class="safari yes" {
						span Safari
						span Yes
					}
					span class="chrome yes" {
						span Chrome
						span Yes
					}
					hr
					span class="opera unknown" {
						span Opera
						span ?
					}
					span class="edge_blink yes" {
						span Edge
						span Yes
					}
					hr
					span class="edge yes" {
						span "Edge (Legacy)"
						span "12+"
					}
					span class="ie yes" {
						span "Internet Explorer"
						span Yes
					}
					hr
					span class="firefox_android unknown" {
						span "Firefox Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="safari_ios unknown" {
						span "Safari iOS"
						span ?
					}
					span class="chrome_android unknown" {
						span "Chrome Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="webview_android unknown" {
						span "WebView Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="samsunginternet_android unknown" {
						span "Samsung Internet"
						span ?
					}
					span class="opera_android unknown" {
						span "Opera Android"
						span ?
					}
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Every "
			a href="#html-elements" id=items:html-elements "HTML element"
			- " may have an "
			dfn data-dfn-for=html-global data-dfn-type=element-attr id=attr-itemscope {
				code itemscope
			}
			- " attribute specified. The "
			code id=items:attr-itemscope {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute is a "
			a href="#boolean-attribute" id=items:boolean-attribute "boolean attribute"
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "An element with the "
			code id=items:attr-itemscope-2 {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute specified creates a new "
			dfn id=concept-item item
			- ", a group of name-value pairs."
		}
		hr
		div class="mdn-anno wrapped before" {
			button class=mdn-anno-btn onclick="toggleStatus(this)" {
				b class=all-engines-flag title="Support in all current engines." ✔
				span MDN
			}
			div class=feature {
				p {
					a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Global_attributes/itemtype" title="The global attribute itemtype specifies the URL of the vocabulary that will be used to define itemprop's (item properties) in the data structure." "Global_attributes/itemtype"
				}
				p class=all-engines-text "Support in all current engines."
				div class=support {
					span class="firefox yes" {
						span Firefox
						span Yes
					}
					span class="safari yes" {
						span Safari
						span Yes
					}
					span class="chrome yes" {
						span Chrome
						span Yes
					}
					hr
					span class="opera unknown" {
						span Opera
						span ?
					}
					span class="edge_blink yes" {
						span Edge
						span Yes
					}
					hr
					span class="edge yes" {
						span "Edge (Legacy)"
						span "12+"
					}
					span class="ie yes" {
						span "Internet Explorer"
						span Yes
					}
					hr
					span class="firefox_android unknown" {
						span "Firefox Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="safari_ios unknown" {
						span "Safari iOS"
						span ?
					}
					span class="chrome_android unknown" {
						span "Chrome Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="webview_android unknown" {
						span "WebView Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="samsunginternet_android unknown" {
						span "Samsung Internet"
						span ?
					}
					span class="opera_android unknown" {
						span "Opera Android"
						span ?
					}
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Elements with an "
			code id=items:attr-itemscope-3 {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute may have an "
			dfn data-dfn-for=html-global data-dfn-type=element-attr id=attr-itemtype {
				code itemtype
			}
			- " attribute specified, to give the "
			a href="#item-types" id=items:item-types "item types"
			- " of the "
			a href="#concept-item" id=items:concept-item item
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			code id=items:attr-itemtype {
				a href="#attr-itemtype" itemtype
			}
			- " attribute, if specified, must have a value that is an "
			a href="#unordered-set-of-unique-space-separated-tokens" id=items:unordered-set-of-unique-space-separated-tokens "unordered set of unique space-separated tokens"
			- ", none of which are "
			a data-x-internal=identical-to href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string-is" id=items:identical-to "identical to"
			- " another token and each of which is a "
			a data-x-internal=valid-url-string href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#valid-url-string" id=items:valid-url-string "valid URL string"
			- " that is an "
			a data-x-internal=absolute-url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#syntax-url-absolute" id=items:absolute-url "absolute URL"
			- ", and all of which are defined to use the same vocabulary. The attribute's value must have at least one token."
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			dfn id=item-types "item types"
			- " of an "
			a href="#concept-item" id=items:concept-item-2 item
			- " are the tokens obtained by "
			a data-x-internal=split-a-string-on-spaces href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#split-on-ascii-whitespace" id=items:split-a-string-on-spaces {
				- "splitting the element's "
				code itemtype
				- " attribute's value on ASCII whitespace"
			}
			- ". If the "
			code id=items:attr-itemtype-2 {
				a href="#attr-itemtype" itemtype
			}
			- " attribute is missing or parsing it in this way finds no tokens, the "
			a href="#concept-item" id=items:concept-item-3 item
			- " is said to have no "
			a href="#item-types" id=items:item-types-2 "item types"
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			a href="#item-types" id=items:item-types-3 "item types"
			- " must all be types defined in "
			a href="#other-applicable-specifications" id=items:other-applicable-specifications "applicable specifications"
			- " and must all be defined to use the same vocabulary."
		}
		p {
			- "Except if otherwise specified by that specification, the "
			a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=items:url URLs
			- " given as the "
			a href="#item-types" id=items:item-types-4 "item types"
			- " should not be automatically dereferenced."
		}
		p class=note {
			- "A specification could define that its "
			a href="#item-types" id=items:item-types-5 "item type"
			- " can be dereferenced to provide the user with help information, for example. In fact, vocabulary authors are encouraged to provide useful information at the given "
			a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=items:url-2 URL
			- .
		}
		p {
			a href="#item-types" id=items:item-types-6 "Item types"
			- " are opaque identifiers, and user agents must not dereference unknown "
			a href="#item-types" id=items:item-types-7 "item types"
			- ", or otherwise deconstruct them, in order to determine how to process "
			a href="#concept-item" id=items:concept-item-4 items
			- " that use them."
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			code id=items:attr-itemtype-3 {
				a href="#attr-itemtype" itemtype
			}
			- " attribute must not be specified on elements that do not have an "
			code id=items:attr-itemscope-4 {
				a href="#attr-itemscope" itemscope
			}
			- " attribute specified."
		}
		hr
		p {
			- "An "
			a href="#concept-item" id=items:concept-item-5 item
			- " is said to be a "
			dfn id=typed-item "typed item"
			- " when either it has an "
			a href="#item-types" id=items:item-types-8 "item type"
			- ", or it is the "
			a href="#concept-property-value" id=items:concept-property-value value
			- " of a "
			a href="#the-properties-of-an-item" id=items:the-properties-of-an-item property
			- " of a "
			a href="#typed-item" id=items:typed-item "typed item"
			- ". The "
			dfn id=relevant-types "relevant types"
			- " for a "
			a href="#typed-item" id=items:typed-item-2 "typed item"
			- " is the "
			a href="#concept-item" id=items:concept-item-6 item
			- "'s "
