		h3 id=find-in-page {
			span class=secno "6.9"
			- " Find-in-page"
			a class=self-link href="#find-in-page"
		}
		h4 id=introduction-10 {
			span class=secno "6.9.1"
			- " Introduction"
			a class=self-link href="#introduction-10"
		}
		p {
			- "This section defines "
			dfn id=find-in-page-2 find-in-page
			- " — a common user-agent mechanism which allows users to search through the contents of the page for particular information."
		}
		p {
			- "Access to the "
			a href="#find-in-page-2" id=introduction-10:find-in-page-2 find-in-page
			- " feature is provided via a "
			dfn id=find-in-page-interface "find-in-page interface"
			- ". This is a user-agent provided user interface, which allows the user to specify input and the parameters of the search. This interface can appear as a result of a shortcut or a menu selection."
		}
		p {
			- "A combination of text input and settings in the "
			a href="#find-in-page-interface" id=introduction-10:find-in-page-interface "find-in-page interface"
			- " represents the user "
			dfn id=fip-query query
			- ". This typically includes the text that the user wants to search for, as well as optional settings (e.g., the ability to restrict the search to whole words only)."
		}
		p {
			- "The user-agent processes page contents for a given "
			a href="#fip-query" id=introduction-10:fip-query query
			- ", and identifies zero or more "
			dfn id=fip-matches matches
			- ", which are content ranges that satisfy the user "
			a href="#fip-query" id=introduction-10:fip-query-2 query
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "One of the "
			a href="#fip-matches" id=introduction-10:fip-matches matches
			- " is identified to the user as the "
			dfn id=fip-active-match "active match"
			- ". It is highlighted and scrolled into view. The user can navigate through the "
			a href="#fip-matches" id=introduction-10:fip-matches-2 matches
			- " by advancing the "
			a href="#fip-active-match" id=introduction-10:fip-active-match "active match"
			- " using the "
			a href="#find-in-page-interface" id=introduction-10:find-in-page-interface-2 "find-in-page interface"
			- .
		}
		p class=XXX {
			a href="https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/3539" "Issue #3539"
			- " tracks standardizing how "
			a href="#find-in-page-2" id=introduction-10:find-in-page-2-2 find-in-page
			- " underlies the currently-unspecified "
			code "window.find()"
			- " API."
		}
		h4 id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found" {
			span class=secno "6.9.2"
			- " Interaction with "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:the-details-element" {
				a href="#the-details-element" details
			}
			- " and "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:attr-hidden-until-found-state" {
				a href="#attr-hidden-until-found-state" "hidden=until-found"
			}
			a class=self-link href="#interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found"
		}
		p {
			- "When find-in-page begins searching for matches, all "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:the-details-element-2" {
				a href="#the-details-element" details
			}
			- " elements in the page which do not have their "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:attr-details-open" {
				a href="#attr-details-open" open
			}
			- " attribute set should have the "
			a data-x-internal=skips-its-contents href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-contain/#skips-its-contents" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:skips-its-contents" "skipped contents"
			- " of their second slot become accessible, without modifying the "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:attr-details-open-2" {
				a href="#attr-details-open" open
			}
			- " attribute, in order to make find-in-page able to search through it. Similarly, all HTML elements with the "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:attr-hidden" {
				a href="#attr-hidden" hidden
			}
			- " attribute in the "
			a href="#attr-hidden-until-found-state" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:attr-hidden-until-found-state-2" "hidden until found"
			- " state should have their "
			a data-x-internal=skips-its-contents href="https://drafts.csswg.org/css-contain/#skips-its-contents" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:skips-its-contents-2" "skipped contents"
			- " become accessible without modifying the "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:attr-hidden-2" {
				a href="#attr-hidden" hidden
			}
			- " attribute in order to make find-in-page able to search through them. After find-in-page finishes searching for matches, the "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:the-details-element-3" {
				a href="#the-details-element" details
			}
			- " elements and the elements with the "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:attr-hidden-3" {
				a href="#attr-hidden" hidden
			}
			- " attribute in the "
			a href="#attr-hidden-until-found-state" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:attr-hidden-until-found-state-3" "hidden until found"
			- " state should have their contents become skipped again. This entire process must happen synchronously (and so is not observable to users or to author code). "
			a href="#refsCSSCONTAIN" "[CSSCONTAIN]"
		}
		p {
			- "When find-in-page chooses a new "
			a href="#fip-active-match" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:fip-active-match" "active match"
			- ", perform the following steps:"
		}
		ol {
			li {
				p {
					- "Let "
					var node
					- " be the first node in the "
					a href="#fip-active-match" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:fip-active-match-2" "active match"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					a href="#queue-a-global-task" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:queue-a-global-task" "Queue a global task"
					- " on the "
					a href="#user-interaction-task-source" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:user-interaction-task-source" "user interaction task source"
					- " given "
					var node
					- "'s "
					a href="#concept-relevant-global" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:concept-relevant-global" "relevant global object"
					- " to run the following steps:"
				}
				ol {
					li {
						p {
							- "Run the "
							a href="#ancestor-details-revealing-algorithm" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:ancestor-details-revealing-algorithm" "ancestor details revealing algorithm"
							- " on "
							var node
							- .
						}
					}
					li {
						p {
							- "Run the "
							a href="#ancestor-hidden-until-found-revealing-algorithm" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:ancestor-hidden-until-found-revealing-algorithm" "ancestor hidden-until-found revealing algorithm"
							- " on "
							var node
							- .
						}
					}
				}
			}
		}
		p class=warning {
			a class=tracking-vector data-x-internal=tracking-vector href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#tracking-vector" id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:tracking-vector" title="There is a tracking vector here." {
				img alt="(This is a tracking vector.)" crossorigin="" height="64" src="https://resources.whatwg.org/tracking-vector.svg" width="46"
			}
			- " When find-in-page auto-expands a "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:the-details-element-4" {
				a href="#the-details-element" details
			}
			- " element like this, it will fire a "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:event-toggle" {
				a href="#event-toggle" toggle
			}
			- " event. As with the separate "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:event-scroll" {
				a data-x-internal=event-scroll href="https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/#eventdef-document-scroll" scroll
			}
			- " event that find-in-page fires, this event could be used by the page to discover what the user is typing into the find-in-page dialog. If the page creates a tiny scrollable area with the current search term and every possible next character the user could type separated by a gap, and observes which one the browser scrolls to, it can add that character to the search term and update the scrollable area to incrementally build the search term. By wrapping each possible next match in a closed "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:the-details-element-5" {
				a href="#the-details-element" details
			}
			- " element, the page could listen to "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:event-toggle-2" {
				a href="#event-toggle" toggle
			}
			- " events instead of "
			code id="interaction-with-details-and-hidden=until-found:event-scroll-2" {
				a data-x-internal=event-scroll href="https://drafts.csswg.org/cssom-view/#eventdef-document-scroll" scroll
			}
			- " events. This attack could be addressed for both events by not acting on every character the user types into the find-in-page dialog."
		}
		h4 id=interaction-with-selection {
			span class=secno "6.9.3"
			- " Interaction with selection"
			a class=self-link href="#interaction-with-selection"
		}
		p {
			- "The find-in-page process is invoked in the context of a document, and may have an effect on the "
			a data-x-internal=document-selection href="https://w3c.github.io/selection-api/#dfn-selection" id=interaction-with-selection:document-selection selection
			- " of that document. Specifically, the range that defines the "
			a href="#fip-active-match" id=interaction-with-selection:fip-active-match "active match"
			- " can dictate the current selection. These selection updates, however, can happen at different times during the find-in-page process (e.g. upon the "
			a href="#find-in-page-interface" id=interaction-with-selection:find-in-page-interface "find-in-page interface"
			- " dismissal or upon a change in the "
			a href="#fip-active-match" id=interaction-with-selection:fip-active-match-2 "active match"
			- " range). "
		}
		h3 id=close-requests-and-close-watchers {
			span class=secno "6.10"
			- " Close requests and close watchers"
			a class=self-link href="#close-requests-and-close-watchers"
		}
		h4 id=close-requests {
			span class=secno "6.10.1"
			- " Close requests"
			a class=self-link href="#close-requests"
		}
		p {
			- "In an "
			a data-x-internal=implementation-defined href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined" id=close-requests:implementation-defined implementation-defined
			- " (and likely device-specific) manner, a user can send a "
			dfn data-export="" id=close-request "close request"
			- " to the user agent. This indicates that the user wishes to close something that is currently being shown on the screen, such as a popover, menu, dialog, picker, or display mode."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "Some example close requests are:"
			ul {
				li {
					p {
						- "The "
						kbd Esc
						- " key on desktop platforms."
					}
				}
				li {
					p "The back button or gesture on certain mobile platforms such as Android."
				}
				li {
					p "Any assistive technology's dismiss gesture, such as iOS VoiceOver's two-finger scrub \"z\" gesture."
				}
				li {
					p "A game controller's canonical \"back\" button, such as the circle button on a DualShock gamepad."
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Whenever the user agent receives a potential close request targeted at a "
			code id=close-requests:document {
				a href="#document" Document
			}
			var document
			- ", it must "
			a href="#queue-a-global-task" id=close-requests:queue-a-global-task "queue a global task"
			- " on the "
			a href="#user-interaction-task-source" id=close-requests:user-interaction-task-source "user interaction task source"
			- " given "
			var document
			- "'s "
			a href="#concept-relevant-global" id=close-requests:concept-relevant-global "relevant global object"
			- " to perform the following "
			dfn data-export="" id=close-request-steps "close request steps"
			- :
		}
		ol {
			li {
				p {
					- "If "
					var document
					- "'s "
					a data-x-internal=fullscreen-element href="https://fullscreen.spec.whatwg.org/#fullscreen-element" id=close-requests:fullscreen-element "fullscreen element"
					- " is not null, then:"
				}
				ol {
					li {
						p {
							a data-x-internal=fully-exit-fullscreen href="https://fullscreen.spec.whatwg.org/#fully-exit-fullscreen" id=close-requests:fully-exit-fullscreen "Fully exit fullscreen"
							- " given "
							var document
							- "'s "
							a href="#node-navigable" id=close-requests:node-navigable "node navigable"
							- "'s "
							a href="#nav-top" id=close-requests:nav-top "top-level traversable"
							- "'s "
							a href="#nav-document" id=close-requests:nav-document "active document"
							- .
						}
					}
					li {
						p Return.
					}
				}
				p class=note {
					- "This does "
					em not
					- " fire any relevant event, such as "
					code id=close-requests:event-keydown {
						a data-x-internal=event-keydown href="https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#event-type-keydown" keydown
					}
					- "; it only causes "
					code id=close-requests:event-fullscreenchange {
						a data-x-internal=event-fullscreenchange href="https://fullscreen.spec.whatwg.org/#eventdef-document-fullscreenchange" fullscreenchange
					}
					- " to be eventually fired."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Optionally, skip to the step labeled "
					a href="#close-request-fallback" "alternative processing"
					- .
				}
				p class=note "For example, if the user agent detects user frustration at repeated close request interception by the current web page, it might take this path."
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Fire any relevant events, per "
					cite "UI Events"
					- " or other relevant specifications. "
					a href="#refsUIEVENTS" "[UIEVENTS]"
				}
				p class=example {
					- "An example of a relevant event in the "
					cite "UI Events"
					- " model would be the "
					code id=close-requests:event-keydown-2 {
						a data-x-internal=event-keydown href="https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#event-type-keydown" keydown
					}
					- " event that "
					cite "UI Events"
					a href="https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#events-keyboard-event-order" suggests
					- " firing when the user presses the "
					kbd Esc
					- " key on their keyboard. On most platforms with keyboards, this is treated as a "
					a href="#close-request" id=close-requests:close-request "close request"
					- ", and so would trigger these "
					a href="#close-request-steps" id=close-requests:close-request-steps "close request steps"
					- .
				}
				p class=example {
					- "An example of relevant events that are outside of the model given in "
					cite "UI Events"
					- " would be assistive technology synthesizing an "
					kbd Esc
					code id=close-requests:event-keydown-3 {
						a data-x-internal=event-keydown href="https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#event-type-keydown" keydown
					}
					- " event when the user sends a "
					a href="#close-request" id=close-requests:close-request-2 "close request"
					- " by using a dismiss gesture."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Let "
					var event
					- " be null if no such events are fired, or the "
					code id=close-requests:event {
						a data-x-internal=event href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#interface-event" Event
					}
					- " object representing one of the fired events otherwise. If multiple events are fired, which one is chosen is "
					a data-x-internal=implementation-defined href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined" id=close-requests:implementation-defined-2 implementation-defined
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "If "
					var event
					- " is not null, and its "
					a data-x-internal=canceled-flag href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#canceled-flag" id=close-requests:canceled-flag "canceled flag"
					- " is set, then return."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "If "
					var document
					- " is not "
					a href="#fully-active" id=close-requests:fully-active "fully active"
					- ", then return."
				}
				p class=note {
					- "This step is necessary because, if "
					var event
