				a href="#node-navigable" id=fully-active-documents:node-navigable "node navigable"
				- ", while not being "
				a href="#fully-active" id=fully-active-documents:fully-active-3 "fully active"
				- ". Here "
				code a.html
				- " is loaded into a browser window, "
				code b-1.html
				- " starts out loaded into an "
				code id=fully-active-documents:the-iframe-element {
					a href="#the-iframe-element" iframe
				}
				- " as shown, and "
				code b-2.html
				- " and "
				code c.html
				- " are omitted (they can simply be an empty document)."
			}
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- c="" "<!-- a.html -->"
					c- cp="" "<!DOCTYPE html>"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" html
					c- e="" lang
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"en\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" title
					c- p="" >
					- "Navigable A"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" title
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" iframe
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"b-1.html\""
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" iframe
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" button
					c- e="" onclick
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"frames[0].location.href = 'b-2.html'\""
					c- p="" >
					- "Click me"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" button
					c- p="" >
					c- c="" "<!-- b-1.html -->"
					c- cp="" "<!DOCTYPE html>"
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" html
					c- e="" lang
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"en\""
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" title
					c- p="" >
					- "Navigable B"
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" title
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" iframe
					c- e="" src
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"c.html\""
					c- p="" "></"
					c- f="" iframe
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p {
				- "At this point, the documents given by "
				code a.html
				- ", "
				code b-1.html
				- ", and "
				code c.html
				- " are all the "
				a href="#nav-document" id=fully-active-documents:nav-document-3 "active documents"
				- " of their respective "
				a href="#node-navigable" id=fully-active-documents:node-navigable-2 "node navigables"
				- ". They are also all "
				a href="#fully-active" id=fully-active-documents:fully-active-4 "fully active"
				- .
			}
			p {
				- "After clicking on the "
				code id=fully-active-documents:the-button-element {
					a href="#the-button-element" button
				}
				- ", and thus loading a new "
				code id=fully-active-documents:document-4 {
					a href="#document" Document
				}
				- " from "
				code b-2.html
				- " into navigable B, we have the following results:"
			}
			ul {
				li {
					p {
						- "The "
						code a.html
						code id=fully-active-documents:document-5 {
							a href="#document" Document
						}
						- " remains both the "
						a href="#nav-document" id=fully-active-documents:nav-document-4 "active document"
						- " of navigable A, and "
						a href="#fully-active" id=fully-active-documents:fully-active-5 "fully active"
						- .
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "The "
						code b-1.html
						code id=fully-active-documents:document-6 {
							a href="#document" Document
						}
						- " is now "
						em not
						- " the "
						a href="#nav-document" id=fully-active-documents:nav-document-5 "active document"
						- " of navigable B. As such it is also not "
						a href="#fully-active" id=fully-active-documents:fully-active-6 "fully active"
						- .
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "The new "
						code b-2.html
						code id=fully-active-documents:document-7 {
							a href="#document" Document
						}
						- " is now the "
						a href="#nav-document" id=fully-active-documents:nav-document-6 "active document"
						- " of navigable B, and is also "
						a href="#fully-active" id=fully-active-documents:fully-active-7 "fully active"
						- .
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "The "
						code c.html
						code id=fully-active-documents:document-8 {
							a href="#document" Document
						}
						- " is still the "
						a href="#nav-document" id=fully-active-documents:nav-document-7 "active document"
						- " of navigable C. However, since C's "
						a href="#nav-container-document" id=fully-active-documents:nav-container-document-4 "container document"
						- " is the "
						code b-1.html
						code id=fully-active-documents:document-9 {
							a href="#document" Document
						}
						- ", which is itself not "
						a href="#fully-active" id=fully-active-documents:fully-active-8 "fully active"
						- ", this means the "
						code c.html
						code id=fully-active-documents:document-10 {
							a href="#document" Document
						}
						- " is now not "
						a href="#fully-active" id=fully-active-documents:fully-active-9 "fully active"
						- .
					}
				}
			}
		}
		h3 id=navigation-and-session-history {
			span class=secno "7.4"
			span id=history
			- "Navigation and session history"
			a class=self-link href="#navigation-and-session-history"
		}
		p "Welcome to the dragon's maw. Navigation, session history, and the traversal through that session history are some of the most complex parts of this standard."
		p "The basic concept may not seem so difficult:"
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					- "The user is looking at a "
					a href="#navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:navigable navigable
					- " that is presenting its "
					a href="#nav-document" id=navigation-and-session-history:nav-document "active document"
					- ". They "
					a href="#navigate" id=navigation-and-session-history:navigate navigate
					- " it to another "
					a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=navigation-and-session-history:url URL
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The browser fetches the given URL from the network, using it to "
					a href="#attempt-to-populate-the-history-entry's-document" id=navigation-and-session-history:attempt-to-populate-the-history-entry's-document populate
					- " a new "
					a href="#session-history-entry" id=navigation-and-session-history:session-history-entry "session history entry"
					- " with a newly-"
					a href="#initialise-the-document-object" id=navigation-and-session-history:initialise-the-document-object created
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:document {
						a href="#document" Document
					}
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The browser updates the "
					a href="#navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:navigable-2 navigable
					- "'s "
					a href="#nav-active-history-entry" id=navigation-and-session-history:nav-active-history-entry "active session history entry"
					- " to the newly-populated one, and thus updates the "
					a href="#nav-document" id=navigation-and-session-history:nav-document-2 "active document"
					- " that it is showing to the user."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "At some point later, the user "
					a href="#traverse-the-history-by-a-delta" id=navigation-and-session-history:traverse-the-history-by-a-delta "presses the browser back button"
					- " to go back to the previous "
					a href="#session-history-entry" id=navigation-and-session-history:session-history-entry-2 "session history entry"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The browser looks at the "
					a href="#she-url" id=navigation-and-session-history:she-url URL
					- " stored in that "
					a href="#session-history-entry" id=navigation-and-session-history:session-history-entry-3 "session history entry"
					- ", and uses it to re-fetch and "
					a href="#attempt-to-populate-the-history-entry's-document" id=navigation-and-session-history:attempt-to-populate-the-history-entry's-document-2 populate
					- " that entry's "
					a href="#she-document" id=navigation-and-session-history:she-document document
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The browser again updates the "
					a href="#navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:navigable-3 navigable
					- "'s "
					a href="#nav-active-history-entry" id=navigation-and-session-history:nav-active-history-entry-2 "active session history entry"
					- .
				}
			}
		}
		p "You can see some of the intertwined complexity peeking through here, in how traversal can cause a navigation (i.e., a network fetch to a stored URL), and how a navigation necessarily needs to interface with the session history list to ensure that when it finishes the user is looking at the right thing. But the real problems come in with the various edge cases and interacting web platform features:"
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					a href="#child-navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:child-navigable "Child navigables"
					- " (e.g., those contained in "
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:the-iframe-element {
						a href="#the-iframe-element" iframe
					}
					- "s) can also navigate and traverse, but those navigations need to be linearized into "
					a href="#tn-session-history-entries" id=navigation-and-session-history:tn-session-history-entries "a single session history list"
					- " since the user only has a single back/forward interface for the entire "
					a href="#traversable-navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:traversable-navigable "traversable navigable"
					- " (e.g., browser tab)."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Since the user can traverse back more than a single step in the session history (e.g., by holding down their back button), they can end up traversing multiple "
					a href="#navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:navigable-4 navigables
					- " at the same time when "
					a href="#child-navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:child-navigable-2 "child navigables"
					- " are involved. This needs to be synchronized across all of the involved navigables, which might involve multiple "
					a href="#event-loop" id=navigation-and-session-history:event-loop "event loops"
					- " or even "
					a data-x-internal=agent-cluster href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-agent-clusters" id=navigation-and-session-history:agent-cluster "agent clusters"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "During navigation, servers can respond with 204 or 205 status codes or with `"
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:http-content-disposition {
						a data-x-internal=http-content-disposition href="https://httpwg.org/specs/rfc6266.html" "Content-Disposition: attachment"
					}
					- "` headers, which cause navigation to abort and the "
					a href="#navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:navigable-5 navigable
					- " to stay on its original "
					a href="#active-document" id=navigation-and-session-history:active-document "active document"
					- ". (This is much worse if it happens during a traversal-initiated navigation!)"
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Various other HTTP headers, such as `"
					code Location
					- "`, `"
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:refresh {
						a href="#refresh" Refresh
					}
					- "`, `"
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:x-frame-options {
						a href="#x-frame-options" X-Frame-Options
					}
					- "`, and those for Content Security Policy, contribute to either the "
					a href="#create-navigation-params-by-fetching" id=navigation-and-session-history:create-navigation-params-by-fetching "fetching process"
					- ", or the "
					a href="#initialise-the-document-object" id=navigation-and-session-history:initialise-the-document-object-2 {
						code Document
						- "-creation process"
					}
					- ", or both. The `"
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:cross-origin-opener-policy-2 {
						a href="#cross-origin-opener-policy-2" Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy
					}
					- "` header even contributes to the "
					a href="#browsing-context-group-switches-due-to-cross-origin-opener-policy" "browsing context selection and creation"
					- " process!"
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Some navigations (namely "
					a href="#scroll-to-fragid" "fragment navigations"
					- " and "
					a href="#navigate-non-frag-sync" "single-page app navigations"
					- ") are synchronous, meaning that JavaScript code expects to observe the navigation's results instantly. This then needs to be synchronized with the view of the session history that all other "
					a href="#navigable" id=navigation-and-session-history:navigable-6 navigables
					- " in the tree see, which can be subject to race conditions and necessitate resolving conflicting views of the session history."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "The platform has accumulated various exciting navigation-related features that need special-casing, such as "
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:the-javascript:-url-special-case {
						a href="#the-javascript:-url-special-case" javascript:
					}
					- " URLs, "
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:attr-iframe-srcdoc {
						a href="#attr-iframe-srcdoc" srcdoc
					}
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:the-iframe-element-2 {
						a href="#the-iframe-element" iframe
					}
					- "s, and the "
					code id=navigation-and-session-history:event-beforeunload {
						a href="#event-beforeunload" beforeunload
					}
					- " event."
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "In what follows, we have attempted to guide the reader through these complexities by appropriately cordoning them off into labeled sections and algorithms, and giving appropriate words of introduction where possible. Nevertheless, if you wish to truly understand navigation and session history, "
			a href="#how-to-read-this-specification" "the usual advice"
			- " will be invaluable."
		}
		h4 id=session-history-infrastructure {
			span class=secno "7.4.1"
			span id=the-session-history-of-browsing-contexts
			- "Session history"
			a class=self-link href="#session-history-infrastructure"
		}
		h5 id=session-history-entries {
			span class=secno "7.4.1.1"
			- " Session history entries"
			a class=self-link href="#session-history-entries"
		}
		p {
			- "A "
			dfn id=session-history-entry "session history entry"
			- " is a "
			a data-x-internal=struct href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct" id=session-history-entries:struct struct
			- " with the following "
			a data-x-internal=struct-item href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct-item" id=session-history-entries:struct-item items
			- :
		}
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					dfn id=she-step step
					- ", a non-negative integer or \""
					code pending
					- "\", initially \""
					code pending
					- "\"."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					dfn id=she-url URL
					- ", a "
					a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=session-history-entries:url URL
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					dfn id=she-document-state "document state"
					- ", a "
					a href="#document-state-2" id=session-history-entries:document-state-2 "document state"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p id=she-serialized-state {
					dfn id=she-classic-history-api-state "classic history API state"
					- ", which is "
					a href="#serialized-state" id=session-history-entries:serialized-state "serialized state"
					- ", initially "
					a href="#structuredserializeforstorage" id=session-history-entries:structuredserializeforstorage StructuredSerializeForStorage
					- "(null)."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					dfn id=she-navigation-api-state "navigation API state"
					- ", which is a "
					a href="#serialized-state" id=session-history-entries:serialized-state-2 "serialized state"
					- ", initially "
					a href="#structuredserializeforstorage" id=session-history-entries:structuredserializeforstorage-2 StructuredSerializeForStorage
					- "(undefined)."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					dfn id=she-navigation-api-key "navigation API key"
					- ", which is a string, initially set to the result of "
					a data-x-internal=generating-a-random-uuid href="https://w3c.github.io/webcrypto/#dfn-generate-a-random-uuid" id=session-history-entries:generating-a-random-uuid "generating a random UUID"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					dfn id=she-navigation-api-id "navigation API ID"
					- ", which is a string, initially set to the result of "
					a data-x-internal=generating-a-random-uuid href="https://w3c.github.io/webcrypto/#dfn-generate-a-random-uuid" id=session-history-entries:generating-a-random-uuid-2 "generating a random UUID"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					dfn id=she-scroll-restoration-mode "scroll restoration mode"
					- ", a "
					a href="#scroll-restoration-mode" id=session-history-entries:scroll-restoration-mode "scroll restoration mode"
					- ", initially \""
					code id=session-history-entries:dom-scrollrestoration-auto {
						a href="#dom-scrollrestoration-auto" auto
					}
					- "\"."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					dfn id=she-scroll-position "scroll position data"
					- ", which is scroll position data for the "
					a href="#she-document" id=session-history-entries:she-document document
					- "'s "
					a href="#restorable-scrollable-regions" id=session-history-entries:restorable-scrollable-regions "restorable scrollable regions"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p id=an-entry-with-persisted-user-state {
					dfn id=she-other "persisted user state"
					- ", which is "
					a data-x-internal=implementation-defined href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#implementation-defined" id=session-history-entries:implementation-defined implementation-defined
					- ", initially null"
				}
				p class=example "For example, some user agents might want to persist the values of form controls."
				p class=note {
					- "User agents that persist the value of form controls are encouraged to also persist their directionality (the value of the element's "
					code id=session-history-entries:attr-dir {
						a href="#attr-dir" dir
					}
					- " attribute). This prevents values from being displayed incorrectly after a history traversal when the user had originally entered the values with an explicit, non-default directionality."
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "To get a "
			a href="#session-history-entry" id=session-history-entries:session-history-entry "session history entry"
			- "'s "
			dfn id=she-document document
			- ", return its "
			a href="#she-document-state" id=session-history-entries:she-document-state "document state"
			- "'s "
			a href="#document-state-document" id=session-history-entries:document-state-document document
			- .
		}
		hr
		p id=state-object {
			dfn id=serialized-state "Serialized state"
			- " is a serialization (via "
			a href="#structuredserializeforstorage" id=session-history-entries:structuredserializeforstorage-3 StructuredSerializeForStorage
			- ") of an object representing a user interface state. We sometimes informally refer to \"state objects\", which are the objects representing user interface state supplied by the author, or alternately the objects created by deserializing (via "
			a href="#structureddeserialize" id=session-history-entries:structureddeserialize StructuredDeserialize
			- ") serialized state."
		}
		p {
			- "Pages can "
			a href="#dom-history-pushstate" id=session-history-entries:dom-history-pushstate add
			a href="#serialized-state" id=session-history-entries:serialized-state-3 "serialized state"
			- " to the session history. These are then "
			a href="#structureddeserialize" id=session-history-entries:structureddeserialize-2 deserialized
			- " and "
			a href="#event-popstate" id=session-history-entries:event-popstate "returned to the script"
			- " when the user (or script) goes back in the history, thus enabling authors to use the \"navigation\" metaphor even in one-page applications."
		}
		div class=note {
			p {
				a href="#serialized-state" id=session-history-entries:serialized-state-4 "Serialized state"
				- " is intended to be used for two main purposes: first, storing a preparsed description of the state in the "
				a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=session-history-entries:url-2 URL
				- " so that in the simple case an author doesn't have to do the parsing (though one would still need the parsing for handling "
				a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=session-history-entries:url-3 URLs
				- " passed around by users, so it's only a minor optimization). Second, so that the author can store state that one wouldn't store in the URL because it only applies to the current "
				code id=session-history-entries:document {
					a href="#document" Document
				}
				- " instance and it would have to be reconstructed if a new "
				code id=session-history-entries:document-2 {
					a href="#document" Document
				}
				- " were opened."
			}
			p {
				- "An example of the latter would be something like keeping track of the precise coordinate from which a popup "
				code id=session-history-entries:the-div-element {
					a href="#the-div-element" div
				}
				- " was made to animate, so that if the user goes back, it can be made to animate to the same location. Or alternatively, it could be used to keep a pointer into a cache of data that would be fetched from the server based on the information in the "
				a data-x-internal=url href="https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-url" id=session-history-entries:url-4 URL
				- ", so that when going back and forward, the information doesn't have to be fetched again."
			}
		}
		hr
		p {
			- "A "
			dfn id=scroll-restoration-mode "scroll restoration mode"
			- " indicates whether the user agent should restore the persisted scroll position (if any) when traversing to an "
			a href="#session-history-entry" id=session-history-entries:session-history-entry-2 entry
			- ". A scroll restoration mode is one of the following:"
		}
		dl {
			dt {
				- "\""
				dfn data-dfn-for=ScrollRestoration data-dfn-type=enum-value id=dom-scrollrestoration-auto {
					code auto
				}
				- "\""
			}
			dd "The user agent is responsible for restoring the scroll position upon navigation."
			dt {
				- "\""
				dfn data-dfn-for=ScrollRestoration data-dfn-type=enum-value id=dom-scrollrestoration-manual {
					code manual
				}
				- "\""
			}
			dd "The page is responsible for restoring the scroll position and the user agent does not attempt to do so automatically"
		}
		h5 id=document-state {
			span class=secno "7.4.1.2"
			- " Document state"
			a class=self-link href="#document-state"
		}
		p {
			dfn id=document-state-2 "Document state"
			- " holds state inside a "
			a href="#session-history-entry" id=document-state:session-history-entry "session history entry"
			- " regarding how to present and, if necessary, recreate, a "
			code id=document-state:document {
				a href="#document" Document
			}
			- ". It has:"
		}
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					- "A "
					dfn id=document-state-document document
					- ", a "
					code id=document-state:document-2 {
