		hr
		p {
			- "Some of the algorithms in this specification, for historical reasons, require the user agent to "
			dfn data-export="" id=pause pause
			- " while running a "
			a href="#concept-task" id=event-loop-processing-model:concept-task-7 task
			- " until a condition "
			var goal
			- " is met. This means running the following steps:"
		}
		ol {
			li {
				p {
					- "Let "
					var global
					- " be the "
					a href="#current-global-object" id=event-loop-processing-model:current-global-object "current global object"
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Let "
					var timeBeforePause
					- " be the "
					a data-x-internal=current-high-resolution-time href="https://w3c.github.io/hr-time/#dfn-current-high-resolution-time" id=event-loop-processing-model:current-high-resolution-time-2 "current high resolution time"
					- " given "
					var global
					- .
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "If necessary, update the rendering or user interface of any "
					code id=event-loop-processing-model:document-7 {
						a href="#document" Document
					}
					- " or "
					a href="#navigable" id=event-loop-processing-model:navigable-8 navigable
					- " to reflect the current state."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "Wait until the condition "
					var goal
					- " is met. While a user agent has a paused "
					a href="#concept-task" id=event-loop-processing-model:concept-task-8 task
					- ", the corresponding "
					a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-processing-model:event-loop-25 "event loop"
					- " must not run further "
					a href="#concept-task" id=event-loop-processing-model:concept-task-9 tasks
					- ", and any script in the currently running "
					a href="#concept-task" id=event-loop-processing-model:concept-task-10 task
					- " must block. User agents should remain responsive to user input while paused, however, albeit in a reduced capacity since the "
					a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-processing-model:event-loop-26 "event loop"
					- " will not be doing anything."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					a data-x-internal=record-pause-duration href="https://w3c.github.io/long-animation-frames/#record-pause-duration" id=event-loop-processing-model:record-pause-duration "Record pause duration"
					- " given the "
					a data-x-internal=duration-from href="https://w3c.github.io/hr-time/#dfn-duration-from" id=event-loop-processing-model:duration-from "duration from"
					var timeBeforePause
					- " to the "
					a data-x-internal=current-high-resolution-time href="https://w3c.github.io/hr-time/#dfn-current-high-resolution-time" id=event-loop-processing-model:current-high-resolution-time-3 "current high resolution time"
					- " given "
					var global
					- .
				}
			}
		}
		div class=warning {
			p {
				a href="#pause" id=event-loop-processing-model:pause Pausing
				- " is highly detrimental to the user experience, especially in scenarios where a single "
				a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-processing-model:event-loop-27 "event loop"
				- " is shared among multiple documents. User agents are encouraged to experiment with alternatives to "
				a href="#pause" id=event-loop-processing-model:pause-2 pausing
				- ", such as "
				a href="#spin-the-event-loop" id=event-loop-processing-model:spin-the-event-loop-7 "spinning the event loop"
				- " or even simply proceeding without any kind of suspended execution at all, insofar as it is possible to do so while preserving compatibility with existing content. This specification will happily change if a less-drastic alternative is discovered to be web-compatible."
			}
			p {
				- "In the interim, implementers should be aware that the variety of alternatives that user agents might experiment with can change subtle aspects of "
				a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-processing-model:event-loop-28 "event loop"
				- " behavior, including "
				a href="#concept-task" id=event-loop-processing-model:concept-task-11 task
				- " and "
				a href="#microtask" id=event-loop-processing-model:microtask-4 microtask
				- " timing. Implementations should continue experimenting even if doing so causes them to violate the exact semantics implied by the "
				a href="#pause" id=event-loop-processing-model:pause-3 pause
				- " operation."
			}
		}
		h5 id=generic-task-sources {
			span class=secno "8.1.7.4"
			- " Generic task sources"
			a class=self-link href="#generic-task-sources"
		}
		p {
			- "The following "
			a href="#task-source" id=generic-task-sources:task-source "task sources"
			- " are used by a number of mostly unrelated features in this and other specifications."
		}
		dl {
			dt {
				- "The "
				dfn data-export="" id=dom-manipulation-task-source "DOM manipulation task source"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "This "
					a href="#task-source" id=generic-task-sources:task-source-2 "task source"
					- " is used for features that react to DOM manipulations, such as things that happen in a non-blocking fashion when an element is "
					a href="#insert-an-element-into-a-document" id=generic-task-sources:insert-an-element-into-a-document "inserted into the document"
					- .
				}
			}
			dt {
				- "The "
				dfn data-export="" id=user-interaction-task-source "user interaction task source"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "This "
					a href="#task-source" id=generic-task-sources:task-source-3 "task source"
					- " is used for features that react to user interaction, for example keyboard or mouse input."
				}
				p {
					- "Events sent in response to user input (e.g. "
					code id=generic-task-sources:event-click {
						a data-x-internal=event-click href="https://w3c.github.io/uievents/#event-type-click" click
					}
					- " events) must be fired using "
					a href="#concept-task" id=generic-task-sources:concept-task tasks
					a href="#queue-a-task" id=generic-task-sources:queue-a-task queued
					- " with the "
					a href="#user-interaction-task-source" id=generic-task-sources:user-interaction-task-source "user interaction task source"
					- ". "
					a href="#refsUIEVENTS" "[UIEVENTS]"
				}
			}
			dt {
				- "The "
				dfn data-export="" id=networking-task-source "networking task source"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "This "
					a href="#task-source" id=generic-task-sources:task-source-4 "task source"
					- " is used for features that trigger in response to network activity."
				}
			}
			dt id=history-traversal-task-source {
				- "The "
				dfn id=navigation-and-traversal-task-source "navigation and traversal task source"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "This "
					a href="#task-source" id=generic-task-sources:task-source-5 "task source"
					- " is used to queue tasks involved in "
					a href="#navigate" id=generic-task-sources:navigate navigation
					- " and "
					a href="#apply-the-traverse-history-step" id=generic-task-sources:apply-the-traverse-history-step "history traversal"
					- .
				}
			}
			dt {
				- "The "
				dfn id=rendering-task-source "rendering task source"
			}
			dd {
				p {
					- "This "
					a href="#task-source" id=generic-task-sources:task-source-6 "task source"
					- " is used solely to "
					a href="#update-the-rendering" id=generic-task-sources:update-the-rendering "update the rendering"
					- ". "
				}
			}
		}
		h5 id=event-loop-for-spec-authors {
			span class=secno "8.1.7.5"
			- " Dealing with the event loop from other specifications"
			a class=self-link href="#event-loop-for-spec-authors"
		}
		p {
			- "Writing specifications that correctly interact with the "
			a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop "event loop"
			- " can be tricky. This is compounded by how this specification uses concurrency-model-independent terminology, so we say things like \""
			a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-2 "event loop"
			- "\" and \""
			a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel "in parallel"
			- "\" instead of using more familiar model-specific terms like \"main thread\" or \"on a background thread\"."
		}
		p {
			- "By default, specification text generally runs on the "
			a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-3 "event loop"
			- ". This falls out from the formal "
			a href="#event-loop-processing-model" "event loop processing model"
			- ", in that you can eventually trace most algorithms back to a "
			a href="#concept-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:concept-task task
			a href="#queue-a-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:queue-a-task queued
			- " there."
		}
		p class=example {
			- "The algorithm steps for any JavaScript method will be invoked by author code calling that method. And author code can only be run via queued tasks, usually originating somewhere in the "
			a href="#script-processing-model" {
				code script
				- " processing model"
			}
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "From this starting point, the overriding guideline is that any work a specification needs to perform that would otherwise block the "
			a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-4 "event loop"
			- " must instead be performed "
			a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-2 "in parallel"
			- " with it. This includes (but is not limited to):"
		}
		ul {
			li {
				p "performing heavy computation;"
			}
			li {
				p "displaying a user-facing prompt;"
			}
			li {
				p "performing operations which could require involving outside systems (i.e. \"going out of process\")."
			}
		}
		p {
			- "The next complication is that, in algorithm sections that are "
			a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-3 "in parallel"
			- ", you must not create or manipulate objects associated to a specific "
			a data-x-internal=realm href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-code-realms" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:realm realm
			- ", "
			a href="#global-object" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:global-object global
			- ", or "
			a href="#environment-settings-object" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:environment-settings-object "environment settings object"
			- ". (Stated in more familiar terms, you must not directly access main-thread artifacts from a background thread.) Doing so would create data races observable to JavaScript code, since after all, your algorithm steps are running "
			em {
				a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-4 "in parallel"
			}
			- " to the JavaScript code."
		}
		p {
			- "You can, however, manipulate specification-level data structures and values from "
			cite Infra
			- ", as those are realm-agnostic. They are never directly exposed to JavaScript without a specific conversion taking place (often "
			a data-x-internal=concept-idl-convert href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#es-type-mapping" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:concept-idl-convert "via Web IDL"
			- "). "
			a href="#refsINFRA" "[INFRA]"
			a href="#refsWEBIDL" "[WEBIDL]"
		}
		p {
			- "To affect the world of observable JavaScript objects, then, you must "
			a href="#queue-a-global-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:queue-a-global-task "queue a global task"
			- " to perform any such manipulations. This ensures your steps are properly interleaved with respect to other things happening on the "
			a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-5 "event loop"
			- ". Furthermore, you must choose a "
			a href="#task-source" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:task-source "task source"
			- " when "
			a href="#queue-a-global-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:queue-a-global-task-2 "queuing a global task"
			- "; this governs the relative order of your steps versus others. If you are unsure which "
			a href="#task-source" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:task-source-2 "task source"
			- " to use, pick one of the "
			a href="#generic-task-sources" "generic task sources"
			- " that sounds most applicable. Finally, you must indicate which "
			a href="#global-object" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:global-object-2 "global object"
			- " your queued task is associated with; this ensures that if that global object is inactive, the task does not run."
		}
		p class=note {
			- "The base primitive, on which "
			a href="#queue-a-global-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:queue-a-global-task-3 "queue a global task"
			- " builds, is the "
			a href="#queue-a-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:queue-a-task-2 "queue a task"
			- " algorithm. In general, "
			a href="#queue-a-global-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:queue-a-global-task-4 "queue a global task"
			- " is better because it automatically picks the right "
			a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-6 "event loop"
			- " and, where appropriate, "
			a href="#concept-task-document" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:concept-task-document document
			- ". Older specifications often use "
			a href="#queue-a-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:queue-a-task-3 "queue a task"
			- " combined with the "
			a href="#implied-event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:implied-event-loop "implied event loop"
			- " and "
			a href="#implied-document" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:implied-document "implied document"
			- " concepts, but this is discouraged."
		}
		p "Putting this all together, we can provide a template for a typical algorithm that needs to do work asynchronously:"
		ol {
			li {
				p {
					- "Do any synchronous setup work, while still on the "
					a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-7 "event loop"
					- ". This may include converting "
					a data-x-internal=realm href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-code-realms" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:realm-2 realm
					- "-specific JavaScript values into realm-agnostic specification-level values."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
