			p "Here are several things to notice about this algorithm:"
			ul {
				li {
					p {
						- "It does its URL parsing up front, on the "
						a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-9 "event loop"
						- ", before going to the "
						a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-7 "in parallel"
						- " steps. This is necessary, since parsing depends on the "
						a href="#current-settings-object" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:current-settings-object-2 "current settings object"
						- ", which would no longer be current after going "
						a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-8 "in parallel"
						- .
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "Alternately, it could have saved a reference to the "
						a href="#current-settings-object" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:current-settings-object-3 "current settings object"
						- "'s "
						a href="#api-base-url" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:api-base-url "API base URL"
						- " and used it during the "
						a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-9 "in parallel"
						- " steps; that would have been equivalent. However, we recommend instead doing as much work as possible up front, as this example does. Attempting to save the correct values can be error prone; for example, if we'd saved just the "
						a href="#current-settings-object" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:current-settings-object-4 "current settings object"
						- ", instead of its "
						a href="#api-base-url" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:api-base-url-2 "API base URL"
						- ", there would have been a potential race."
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "It implicitly passes a "
						a data-x-internal=list href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:list-4 list
						- " of "
						a data-x-internal=string href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:string-2 strings
						- " from the initial steps to the "
						a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-10 "in parallel"
						- " steps. This is OK, as both "
						a data-x-internal=list href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:list-5 lists
						- " and "
						a data-x-internal=string href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#string" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:string-3 strings
						- " are "
						a data-x-internal=realm href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-code-realms" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:realm-3 realm
						- -agnostic.
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "It performs \"expensive computation\" (waiting for 100 milliseconds per input URL) during the "
						a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-11 "in parallel"
						- " steps, thus not blocking the main "
						a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-10 "event loop"
						- .
					}
				}
				li {
					p {
						- "Promises, as observable JavaScript objects, are never created and manipulated during the "
						a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-12 "in parallel"
						- " steps. "
						var p
						- " is created before entering those steps, and then is manipulated during a "
						a href="#concept-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:concept-task-2 task
						- " that is "
						a href="#queue-a-global-task" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:queue-a-global-task-7 queued
						- " specifically for that purpose."
					}
				}
				li {
					p "The creation of a JavaScript array object also happens during the queued task, and is careful to specify which realm it creates the array in since that is no longer obvious from context."
				}
			}
			p {
				- "(On these last two points, see also "
				a href="https://github.com/whatwg/webidl/issues/135" "whatwg/webidl issue #135"
				- " and "
				a href="https://github.com/whatwg/webidl/issues/371" "whatwg/webidl issue #371"
				- ", where we are still mulling over the subtleties of the above promise-resolution pattern.)"
			}
			p {
				- "Another thing to note is that, in the event this algorithm was called from a Web IDL-specified operation taking a "
				code sequence
				- <
				code id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:idl-usvstring {
					a data-x-internal=idl-usvstring href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#idl-USVString" USVString
				}
				- ">, there was an automatic conversion from "
				a data-x-internal=realm href="https://tc39.es/ecma262/#sec-code-realms" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:realm-4 realm
				- "-specific JavaScript objects provided by the author as input, into the realm-agnostic "
				code sequence
				- <
				code id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:idl-usvstring-2 {
					a data-x-internal=idl-usvstring href="https://webidl.spec.whatwg.org/#idl-USVString" USVString
				}
				- "> Web IDL type, which we then treat as a "
				a data-x-internal=list href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#list" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:list-6 list
				- " of "
				a data-x-internal=scalar-value-string href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#scalar-value-string" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:scalar-value-string-2 "scalar value strings"
				- ". So depending on how your specification is structured, there may be other implicit steps happening on the main "
				a href="#event-loop" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:event-loop-11 "event loop"
				- " that play a part in this whole process of getting you ready to go "
				a href="#in-parallel" id=event-loop-for-spec-authors:in-parallel-13 "in parallel"
				- .
			}
		}
		h4 id=events {
			span class=secno "8.1.8"
			- " Events"
			a class=self-link href="#events"
		}
		h5 id=event-handler-attributes {
			span class=secno "8.1.8.1"
			- " Event handlers"
			a class=self-link href="#event-handler-attributes"
		}
		div class="mdn-anno wrapped" {
			button class=mdn-anno-btn onclick="toggleStatus(this)" {
				span MDN
			}
			div class=feature {
				p {
					a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Guide/Events/Event_handlers" title="Events are signals fired inside the browser window that notify of changes in the browser or operating system environment. Programmers can create event handler code that will run when an event fires, allowing web pages to respond appropriately to change." "Events/Event_handlers"
				}
			}
		}
		p {
			- "Many objects can have "
			dfn data-export="" data-lt="event handler" id=event-handlers "event handlers"
			- " specified. These act as non-capture "
			a data-x-internal=event-listener href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-event-listener" id=event-handler-attributes:event-listener "event listeners"
			- " for the object on which they are specified. "
			a href="#refsDOM" "[DOM]"
		}
		p {
			- "An "
			a href="#event-handlers" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handlers "event handler"
			- " is a "
			a data-x-internal=struct href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct" id=event-handler-attributes:struct struct
			- " with two "
			a data-x-internal=struct-item href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#struct-item" id=event-handler-attributes:struct-item items
			- :
		}
		ul {
			li {
				p {
					- "a "
					dfn data-dfn-for="event handler" data-export="" id=event-handler-value value
					- ", which is either null, a callback object, or an "
					a href="#internal-raw-uncompiled-handler" id=event-handler-attributes:internal-raw-uncompiled-handler "internal raw uncompiled handler"
					- ". The "
					code id=event-handler-attributes:eventhandler {
						a href="#eventhandler" EventHandler
					}
					- " callback function type describes how this is exposed to scripts. Initially, an "
					a href="#event-handlers" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handlers-2 "event handler"
					- "'s "
					a href="#event-handler-value" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handler-value value
					- " must be set to null."
				}
			}
			li {
				p {
					- "a "
					dfn data-dfn-for="event handler" data-export="" id=event-handler-listener listener
					- ", which is either null or an "
					a data-x-internal=event-listener href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-event-listener" id=event-handler-attributes:event-listener-2 "event listener"
					- " responsible for running "
					a href="#the-event-handler-processing-algorithm" id=event-handler-attributes:the-event-handler-processing-algorithm "the event handler processing algorithm"
					- ". Initially, an "
					a href="#event-handlers" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handlers-3 "event handler"
					- "'s "
					a href="#event-handler-listener" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handler-listener listener
					- " must be set to null."
				}
			}
		}
		p "Event handlers are exposed in two ways."
		p {
			- "The first way, common to all event handlers, is as an "
			a href="#event-handler-idl-attributes" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handler-idl-attributes "event handler IDL attribute"
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "The second way is as an "
			a href="#event-handler-content-attributes" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handler-content-attributes "event handler content attribute"
			- ". Event handlers on "
			a href="#html-elements" id=event-handler-attributes:html-elements "HTML elements"
			- " and some of the event handlers on "
			code id=event-handler-attributes:window {
				a href="#window" Window
			}
			- " objects are exposed in this way."
		}
		p {
			- "For both of these two ways, the "
			a href="#event-handlers" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handlers-4 "event handler"
			- " is exposed through a "
			dfn id=event-handler-name name
			- ", which is a string that always starts with \""
			code on
			- "\" and is followed by the name of the event for which the handler is intended."
		}
		hr
		p {
			- "Most of the time, the object that exposes an "
			a href="#event-handlers" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handlers-5 "event handler"
			- " is the same as the object on which the corresponding "
			a data-x-internal=event-listener href="https://dom.spec.whatwg.org/#concept-event-listener" id=event-handler-attributes:event-listener-3 "event listener"
			- " is added. However, the "
			code id=event-handler-attributes:the-body-element {
				a href="#the-body-element" body
			}
			- " and "
			code id=event-handler-attributes:frameset {
				a href="#frameset" frameset
			}
			- " elements expose several "
			a href="#event-handlers" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handlers-6 "event handlers"
			- " that act upon the element's "
			code id=event-handler-attributes:window-2 {
				a href="#window" Window
			}
			- " object, if one exists. In either case, we call the object an "
			a href="#event-handlers" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handlers-7 "event handler"
			- " acts upon the "
			dfn id=event-handler-target target
			- " of that "
			a href="#event-handlers" id=event-handler-attributes:event-handlers-8 "event handler"
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "To "
			dfn id=determining-the-target-of-an-event-handler "determine the target of an event handler"
