		h4 id=worklets-lifetime {
			span class=secno "11.3.3"
			- " The worklet's lifetime"
			a class=self-link href="#worklets-lifetime"
		}
		p {
			- "The lifetime of a "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:worklet {
				a href="#worklet" Worklet
			}
			- " has no special considerations; it is tied to the object it belongs to, such as the "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:window {
				a href="#window" Window
			}
			- .
		}
		p {
			- "Each "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:document {
				a href="#document" Document
			}
			- " has a "
			dfn id=concept-document-worklet-global-scopes "worklet global scopes"
			- ", which is a "
			a data-x-internal=set href="https://infra.spec.whatwg.org/#ordered-set" id=worklets-lifetime:set set
			- " of "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:workletglobalscope {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" WorkletGlobalScope
			}
			- "s, initially empty."
		}
		p {
			- "The lifetime of a "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:workletglobalscope-2 {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" WorkletGlobalScope
			}
			- " is, at a minimum, tied to the "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:document-2 {
				a href="#document" Document
			}
			- " whose "
			a href="#concept-document-worklet-global-scopes" id=worklets-lifetime:concept-document-worklet-global-scopes "worklet global scopes"
			- " contain it. In particular, "
			a href="#destroy-a-document" id=worklets-lifetime:destroy-a-document destroying
			- " the "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:document-3 {
				a href="#document" Document
			}
			- " will "
			a href="#terminate-a-worklet-global-scope" id=worklets-lifetime:terminate-a-worklet-global-scope terminate
			- " the corresponding "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:workletglobalscope-3 {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" WorkletGlobalScope
			}
			- " and allow it to be garbage-collected."
		}
		p {
			- "Additionally, user agents may, at any time, "
			a href="#terminate-a-worklet-global-scope" id=worklets-lifetime:terminate-a-worklet-global-scope-2 terminate
			- " a given "
			code id=worklets-lifetime:workletglobalscope-4 {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" WorkletGlobalScope
			}
			- ", unless the specification defining the corresponding worklet type says otherwise. For example, they might terminate them if the "
			a href="#worklet-agent" id=worklets-lifetime:worklet-agent "worklet agent"
			- "'s "
			a href="#concept-agent-event-loop" id=worklets-lifetime:concept-agent-event-loop "event loop"
			- " has no "
			a href="#concept-task" id=worklets-lifetime:concept-task tasks
			- " queued, or if the user agent has no pending operations planning to make use of the worklet, or if the user agent detects abnormal operations such as infinite loops or callbacks exceeding imposed time limits."
		}
		p {
			- "Finally, specifications for specific worklet types can give more specific details on when to "
			a href="#create-a-worklet-global-scope" id=worklets-lifetime:create-a-worklet-global-scope create
			code id=worklets-lifetime:workletglobalscope-5 {
				a href="#workletglobalscope" WorkletGlobalScope
			}
			- "s for a given worklet type. For example, they might create them during specific processes that call upon worklet code, as in the "
			a href="#worklets-example-registering" example
			- .
		}
		h2 id=webstorage {
			span class=secno "12"
			- " Web storage"
			a class=self-link href="#webstorage"
		}
		div class="mdn-anno wrapped" {
			button class=mdn-anno-btn onclick="toggleStatus(this)" {
				b class=all-engines-flag title="Support in all current engines." ✔
				span MDN
			}
			div class=feature {
				p {
					a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Storage_API" title="The Web Storage API provides mechanisms by which browsers can store key/value pairs, in a much more intuitive fashion than using cookies." Web_Storage_API
				}
				p class=all-engines-text "Support in all current engines."
				div class=support {
					span class="firefox yes" {
						span Firefox
						span "3.5+"
					}
					span class="safari yes" {
						span Safari
						span "4+"
					}
					span class="chrome yes" {
						span Chrome
						span "4+"
					}
					hr
					span class="opera yes" {
						span Opera
						span "10.5+"
					}
					span class="edge_blink yes" {
						span Edge
						span "79+"
					}
					hr
					span class="edge yes" {
						span "Edge (Legacy)"
						span "12+"
					}
					span class="ie yes" {
						span "Internet Explorer"
						span "8+"
					}
					hr
					span class="firefox_android yes" {
						span "Firefox Android"
						span "6+"
					}
					span class="safari_ios unknown" {
						span "Safari iOS"
						span ?
					}
					span class="chrome_android unknown" {
						span "Chrome Android"
						span ?
					}
					span class="webview_android yes" {
						span "WebView Android"
						span "37+"
					}
					span class="samsunginternet_android unknown" {
						span "Samsung Internet"
						span ?
					}
					span class="opera_android yes" {
						span "Opera Android"
						span "11+"
					}
				}
			}
			div class=feature {
				p {
					a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Storage_API/Using_the_Web_Storage_API" title="The Web Storage API provides mechanisms by which browsers can securely store key/value pairs." "Web_Storage_API/Using_the_Web_Storage_API"
				}
			}
		}
		h3 id=introduction-15 {
			span class=secno "12.1"
			- " Introduction"
			a class=self-link href="#introduction-15"
		}
		p {
			i "This section is non-normative."
		}
		p {
			- "This specification introduces two related mechanisms, similar to HTTP session cookies, for storing name-value pairs on the client side. "
			a href="#refsCOOKIES" "[COOKIES]"
		}
		p "The first is designed for scenarios where the user is carrying out a single transaction, but could be carrying out multiple transactions in different windows at the same time."
		p "Cookies don't really handle this case well. For example, a user could be buying plane tickets in two different windows, using the same site. If the site used cookies to keep track of which ticket the user was buying, then as the user clicked from page to page in both windows, the ticket currently being purchased would \"leak\" from one window to the other, potentially causing the user to buy two tickets for the same flight without noticing."
		p {
			- "To address this, this specification introduces the "
			code id=introduction-15:dom-sessionstorage {
				a href="#dom-sessionstorage" sessionStorage
			}
			- " getter. Sites can add data to the session storage, and it will be accessible to any page from the same site opened in that window."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "For example, a page could have a checkbox that the user ticks to indicate that they want insurance:"
			pre {
				code class=html {
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" label
					c- p="" >
					c- p="" <
					c- f="" input
					c- e="" type
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"checkbox\""
					c- e="" onchange
					c- o="" "="
					c- s="" "\"sessionStorage.insurance = checked ? 'true' : ''\""
					c- p="" >
					- " I want insurance on this trip. "
					c- p="" "</"
					c- f="" label
					c- p="" >
				}
			}
			p "A later page could then check, from script, whether the user had checked the checkbox or not:"
			pre {
				code class=js {
					c- k="" if
					c- p="" "("
					- sessionStorage
					c- p="" .
					- insurance
					c- p="" ")"
					c- p="" "{"
					c- p="" ...
					c- p="" "}"
				}
			}
			p "If the user had multiple windows opened on the site, each one would have its own individual copy of the session storage object."
		}
		p "The second storage mechanism is designed for storage that spans multiple windows, and lasts beyond the current session. In particular, web applications might wish to store megabytes of user data, such as entire user-authored documents or a user's mailbox, on the client side for performance reasons."
		p "Again, cookies do not handle this case well, because they are transmitted with every request."
		p {
			- "The "
			code id=introduction-15:dom-localstorage {
				a href="#dom-localstorage" localStorage
			}
			- " getter is used to access a page's local storage area."
		}
		div class=example {
			p "The site at example.com can display a count of how many times the user has loaded its page by putting the following at the bottom of its page:"
