		p class=note {
			- "Typically, the "
			a href="#special" id=the-stack-of-open-elements:special special
			- " elements have the start and end tag tokens handled specifically, while "
			a href="#ordinary" id=the-stack-of-open-elements:ordinary ordinary
			- " elements' tokens fall into \"any other start tag\" and \"any other end tag\" clauses, and some parts of the tree builder check if a particular element in the "
			a href="#stack-of-open-elements" id=the-stack-of-open-elements:stack-of-open-elements-6 "stack of open elements"
			- " is in the "
			a href="#special" id=the-stack-of-open-elements:special-2 special
			- " category. However, some elements (e.g., the "
			code id=the-stack-of-open-elements:the-option-element {
				a href="#the-option-element" option
			}
			- " element) have their start or end tag tokens handled specifically, but are still not in the "
			a href="#special" id=the-stack-of-open-elements:special-3 special
			- " category, so that they get the "
			a href="#ordinary" id=the-stack-of-open-elements:ordinary-2 ordinary
			- " handling elsewhere."
		}
		p {
			- "The "
			a href="#stack-of-open-elements" id=the-stack-of-open-elements:stack-of-open-elements-7 "stack of open elements"
			- " is said to "
			dfn id=has-an-element-in-the-specific-scope {
				- "have an element "
				var "target node"
				- " in a specific scope"
			}
			- " consisting of a list of element types "
